      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>THE man who is frightened at ghosts is afraid oj nothing. FINE feathers may fail sometimes to make fine birds, but they can be depended on f.r a bill at least. '</p>
        <p>EDWIN—" A man shouldn't ma-ry his coook.' Angelina—" No. Nor should he expe t the girl he marries to be_ome his cook." _ _</p>
        <p>THE MYSTERY OF IT. M rs. Su ffwell : "Why, Bridget, you have been eating Onions!' Bridget: "Shure, mum your'e a moind reader." "WHEN Jack Ford was in an Indian cathedral he lit his pipe at a sacred lamp betore one of the altars." What did the priest say ?" "Holy</p>
        <p>smoke !" THE congregation of a country church were horrified during a morning service last summer to hear their new (and athletic) curate cry out at the end of the lesson, " Ile-ah-endeth the first innings." How can Ige out of this ?" asked an English- man, up to his arm-pits in a Scotch tog, of a passer-by. "1 dinna think e can get o•-. 4 of it," was the response of the Highlander, as he went on his way. FOR THE TIME BEING.</p>
        <p>"There is a pe• iod in</p>
        <p>a woman's life when she thinks _of noting but</p>
        <p>dres.</p>
        <p>"What period is that ?" "Froiii the</p>
        <p>cradle to the grave:" "WHAT blessings children are !" as the parish clerk said, when he took the fee after their</p>
        <p>christening. Emu Er. SAID THAN DONE.—Doctor: "This swelling at the back of your neck is not exactly dangerous, but you will do well not to lose sight of</p>
        <p>it!" EMPLOYER "Boy, take this letter and wait for an answer." New Boy "Yes, sir." Employer: "Well, what, are you waiting for?" New Boy:</p>
        <p>"The answer, sir.'" IT DEPEN DED ON THE D OWRY. —Matrimonial. Agent: "You have now seen Miss Pearlstone ; what do you think of her !" Would-be Bride- groom: "Hum ! it depends entirely oil her dowry whether I ought to say she has hollow cheeks or fascinating dimp!es in her face !" A VULGAR FRACTION. —6 Humble as I am," said a Yankee spouter to a mass meeting, "I still remember that I am a fraction of this magnidcent republic." "You are, indeed," said a bystander, "and a vulgar one, into the bargain." FEMININE GENDER, POSSESSIVE CASE. —Hoch- stadter : "Woggle %awn vants der courts to change his name. I don't see why. Vat's in a name ?" Mergentlial (who knows) : "Dot depends. If it vas your vite's name, sometimes der whole bisness</p>
        <p>vas in it !" "WHAT reason had you for knoeking him down ?" asked the inauistrate. "Had he made an assualt on you ?" " Wsrse than that, judge," answered the prison- r. "He made fun of the church I belong to !" A commercial complaining of the smallness of some chops brought to the table, an incorrieible wag observed, "Probably the sheep was fed on short commons I" A LIGHT REPAST.—Dentist : "Will you take gas, sir ?'' Irishman: "No, sorr ;Oi can't stand the smell av it. Oi'll take the elictric bight, plase !" Once when Henry Russell was singing "Wood- man spare that tree," an old gentleman cried out: "Mr. Russell, was the tree saved?" "It was, air." "Thank heaven for that !" the old gentle- man answered, with a sigh of relief. Ha CAME BY WATER.—" What trade are you?" asked his worship. "Shore, now, your honour, an' I'm a sailor." "You a seafaring man? I question whether you were ever at sea in your life:' "Be jabers, now, an' does your honour think I come over from Oireland in a waggin ?" SAVE ME FROM MY FRIENDS !—"I hear you had an accident yesterday," said a friend to a returning sportsman: "shot a man instead of a. partridge, or something, eh?" "Yes,' said the sportsman mournfully. "It was a sad business. I'm afraid he is seriously hurt." " Won't it be a rather serious affair for you? Heavy damages' if nothing worse." "Oh, no; thank heaven 1 He was one of my very best friends." POOR ROBERT I—" Mamma, are policemen very poor ?" asked little Kitty. "No, dearie ; what puts that in your little head ?" "Because I saw Susan feeding one downstairs yesterday !" SMALL BOY (to grocer): "If you please, Mr. Selby, my mother wants to know if you will give her an alinanack ?'' Grocer (leaning over the counter) : "But, my little man' your mother does not get her groceries here." Small Boy: "No, Mr. Welby, but we often borrow your wheelbar- row." "WELL, my dear, are you getting on nicely with your music?' "Oh yes, mamma; last month when I played with my music teacher, I was always a couple of bars behind. Now lam always at least three bars ahead." • SCHOOL TEACHER: "What little boy can tell me where is the home of the swallow? Bobby: "I kin, please." School Teaeher : "Well, Bobby ?" Bobby: "The home of the swallow is the stummick." THE USE OF IT. —A school inspector, having a few minutes to spare after examining the school, put a few questions to the lower form boys, on the common objects in the schoolroom. "What is the use of that map ?" he asked, pointing to one stretched across the corner of the room: and half a dozen shrill voices answered in measured arti- culations, "Please, sir, it's to hide master's bicycle !" IN Too GREAT A HURRY.—OId aunt (on her death-bed) : "I am just making nay will, my dear Henry. I know, alas I too well, that you are not religiously disposed, and have no desire to promote the cause of "—Nephew (hastily) : "Beg your pardon, aunt; quite the contrary." Aunt: "Heaven be praised. Then you will be glad to hear that I have left all my property to the Church ! PORE BLOOD.—Mrs. Sangazure : "Doctor, what do you think is the matter with me?" Doctor: "1 am inclined to think your blood is not pure. I'll have to give you ,omething to purify your blood." Mrs Sangazare, haughtily: "You are probably not aware that I belong to one of the oldest families in England." Nov SENSITIVE. —A father had occasion to reprove his pretty little (laughter he other day. "Mary I wish you would e a better little girl : you have no idea how sorry I am that mamma has to scold you so much." "Oh, dont't worry about it, papa," was the reply. " I'm not one of those sensitive children—half the time I make a point of not hearing what she says" A CANDID JUDGE. —A well-known judge, who was not pa • Ocularly famous for his rilliancy, on one of his country circuits had to try a man for stealing some copper. In his charge he had frequeua occasion to mention the "copper," which he uniformally called "lead," adding ; "I beg your pardon, gentleman—copper but I can't get the leqd ow of my head !" At thi, somewhat candid admission the whole court was convulsed with laughter. A SADLY SINFUL WORLD. —Housekeeper : "Was it _hard times that threw you out of work ?" Mouldy Mike: "No, mum; it was the wicked- ness of the people, mum. I used to earn ten dqllars a week an travellin expenses but the sin- ful world has lost all interest in di' cause I was aehelpin', muin." " What cause?' "The glorious cause of temperance, mum." " Goodness ! Were you a temperance lecturer ?" "No, mum; I was the horrible example ' ! A CHINAMAN, lately returned from a trip to Europe, treate.l his countrymen to the following description of the piano : —" The Europeans keep a four-hie'ged beast, which they can make sing at will. Aalan, or more fe equently a woman, or even a feeble girl sits down in front of the animal and steps on its tail, while at the same tune striking its white teeth with his or her fingers, when the creature begins to singe The singing, though mach louder than a bird, is pleasant to listen to. The beast does not bite, nor does it move, though it is net tied up. TII2:N HE WAS Feefous. The influence of American travellers in aurope is well-known to be considerable, but in this instance there must ha' e been exaggeration. A crippled old woman (says the name or) whom I met in Leamington, amesed me by her original speeches. One day I spoke of Shakespeare, anil hinted at a visit to Stratford-on-Avon. " Law . said the old woman, in a scornful tone ; " who was he? Oa'y a plough- boy, and he was never thought nothha; of till them Americans came over and took him up."</p>
      </div>
