      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>" CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>" CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>An exhibition of the arts, crafts, and industries of Finland will be opened in London next month. Weaving is the common handicraft of the Finnish people, and few machine-made materials are used by them, though on the western boundary the petty trader has from time to time introduced foreign fabrics, which have been adopted by the more sophisticated townspeople. A small model loom will be on view. These looms are suitable for children's playthings, and the mechanism is quite simple enough to be worked by them. The em- broidery exhibits will be of the greatest interest, as pointing to the Oriental origin of this ancient peo- ple. The embroideries are heirlooms handed down trom mother to daughter, and anyone comparing the Runo singer's costume and that of the North American Indian will notice the curious similarity. Most of the embroiderers know 73 of these patterns, and some of these have named over 300. Every small portion of the pattern has a distinguishing name of its own, borrowed from the pine-needles, birds' eyes, feathers, and, strange to say, Buddhist symbols which they resemble. Students of enth- nology cannot fail to be interested in this suggestive exhibition, and more frivolous people will doubt- less welcome the opportunity of a novel Christmas. present.</p>
        <p>The Parnellites declare that they have their can- didates ready for the constituences which they pro- pose to fight, but where are the funds to come from? The return of nine Parnellite members necessitates raising money for the support of' at least some of them, for all are not able to give their services unless they are paid. Some wisdom has been shown, 'iowever, in selecting certain candidates who will not require to be salaried. Among these are Dr. Ffrench Mullen, R.N., a gentleman who, like Colonel Nolan, is in receipt of a pension for services to the Queen and country; Mr. Rochfort Maguire, M.P., whose successful career in South Africa has ren- dered him a wealthy man; Dr. Kenny, M.P., w'iose position as coroner for the city of Dublin brings clim in something like £6OO a year; C)lonel Nolan, M.P., and possibly Mr. J. H. Parnell, brother to the late Irish I,ader. Still a considerable sum will probably be required each year, far larger than the funds of the National League, judging from the .übscriptions received and acknowledged each fort- nigh t,could supply.</p>
        <p>The cutting of faggots in payment of the quit- I rent of certain lands in Salop, which will take place on Monday in the Queen's Remembrancer's room at the Law Courts, is not the only quit-rent them tl be tendered. The rent of a forge in the parish of St. Clement Danes will be paid by the presentation to the Queen's Remembrancer by the City Solicitor of BiX horse-shoes and sixty-one hob- nails. The forge, which is in Milford-lane, is said to have been on the site of the old tilting-ground, and it is supposed that the quit-rental was granted for service rendered at a tourney. The practice of cutting the faggots has been traced back to Dooms- day-book. A modern quit-rent is that of the estate of Strathfieldsaye, which was granted to the Duke of Wellington in recognition of his victory at Waterloo. On each anniversary of that memorable , battle a flag is sent to the Queen in lieu of rent,and I these Stratlifieldsaye flags are all kept in one of the I rooms at Windsor Castle.</p>
        <p>There is no foundation for the statement which has been circulated in Tory quarters that Mr. Naoroji, M.P. does not intend to seek re-election for Central Finsbury. Experience has repeatedly proved that supposed gains in the revision court are delusive, and whatever may have been the result of the recent inquiry, Mr. Naoroji is con- fident that he has not lost ground in the constitu- ency which he represents. For some reason or other, perhaps because Lord Salisbury called him "a black- man," the member for Central Finsbury seems to be the object of special aversion to some of his politi- cal opponents. Yet he is one of the most courteeus and kind-hearted of men,and his attractive personal qualities have endeared him to some of the people in Finsbury who did not vote for him at the last election.</p>
        <p>The Princess of Wales did not give a glimpse of her face on Tuesday on her way from Balmoral to Sandringham, but preferred to go a long way round by railway to reach her Norfolk home, in order to escape crossing London. She is in excellent health, I hear, but is greatly distressed by the reports of the Czar's health as telegraphed daily by her sister. The Czar is a great favourite with the Princess, it being common knowledge that they were always together at the summer gathering of the family at Copenhagen. •</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain's social programme is a source of great merriment to the Advanced Labour men here. So is his letter declining to meet Mr. llynd- man in public controversy—not because he re- fuses, but because of the reasons,he gives for his refusal. If Mr. Chamberlain, with his vast oppor- tunities and responsibilities, had bestowed one-half the attention upon Socialism that has been bes- towed upon it by thousands of working men, he would never have been led into the blunder of -peaking of different kinds of Socialism. He would have known that there was only one kind,and that it had a very distinct cardinal doctrine which gives it its scientific value as a system of economy, and which was clearly and well expressed in the resolution passed at, the last Trades' Union Con- gress. A working man holding that cardinal doc- trine cannot accept Mr. Chamberlain's industrial and working class programme ; in fact, he cares nothing for it; and his eye is fixed upon much more fundamental objects, Which are utterly in- consistent with Mr. Chamberlain's social ideas and opposed to them root and branch. Mr. Chamberlain has in his programme presented nothing to that older-fashioned section of the democracy which is as yet unpermeated by Socialist influence that is calculated to capture it; and as for the newer de- mocracy, iv simply laughs at his nostrums, and avers that he is blind to the real evils afflicting the labour world.</p>
        <p>Though there may be a few changes in the an- nounced result of the Belgian electious,the charac- ter of it is now accurately known. It has almost wiped out the so-called Liberal party, and left the Roman Catholics and the Socialists face to face. It is impossible to estimate the consequences in the future of the extinction of all that is moderate in Belgian Liberalism, but the situation has to be ac- cepted. The air is now cleared. In the meantime the election has given the Catholics a predominant position which will only nourish and strengthen more rapid:y the Socialism that is apparently des- tined to throw them ultimately from power. But the issue of the elections is both startling and instructive, though it by no means follows that what has happened in Roman Catholic Belgium wortld happen in Protestant England.</p>
        <p>Cardinal Vaughan kept the secret of his attitude towards the School Board controversy well until he thought proper to reveal it formally to thd world. The manifesto he has caused to be read in all the Roman Catholic churches of the metropolis advis- ing the Roman Catholic electors to support the Church of England party, describes the whole con- troversy as one between denominational education and godlessness. This is quite in accordance with the whole theory of the Homan Catholic position on the subject of education, and the manifesto, although it is leading to great exasperation in many quarters, need surpri,e no one. The only policy , open for the Roman Catholic section!of the voters, , I so long as the controversy was viewed as a merely religious one, was either to abstain from voting al- I together or to support the Church party. Many of the opponents of that party believed and expected that the Catholic voters would stay away from the poll; but since the Card.inal has marshalled them practically under the banner of the English Church for the moment, we shall now see concentrated on the other side an intense combination ot Progres- sive and Dissenting forces to resist this strange Catholic and Anglican alliance. If the question at stake is of such importance as to produce this singular clerical combination, it is also of sufficient importance to produce a counter-combination of the most discordant elements of Liberalism, and , that is what will happen.</p>
        <p>• About three hundred organ grinders arrive in London every June from, Italy and leave again about October. The Post Office makes four thou•and pounds a year by unclaimed money orders.</p>
        <p>11.10XON'S NURSERY BOON (Unequalled for DETROYING NITS, and PARASITES in the HUMAN HAIR.) OF CHEMISTS and the PROPRIETOR, J. L. 11 0 X 0 N, 252, PARK-ROAD. LIVERPOOL. Cd. and Is. per box. Post Free 7'E and is. me:it:on I:111!pacr.</p>
