      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATUIWAY, OCTOBER 20, 1894. LADIES' LETTER. THE DOUBLE LIFE: OR, THE HAMPTON MYSTERY. By the Author of " DARKWOOD."</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATUIWAY, OCTOBER 20, 1894. LADIES' LETTER. THE DOUBLE LIFE: OR, THE HAMPTON MYSTERY. By the Author of " DARKWOOD."</p>
        <p>The Queen in her fifty-seven years of power has seen every throne in the world vacated at least once, and some of them several times. I</p>
        <p>n China they tie a red cord round a baby's wrists, so that it may grow up quiet and obedient. Should a child turn out bad, they say, "his parents for- got to bind his wrists." People should never go in the early morning to get boots and shoes fitted. In the latter part oi the day the feet are at their maximum size. Emperors and empresses, kings and queens, write to each other as brother and sister ; reigning grand dukes also enjoy this privilege when addressing kings, but sovereigns not possessing royal honours are designated as cousin. Miss Lillian Russell,the American singer. is very beau- tiful. Her hair and complexion are said to be the most wonderful in America. The former is like spun silk,and golden in colour. The latter is exquisitely fresh. Miss Russell may settle here permanently. A big toy-maker in London received, the other day,an order from no less a personage than the Czar of All the Russias. Few people know how strong is his love for a plaything. He has in his collection some of the prettiest models ef ironclads imaginable. One of them cost over one thousand pounds. Mrs. C. Duplock, collector for the vestry of St. Giles Without, Cripplegate, has successfully vindicated the claims pf women to be considered capital rate collectors. When the lady succeeded her husband in the post some critics prognosticated a complete and dismal failure ; but she has has managed to reduce the arrears from £l9B to £97, and hopes soon to wipe them out alto- gether. Miss Edith Pechey Phipson, who recently resigned her Government appointment as Senior Physician to the Carna Hospital, has returned to Bombay, where she in- tends to resume her private practice. She did splendid service in organising and carrying on the work of the hospital, which was regarded by many as a model of what such hospitals should be.</p>
        <p>A Millionaire and his Daughters. Mr. John D. Rockefeller, the American millionaire, whose fortune was founded on" lie," is a stern parent of the fine old type, Not long ago one of his daughters became informally engaged to a penniless sprig of one of the Italian ducal houses. When the father was approached on the subjeet of settlements, he offered to 66 put up "a cheque for a sum equal to the amount that the young nobleman's family were willing to advance, but refused to give a penny more. The result was that the Italian returned to his native shores still unattached. Mr. Rockefeller has lately followed this up by announc- ing, for the benefit of titled paupers in general, and Ills daughters in particular, that the latter are not to rega with a paltry twenty thousand pounds a piece,and le ce theth emr es se tl voefs hiss ,h, epiires,s, etso, as he intends to cut them 01 public institutions.</p>
        <p>A Famous Painter. Lady Butler, whose picture "Scotland for Ever" has made such a success, has unique opportunities for the study of military subjects. To he the wife of a General of Brigade at Aldershot, and to live in camp among soldiers and horses-_.a man himself has hardly better opportunities than this for the exercise of the military painter's craft. Anyhow, if a man has better opperrtun- sties, he has not found himself able in modern England to make use of them. For amongst English artists, without distinction of sex, Lady Butler stands by her- self as a delineator of the soldier and the horse—most of all of the soldier and the horse in action. Her earlier picture, "The Roll Call," was the Academy success of the year. The Queen, unwilling to wait till the exhibi- tion was over, and unable to come to the picture, made the unique request that the picture might come to her. On the day it went to Windsor, a blank space appeared for the first time in Academy annals on its exhibition walls. All around there seemed to be a breaking of the record.</p>
        <p>Aches and Pains.</p>
        <p>The meaning of aches and be better under- stood if we consider for a moment the exact significance of pain. Everybody knows that pain is associated in some way with nerves, and people are often heard talk- ing learnedly about nerves when in reality they know very little about them. Every part of the body is says " Cassell' s Family Magazine," abundently supplied with nerves, which are of two kinds—motor nerves, which carry messages from the brain to the muscles, and thereby enable us to move ; and sensory nerves, which convey impressions to the brain frem the sensory organs, such as the eye, the ear, and the skin. The brain receives the impressions and interprets them, turning them into what we call sensations, and according to the nature of the sensation we call it pleasurable or painful. We thus see that in many cases the nerves themselves have only a subsidary share in the matter ; they correspond merely to telegraph wires, and transmit mes- sages from on part of the body to another. If the connectien be broken at any point, messages can no longer travel--for example, if the nerves of the arm are cut, we should feel no pain, even if the hand were com- pletely burnt and destroyed. We are now in a position to underntand how pain may originate. It may depend upon an unhealthy state of the brain, upon injury or disease of a sense organ, or sometimes it may depenol upon the condition of the nerve itself. In many in- stances the difference between pleasure and pain, de- pends simply on the amount of stimulation which is ap- plied. There may be only a comparatively slight differ- ence between the heat which warms us pleasantly and that which scorches us, giving rise to a distinctly pain- ful sensation. The eye is specially sensitive to such differences. It is stimulated by light, and when the light is too intense or glaring, as when the sun shines brightly after a recent fall of snow, we soon begin to suffer from aching and pain in the eyes.</p>
        <p>[The following is a synopsis of that portion of "The Double Life" which has been already' published. The heroine is the Lady Beat- rice Hampton. The hero is GeOffrey Trevel- yan (nephew of Lord Trevelyan). Lord Adlowe, Geoffrey's cousin, had drugged him, and got him into such a false position while under the influence of the drug, that his uncle, Lord Trevelyan, believed Geoffrey intended to assassinate him. The old lord's wrath was so great that Geoffrey had to flee to South America to escape his vengeance, where it was believed he had died. But he, in fact, returned to England disguised as a Spaniard, and the Lady Beatrice had secretly married him; and he, under the name of Count Arevalo, with their children, were living in seclu- sion at a place called The Evergreens. Lord Ad- lowe, who had just returned to England after eight years' absence, determines to win the hand of the Lady Beatrice, whose father urges her to marry him. Adlowe does not know that she has been privately married, but yet he suspects she has some cherished secret, and sets a detective to watch her. Giralda, her eldest daughter, supposing that her mother is an actress, and that her parents are poor, resolves to aid them, and so secretly leaves home, and in looking for a situation, by chance engages herself as secretary to old Lord Trevelyan, her father's deadiest foe. The old lord takes a fancy to her, not knowing who she is, and observes that she has eyes like those of his once favourite nephew, Geoffrey. Wishing to adopt her as his grand- daughter, he takes her from his country seat up to London to find her mother, and goes to the Hay- market Theatre, where Giralda, suspects her mother is playing. At the theatre, Giralda dis- covers her mother in the Lady Beatrice Hamp- ton, and the mother at the same time discovers her daughter under the protection of Lord Trevelyan. The mother and daughter subsequently have a secret interview, and it is arranged that Giralda, who is now for the first time let into the secret of her parents' life, shall continue to live with Lord Trevelyan, and if possible soften his heart in her father's favour. But Lord Adlowe tracks the Lady Beatrice to her home at The Evergreens, and find- ing how he has been befooled, has G.ralda carried off from Trevelyan Park, with the intention of forcing her to become his wife. But she is rescued by young Lord Grosvenor, who falls in love with her, and devotes himself to the clearing of her father'., name from the crime which Adlowe had fastened upon him. Adlowe, in quitting The Evergreens, carried off Giralda's little brother, Fay, and had him hidden in a remote place in the country, and then returned to Trevelyan Park to re-abduct Giralda, whom he had found in the shrubbery and seized, and whose screams brought the old lord to her r scue. The succeeding chapter tells what then occurred.]</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXVII.</p>
        <p>THE EBVOLITTION. They did not know how hate can burn In hour is Tice changed from soft to stern Nor all tht: false and fatal zeal The convert, of revenge can feel.</p>
        <p>BYRON.</p>
        <p>The ,first impulse of Giralda, as the Marquis of Trevelyan so suddenly and opportunely made his appearance in the shrubbery, was to Hy to his side and implore his protection. But the struggle with her emotions had left her weak, and she could only sit down ali'd look at his lordship with a gaze made up of mingled dread, terror, and gratitude that he had saved her from her enemy.</p>
        <p>The first inipulso of Lord Adlowe also was to flee. Conyzering it, he faced his uncle with desperation and defiance.</p>
        <p>"'ua hero?" eriea Lord Trevelyan, glowering at his; nephew. "And clandestinely ? What does this mean? Did you not receive the message I sent you by Wihy, forbidding you ever to et foot again in the house or grounds of Trevelyan Park while I live? Degenerate wretch 1 how dared you face again this innocent young girl, after your recent outrage on her liberties? I would do well to chastise you for the base hound that you are!" Ile lifted his heavy staff menacingly, and drew up his iiercuiea,n figure, and his black eyes flashed. under his thick white brows, and every line of his rugged, grand old face expressed a stern and lofty contempt kr his nephew. - -</p>
        <p>"1 received your message," answered .Adlowe, sullenly. "I did not intend to intrude into your house, my lorsl." And his tone grew mocking. "I desired only an interview with your niece." "And you chose to come upon her when she was aloDi; and unprotected," sneered the marquis. " Au ! ' he added, a sudden thought striking him, "you were about to abduct her again. Her scream —nee fright—l see it all now." And lightning leaped from his eyes. "It seems that I was knit in Little." Adlowe shrank a Attie before the old lord's blaz- ing v. tant. "For your ether offence against my niece," con- tinued Lord Trevelyan, in a voice of concentrated wrath, "1 was willing that you should suffer no other punishment than banishment from my house and the stings of your own conscience. The for this last offence you shall remember while you live. Choose between a public prosecu- tion, or a horse whipping from these premises, and choose quickly.' Lord Adlowe's face grew livid. A streak of sul en red raised itself on his cheeks, as if from the impriut of the threatened lash. "Take car- !" he cried, warningly. "Take care ! ISefote you threaten, old man, make sure that I. was not hire by appointment with your niece. Miss Arevalo,- he said, turning ,o her, "you will not dare deny teas; you appointed to meet me at this hour in the hrubbery. You will not dare, I say—'' " But I do dare !" interrupted Giralda, spring- ing up in glow of royal indiguation. "I deny that y u were here oy my appointment, Lord Adlowe. You came mien Inc unawares, as I sat here alone. You offered- me marriage, .e.id when I again de- clined, you threatened to again carry me off. You advanced upon tile for the purpose of capturing me, and it wise then I screamed for aid. Aillowe glared at the maiden in an awful fury, .as if he ionged to annihilate her. Lord Trevelyan drew up his towering figure, sung back his haughty head, and advanced upon his Dept' w with up if Led staff. "Hold!' cried Adlowe, waving him back and speaking hissingly. " Uncle, I have something to say to you—a confession to make." The marquis half halted, impressed by the un- governable rage of his nephew. " She has brought it ou herself," continued Ad- love, pointing a., Giralda, and flinging all caution or consideration to the winds. "I offerral to keep her secret, buL she would not have my friendship. You shall know all, 'my lord—the secret of this beauty—the setret of lier—" "Oh, no! no !" cried Giralda,, convulsed with terror. "Oh ! my lord !my lord ! have mercy Adlowe's lips wreathed themselves into a sardonic tie feaeed towards the yntnig girl, a grow- ing light in its sinisler eyes. you a chance to save yourself and them," he whispered—" a last chance. Will you be - my wife "On, no, no I' exclaimed Giralda, shudderingly, while; with angu.eie "I cannot, my lord ! can- not 1" The marquis looked from one to the other, be- WHeeled Hi) stiliel. " What does this mean '?" he demanded, dis- trust fully .and impatiently. 'lt means, my lerd, ' responded Adlowe, deli bet ate ly, lila horning eyes fixed on the shrinking figure of the tor uteri girl.—'' it means— Shall I teh hint, Ciraltta !" he said, in a quick whisper. " Will ou still refuse to 'save your father The etai ;en with difficulty repressed the shriek that awe,: to iitr lips. It Wits 3 a horrible position for her, with r Lender, sensitive heart, full of a daughter', fondest love. She struggled with her- self. She was tempted to yield to Adlowe's monstrous demand. But, .11.)i, o'. rim 61 111 iS w fel peril could she be 4 e.seei.ska ei her litetime, to the Instincts of her soul. Her love for her parents, for Lord Grosvenor, for the right, forbade her yield- ing. With a moan of utter anguish she shook her bead, and said, in a hushed whisper: "I cannot save them I I cannot marry you, Lord Adlowe " The anger of the profligate lord deepened to temporary madness. • "What is all this mystery ? " demanded Lord Trevelyan. "What are you saying to Giralda,, Adlowe?" 14,1 MY lord," Asi.isi Acilvweolot</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>MY DEAREST NELLIE,</head>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>Toques are greatly worn, and are peculiarly suited to this time of year. A model I have seen shows a straw shape, which, however, is so closely trimmed that the straw is scarcely apparent. Folds of nut-brown velvet band the brim, and finish the front with a stylish bow; the whole of the velvet is edged with a passementerie of cut jet, which is remarkably effective laid thus over the colour. A black ostrich feather is added at the back, and a crimson rose appears in front. Here is a model for a very dainty hat, and one that may be copied with advantage, while to those w.bp cannot afbrd to be always buying, let them rtrip their summer straw, and atter thoroughly b:ushing, to rid it of every particle of dust, pro- ceed to paint it over, with an admixture of gum and back ink—using three parts ink and one part gum—which will entirely renovate its appearance. A piping of black velvet may then be sewn under the brim, and a few long looped bows of the new oh,Jrry-coured velvet be used as trimming. If you have a couple of ostrich tips at hand, all the better; place tbeEe on the left side of the crown, bend the brim of your ohapsau to shape, and you will own a pretty hat tAt will carry yo a through for some weAs most sueceqiiully.</p>
        <p>Fils'ets are being made so full that many of them arc lined with flounces to make them tand out; petticoats are flounced on the outside to add to the ballooning effect, and well starched white jupons are naturally preferred to all others for the eutfici,ent reason that, while throwing out the skirt, they are light and cool to wear. A mode that is to some extent gaining ground is that of making the lower half of the skirt of a material differing from that of the upper part. The fashion has so far been chiefly utilised for heavy guipure and other laces over silk, but in some dresses lately made the lower half of the silk skirt is composed of very rich brocade joined on under a narrow bead galon. The corsage is usually of the brocade, with a fanotfully•cut jacket or corselet, and sleeves of the plain silk trimmed with galon. A beautiful after- noon at home dress which has been brought under my notice has the skirt slightly trained, with full bodice and draped sleeves in iron-grey Bengaline silk, spotted with a darker tint, %r(1 trimmed with cream Venetian lace, forminl a Louis XIII. collar. Rows of edgings simulated a tunic front, and caught down at the lower part with windmill bows in grey moire silk. It has an Empire .?ash and bow to match, with long close sleeves in lace over</p>
        <p>Tailor-msdA coats and skirts are fashionable in pn cloth, hcme-spun, chequered tweeds, serge, 41k worn over waistcoats made of brocade, moire and even cloth lined with flannel to afford auffir.dent protection. The waistcoats are likewise made with sleeves. Both single and double- breasted styles are in vogue, also open or closed to throat, and buttons in out steel are put on them. There is nothing nicer than a soft-felt bo,lt-shape hat, with a dent in the centre, for use wi, h these, either dark, grey, or black, whichever suits beet thg tweed of the costume. There appears to be a decided reaction in favour of coats and jackets, all In the Directoire style, double-breasted or open over vests of different colour, or waistcoats double- breasted ending at the waist; for smarter gowns the waistcoat has a small bias basque which curves</p>
        <p>ever the hips and comes into a point is front. A smart costume shows a coat and skirt of fawn box cloth, with strapped seams, and coat out so as to be worn either open or closed. Yours lovingly,</p>
        <p>JBNITITL</p>
