      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>SAVAGE WARFARE.</head>
        <p>SAVAGE WARFARE.</p>
        <p>AN INDIAN'S CUNNING.</p>
        <p>"I hadn't been long standing here, peering round till my eyes ached, when I heard a rustling about fifty yards away. I looked and saw an American hg of the sort that are common enough in these parts, coming down the glade opposite, crawling along the ground and sniffing to the right, and left—just as if he'd no busi- ness in life but to sniff about. for nuts under the fallen leaves and all about the roots of the trees. Boars are common enough, so I gave him a glance and didn't take much notice for some minutes. But after awhile,thinks I to myself—' No doubt the others kept their eyes about them sharp enough,and were only took in by neg- lecting something that seemed of no account ; so being on the alarm and having no idea what was to be feared and what was not, I woke up after some minutes, and determined to keep my eyes on it and watch how it passed in and out among the trees. For I thought, if it comes on an Indian skulking about yonder, I may be able to learn something from its movements. Indians are thick enough here and to spare ; but they're not so thick as nuts, for all that. So I kept glancing at the hog, and then looking round and glancing again. Not another creature was in sight ; not a leaf rustling. And then, all of a sudden—l caret tell why—it struck me as queer that the animal was shuffling around among the trees and making off to the right, seemingly for the thick coppice just behind my post. I didn't want any- thing behind me. you may be sure, nct even a hog, and as it was now only a few yards from my coppice I kept my eye more constantly on it, and cast up in my mind whether I should fire or not. It seemed foolish enough to rouse you all up by shooting a pig I I fingered my trigger, and couldn't for the life of me make up my mind what to do. I looked and looked, and the morel looked the bigger fool I thought myself for being alarmed at it. It would be a rare jest against me that I mistook a pig for an Indian ; ana this was a hog sure enough. You've all seen scores of them, and know how they move. Well, this one was for all the world like any other, and I was almost saying to myself that 'twas more like the average hog than any hog I'd ever seen, when just as it had got close to the thicket I fancied it gave an unusual spring. At any rate, fancy or Co, I didn't hesitate. I took crol aim, and directly I did so, felt sure I was right. The beast stopped in a hesitating sort of way, aud by that I knew it saw what I was about, though up to the moment it had never seemed to be noticing me. An Indian's trick, for a sovereign,' thought I, and pulled the trigger. It dropped over like a stone; and then, as I stood there, still doubt- ing if it were a trap that I should fall into by running to look, I heard a groan—and the groan of a man too. I loaded my musket and ran up to it. I had shot an Indian, sure enough, and that groan was his last. He had wrappod himself in the hog's skin so completelv,and his hands and feet were so neatly hid, and he imitated the animal's walk and noise so cleverly, that. I swear, if you saw the trick played again, here before you, your honour would doubt your honour's eyes. And seeing him at a distance, in the shadow of the trees, no man who had not lost three comrades before him. as I had, would ever have guessed. Here's the knife and toma- hawk the villain had about him. You see, once in the coppice he had only to watch his moment for throwing off the skin and jumping on me from behind; and a dig in the back before a man had time to fire his piece was "easy work enough. After that it's envier still to drag the body off and hide it under a heap of leaves. The rebels pay these devils by the scalp, and no doubt if your honour looks atsout, you'll find the collection our friend here has already made to-day."—From "The World of Adventure " for October.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>/,7 117 . ..t a , . P fritithisf hiarderi.</head>
