      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <p>stirring nis scorching race rrom mat or his in- tended victim. "I have made a discovery. It is that your assassin nephew,,your intended robber and murderer, the infamous Geoffrey Trevelyan, still lives." Giralda uttered a low, wild wail. The marquis staggered as if he had received a mortal wound, and leaned heavily against a tree, staring wildly at Adlowe. "Geoffrey alive ! " he muttered, incredulously. "It is not too late' Giralda," muttered Adlowe, under his breath. "Speak !" Giralda slowly shook her head. "You said he was alive—that Geoffrey was alive?" asked the marquis, unheeding this by- play. 'Alive, and in England !" cried Adlowe, shrilly, raising his voice. "That letter from Brazil, an- nouncing his death, was a forgery. He played a clever game against us, that's all. He came back to England, and has been in hiding here since, un- der an assumed name, waiting for you, my lord, to die, that he may come forward and inherit the title and estates." "Alive, and in England !" rereated the mar- quis, in a dazed way. "So near me all these years, when I thought him dead! Alive !" " Yes, alive !" reiterated Adlowe' savagely. " And lying off in his secret web, like a spider waiting for prey—waiting for your death !" All the fierce and revengeful passions of the old lord's soul were stirred like a nest of serpents by Adlovve's words and tones.</p>
        <p>Where is he?" he cried, looking around him, as if he expected to behold his bated nephew ad- vance from the close-settling shadows. "Is he here?"</p>
        <p>"Oh, no !" cried Adlowe, with a cruel laugh. "He is living near London. I have just been to see him at his luxurious home. I can tell you, my lord, that he is no miser in his living. He knew rne at once.- And I knew him, despite his clever diguise. He spoke of you, and jeered at you, just as he used to do. He offered me a handsome sum to tell him the exact state of your health, ad just how long you are likely to live—" The ma,uuis threw up his arms with a cry of anguish. fie doubted none of Adlowe's asser- tions, for the profligate's manner in assuring him that Geoffrey lived were full of an unmistakable sincerity. "Geoffrey is secretly married, and has a family," pursued Adlowe, not swerving his gaze from the young girl. "So that even if you succeeded in sending him to a penal colony to expiate his crime, you could not deprive his offspring of their inheri- tance, nor prevent his knowing of their grandeur. You see how cleverly he has outwitted us. But be has guarded against being taken, I doubt not. He had a pretty scheme—a very pretty scheme,_ my lord, against you which remains to be unfolded." "What was it?" asked the marquis, in a broken voice.</p>
        <p>"lie has a daughter, beautiful, accomplished, and winning. With her aid, he resolved to outwit and mock you, my lord—" Giralda interrupted her enemy by a pitiful cry. "It is still not too late, Giralda," whispered Lord Adlowe with feverish eagerness. "Promise me what I ask, and I will save your father. Prom- ise, and I will save you from my uncle's scorn and hatred." " I cannot promise," answered Giralda, brokenly. "But as you will pray for mercy one day at the hands of God, have mercy on me now." Her looks her words, her attitude, so utterly despairing, all declared her resolution to be unal- terable. With a fierce gesture, Adlowe turned from her, hatred seething in his heart, inspiring him with an unwonted recklessness. "As I was saying, my .lord," he resumed, "Geoffrey has a beautiful daughter, as artful as a siren, as innocent in looks as a dove. Geoffrey has gone on from bad to worse since you cast him off; and he informed himself of your miserly habits and of the probable amount of your savings, and formed the project of sending this girl of his to your home. He hoped and he espected that she would wind herself into your heart, and win for herself the savings of your years of penuriousness. He thought that this girl would procure from you his Pardon, and his reinstatement, wiyh his family at the Park." " Let him send his daughter 1" ejaculated the - rnarquis, with brows as black as a thunder-cloud. "Let him try his pretty game Adlovre looked again at Giralda. She was look- ing at him with a world of beseeching in her eyes, but there was no sign of yielding in her coun- tenance or manner. He saw that she would die rather than marry him. He saw that not even to save her idolised father would she perjure her soul. - -</p>
        <p>He pursued his false narration remorselensly. "At a moment most opportune Mr Geoffrey's schemes, my lord," he said, "you advertised for a nurse, and Geoffrey sent his daughter, trusting to her woman's cunning, to procure the vacant situation. He had trained her Most carefully. She came to the Park, she told a pitiful story—tke story she had learned by heart---" "Oh, my lord, it's false ! " interposed Giralda, • ,ber face white with a death like pallor, her eyes like frightened stars. "Oh, uncle, believe me, he speaks falsely!" She ran before the marquis, clasping her hands in anguished pleading. She would have knelt at his feet, but that he put out one slaiklag hand and gently restrained her. What do you know about it, Giralda?" he said, still not comprehending, still bewildered. "No one shall harm you.'" 'How Geoffrey Trevelyan would laugh in his soul after that assurance,' said Adlowv, NV"itil a demon's sneer. " How well your lordship has been entrapped by your assassin nephew! How completely you have fallen a victim to the wiles of his siren daughter 1 You have made a will bequeathing her a fortune. You have taken her into your heart. You have made her mistress of your house—" " Wllo ? " "Geoffrey Trevelyan's daughter I That siren- faced girl at your feet, Giralda Trevelyan 1" The marquis now turned his gaze fully upon the now kneeling girl. Her white face gleamed through the twilight like the face of a dead woman. Her eyes' wild and anguished, shone on him in their full splendour—those eyes SO like those of Geoffrey Trevelyan ! The truth of her identity rushed upon his soul in a quick flood! He saw not her sweet, innocent face, her looks of frightened pleading, her utter terror—he saw only that she had under her fair brows, eyes fatally like those of the man he cruelly hated. • " Ah, I have been duped—duped !" he hissed between his closed teeth. "It is the second - time I And I believed her as innocent as an angel I She has warmed herself into nay heart as Geoffrey did, only to sting me more cruelly. Oh, Heaven!" and his voice rang out in a great burst of anguish, and he ilooked wildly upward. "Surely Heaven must pitf one so'wronged and deceived as I!' " Uncle ! uncle ! " cried Giralda, clinging to his knees in wild despair, "papa is innocent. Oh, forgive him ! He did not send me here. I did not know until we went to London that papa was Geoffrey Trevelyan—that I was really your niece by blood! Oh, uncle, take papa back! He never banned you—never 1 " "She acknowledges it !" said the marquis, hollowly, continuing to gaze upward. "She owns that she is the daughter of Geoffrey Trevelyan. Until now I had hoped 1 " "My Lord !" said Adlowe, bitterly, "I came here from abroad unknowing that Geoffrey lived. That girl's eyes aroused my suspicions. I hired a detective and tracked out my unworthy cousin Geoffrey. I learned the whole story of his base plotting. I beheld myself threatened with poverty, after years of expected wealth. In my despair, I resolved to meet Geoffrey's plots with counter- plots. I resolved to marry his daughter, and thus secure myself against a penniless future. I pro- posed marriage to her, and she refused me. I carried her on, and she escaped. 1 came here Is carry her off again, if she should again refuse me. &amp;111 a reckless, desperate man, my lord. I have been outwitted in every way,, defeated in all my dearest schemes, and now my infamous cousin steps in between me and my expected inheritance." In the depths of his own despair, Lord Trevel- yan thought less harshly of the wrong-doing of his nephew. "Say no more, Adlowe," he said, huskily. "We will join hands in pursuit of Geoffrey. The sum I intended for this girl shall be yours. We will be revenged upon Geoffrey, and punish him as he deserves. We. will crush his family. You and I will work together. He shall see that he has mocked at me too soon." Overcome with joy, Adlovve stretched out his hand. Tne marquis did not take it. " Uncle ! Oh, uncle 1" moaned Giralda, clasping I his knees, "papa is innocent. He loves you, uncle. Be merciful to him, to mamma, to my poor brothers!" "Hush I" said his lordship, harshly, turning away his head, for he dared not look upon the sweet face he still so dearly loved, and be dared not to 'ger trunnion&amp; voices which yet had</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>1 power t stir his soul to its lowest depths. "1 loved you, and you deceived me. I might have known that you would have been base and schem- ing, for you had his eyes ! Out of my sight, girl ! I cannot bear to look upon your face. Away! Leave my house! Let me never look into your treacherous eyes again ! " He broke from her roughly. Giralda tottered to her feet, and stood facing him, compelling him by her steadfast gaze to look at her. He could just see that her cheeks glowed, and that her eyes shone like stars under her clustering rings of hair, and that she bore herself like an insulted prin- cess. "Lord Trevelyan," she said, in a tone that en- forced his attention, "you have cruelly wronged papa You have cruelly wronged me! In send- ing me away you send from you the heart that loves you best in all the world! Despite your harshness and cruel prejudices and revengeful- ness, I love you now, uncle. If you should ever need me I will come to you. This man," and she indicated Lord Adlowe, "has been your evil genius! It was he who concocted that plan to ruin poor papa! It is he who has persecuted me from the hour he beheld me under your roof. Some day, my lord, you will know the truth. Some day justice must triumph and wickedness be punished! When I can prove papa's innocence, and Lord Adlowe's guilt, we shall meet again Till then, farewell 1" She turned away, gliding like a spirit into the shadows. Lord Trevelyan aroused himself from the brief trance into which he had fallen, and his love for Giralda asserted itself, and, forgetting all the charges of Adlowe, forgetting his hatred of Geoffrey Trevelyan--remembering only that mighty love—he stretched out his arms and cried, in the anguish of his storm-tossed soul: "0, Giralda! my darling! come back! come back 1" _</p>
        <p>(To be continued).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>NARROW EoUAYII, OF A GENERAL'S DAUGHTER.</head>
        <p>NARROW EoUAYII, OF A GENERAL'S DAUGHTER.</p>
        <p>A TIMELY RESCUE. (Fam THE HAMPSHIRE INDEPENDENT.) There is nothing more interesting, nay, fascinating, than the talk of our brave defenders, 'who have served</p>
        <p>their Queen and country in far dis.tant lands. To talk with an Indian officer, hearing his reminiscences and</p>
        <p>adventures, is what those who have enjoyed it always</p>
        <p>appreciate. Consequently (writes a special reporter of</p>
        <p>the "Hampshire Independent ") I was delighted to receive instructions to interview Lieutenant-General Clement R. Shaw, who has won his spurs in India, and</p>
        <p>s now living, with his familv,in honourable retirement, at S. Paul's Vicarage, Shanklin, Isle of Wight. The</p>
        <p>Vic arage is a model haven of lest. Enclosed in its own</p>
        <p>grounds, it rests beneath the shadow of the sacred pile from which it obtains its name. In the trees were nests of sweet songsters, who are never disturbed. But there</p>
        <p>was no time to wax poetic. I had grasped the bell-put</p>
        <p>and given it one tug vben the dcor opened, and the</p>
        <p>General stood before me. You knew he was a soldier at</p>
        <p>once. His manl, upright bearing, his smile, his pleas-</p>
        <p>ant voice—all told you that you stood in the presence of one of Nature's gentlemen but, alas! he held a time- table, and I felt that the interview must needs be abort.</p>
        <p>However, he ushered me in, and at once put me at my ease by his affable conversation. "I am afraid," he said, "that you have come a long distance; but let me know the precise object of your</p>
        <p>visit." I explained to the General that I vas most anxious with his consent, to obtain some personal explanation as to the narrow escape I had heard one of his daughters had recently experienced.</p>
        <p>At that he b;ightened visibly. "You must know," he said," I'm just a bit of an enthusiast on this point: but</p>
        <p>the tale is very short. Nearly two years ago my daughter—the one you referred to—can-.e home from</p>
        <p>India, and when I joined her in London I found her il in bed. She had rheumatic and neuralgic pains; shel</p>
        <p>was perfectly bloodless, listless, and in a generally weak and prostrate condition. We called in a doctor, and I am bound to say that he succeeded in getting her out of bed ; but she was still absolutely colourless, and was in great wretchedness, and suffering from what I should call anemia or bloodlessness. She had a kind of fever,</p>
        <p>nervous headache, and other pains. Well, we went to Bembridge, in the Isle of Wight, and there I first heard of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Indeed, I was recommended to try them, and I did FO. My daughter took the first box, arid they had a marvellous effect upon her. She regained her colour; she lust her pains ; and became altogether different, and after she had been taking them some time she came in one day after her walk and said to me, Why, father, I feel quite a glow through me.' She went on taking the Pills, and I am glad to tell you that she recovered cempletely. I recommended Dr.-Williams' Pink Pills to all with whom I came in contact, and all who tcok them derived great benefit therefrom. We came to live at Sha.eklin, and just now my daughter has had another attack. She resorted again to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and she is getting better very rapidly." " You said, General," I remarked, " that you heard of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People at Bembridge. Would you mind telling me' how that came about ?" " Oh, yes," he replied. " I have a sister at Jersey, and she has taken them for a very long time, and has always recommended them to other people, and found them to do her a great deal of good, and all to whom she has recommended them have been befitted ; and Imy self, when I have heard of peeple being ill, have taken them or sent them some of these Pills." " With what result P' I asked.</p>
        <p>"Well," was.the reply, "my daughter's recovery was due to Dr. William's Pink Pills for Pale People without a doubt. Their effects were marvellous, and I did not speak without first ascertaining the facts. I have had a great deal to do with medical men in this country and in India, and they laugh at the idea that patent medicines cure any disease. But I have given you this case, and that I knew ; I won't deal with any beyond my own personal experience and knowledge,further than to say that I believe the results to have been satis- factory."</p>
        <p>"Then I may say, General, that y our daughter's re- covery was attributable to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills ? " " Decidedly," replied the General, " and as I said, I have heard of other cases of a similar character, but into them I will not go, as I prefer only to tell you what I know to be true from my own personal observa- tion."</p>
        <p>" In the second place," I said, "may I make your name public as giving me the information? Your name and standing would show how valuable is the testimony." "Oh," replied the General, "I see no reason for withholding it. Yes, you may publish it, and I wish I c.ould have given you more information." And so our interview ended. As I left the pleasan Rectory and its genial inhabitant I could not help re- flecting that whilst the General had put down the sword, he was doing a humane, kindly,and peaceful act in telling to sufferers what great good had arisen through the timely use of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale Peeple are a perfect blood builder and nerve restorer, curing such diseases as rheumatism, sciatica, neuralgia, partial paralysis, loco- moto ataxy, St. Vit us' dance,nervous headache,nervous prostration and the tired feeling resulting therefrom, diseases depending upon hntnours in the blood, such as scrofula, chronic erysipelas, &amp;c. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills restore pale and sallow complexions to the glow of health, and are a specific for all the troubles peculiar to the female sex, while in the case of men they effect a radical cure in all cases arising from mental worry,over- work, or excesses of whatever nature. These Pills are manufactured by the Dr. Williams Medicine Company, of 46,1101b0rn Viaduct, London,and are sold by chemists everywhere (but only in boxes with pink wrappers bearing the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People) at 2s. 9d. a box, or six boxes for 13s. 9d. They may be had direct by nost from the Com- pany at the same price. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are never sold in bulk, or by the dozen or hundred, and any dealer who offers substitutes in this form is trying to defraud, and should be avoided. Bicycles are generally cowidered very mo ons ; but some of the Egyptian obeli-' mounted on two-wheeled vehicles rele- blieg the 14 s.</p>
      </div>
