      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WARRINGTON BANDS</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON BANDS</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The season of 1.894 was brought to a close on Saturday afternoon, by a grand united gathering of the members of the association, who met in front of the Town Hall to render a short hut excelleut programme. There were 152 instrumentalists pre- scnt, and the fAlowing• are the hands which took part. Volunteer Band, St. Paul's Band, Borough Band, League of the Cross Band, Buckley-street Band, Latchford Band, Nlessis. Fletcher and Company's Band, and St. George's Mission Band. The clay was an exceilent one, as the weather was everything that could be desired, and as a result, there was a very !are,. audience includinE., the Mayoress (Mrs. E. Greenal!), Mrs. James Fai!enugh,Mr. Robert Pierpoint, M.P., Aldermen Wright and Hutchin- son ; Councillors Shillcock, Smethurst, Dr. Cannell, Alfred Wood, Bennett, and Shaw ; th- Rev. E. C. E. and Mrs. Carleton, the Rev. H. V. and Mts. Pigot, the Rev. A H. Townsend, Major and Mrs. ;Pickmere. Mrs. Smethurst, Mrs. Hill, Mr. and Mrs. Voispy Miss Wilsey, the Town Clerk and Mrs. Whittle, the Borotp-di Surveyor and Mrs. Lonedin, Miss Cinnell, Mr. John Harri3on,Mr. H. Toft, Mr. William Diggle,and Mr. Joseph Tafor (hon. secretary of the Ass.eiation of Binds). At about four o'clock, when all the bands had arrived, and the conductor (Mr. T. Armstronci) had taken his seat on the temporary platform provided for t:o occasion,</p>
        <p>Mr. ROBERT piERpoiNT, Mr., who spoke from the Town Fit steps, said ho thought he mieht h. allowed to- thank the instrumentalists, on behalf of the people Cl'. Warrineton, for the work thee had done .during the vett year for their benefit and amusement. The re-corm,nn which was about to take place would be •h forieth which had been given during. the season,aed tie 3 avrage attendance had bean ehout 4,000 He reerettod that the president of the society, Mr. Down, was iinav i iably absent. Connected with the performance that after. noon there was, as no doubt they all knew, having had to rum the sepintlet of the c allecting Waxes. a callection being made on behalf of the Lifeboat Fund. This con- nection between the one end the other came from an offer made by the bands of their own initiation and of their own free will to the Mayoress to act in so tripod a cause; in fact they had offered, as it were, to collect on behalf of a fund in which the Mayoress was so much interested, namely, the Lifeboat Saturday Fund. He thought he need not detain them by remin.line them of the benefit to their fellow countrymen and countrywoman of the lifeboats. He could only appeal to them that afternoon to give as ley within -their means to such an ' excellent fund ; and in making these few remarks he should be wantine in courtesy and in propriety if he were not t suggest to them, as inhabitants of Warring- ton, the great obleatien they wera under to the worthy Mayor of that town, Mr. Alderman Greenall, and to his exceedingly kind, good ar.d clever wife. (Cheers.) The programme was then proceeded with, but it was only a short one. The only nieces played, except the National Anthem, were "The Tue of War," and a march. Oeing to inadvertence there was no vote of thanks proposed to Mr. J. Taylor,the hnn.secretary,who has been so indefatigihle in his work on behalf of the association. He has been ably assisted by a committee, consisting of the Town Clerk as treasurer, Mr. T. J. Down as president, and the bandmaster and two mem- bers of each of the following bands which have occupied the bandstand, viz., Volunteers, St. Paul's, Borough. League of the Cross,Measrs. Fletcher and Co.,St.George's Mission, Cockhedge Mission, Buckley-st.. Latchford (Christ Church), Industrial *School, and last, but not least, Mr. T. J. Down's band. Altoeether 40 perform- ances have been given during the season. The sum of a was taken on behalf of the Lifeboat fund.</p>
        <p>Wash your sheets with Hunswea. thy SWEET SLEEP will a ways be sweet as roses, fresh as rt sea breezes. HUDSON'S SOAP is * Fine SWEET SHEETS. Powder in Packets.</p>
        <p>Leaves no smell.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. T. W. Mitchell. chairman of the Wholesale operative Society, speaking on Tuesday night at the Co- operative Productive Exhibition, s4id that the idea that the co-operative principle when applied to production failed was a mistake. The Wholesale Society had some- thing like £400.000 invested in manufacturing concerns, and on that, atter allowing for depreciation, they had made a profit of about £70,000</p>
