        <p>PEOPLE'S MEETING AT WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>SPEECHES BY MR.W.CROSFIELD AND OTHERS.</p>
        <p>A well-attended meeting in connection with the autumnal meetings of the Congregational Union was held in Wycliffe Church, Bewsey-street,Warrington, on Thursday night last. Before the meeting commenced, an organ recital was given by Mr. W. H. Payton (organist of the church) with his usual skill. The meeting itself was commenced at eight o'clock, the chairman being Mr. Wm. Crosfield, M.P., and the other speakers were the Rev. J. D. Jones, N1.A.,8.D,, (Lincoln); Mr. Albert Spicer, M.P., and the Rev. W. J. Woods (secretary of the Congregational Union of England and Wales.) Mr. Was. CROSFIELD, M.P., who met with a hearty reception, stated that he had on the platform his friend, Mr. Albert Spicer, with whom he had the honour of being associated in many things. Their history was somewhat similar. When he (the speaker) was fighting in Warrington and was more frequently among them than he had been for some years past, Mr. Spicer was fighting in another part of the country. He found an 1 ungrateful constituency that did not know a good man 1 when they saw one and he was defeated. But he had won a seat since, and was there as an honoured man of their dencmination and the member for the Monmouth Boroughs. The speaker afterwards referred to the Rev. J. D. Jones, M.A., 8.D., of Lincoln, and apologised for the absence of the Rev. J. Guiness Rsgers, of London, but said he had sent the Rev. W. J. Woods, the secretary of the Congregational Union of England and Wales, to take his place. The Congregational Union was holding its autumnal meetings in the neighbouring little town of Liverpool, the town which used to be called Liverpool near Warrington. It was that meeting at Liverpool which had brought them there that night and as there was a difficulty in getting the people of Warrington to their meetings,Mr. Yontrehad br night a meeting to them (applause.) They were there to give them some informa- tion of what they had been saying and doing at their meetings during the week. They had been glorying in their freedom as a church, in their puritan traditions, and they had been hoping that if testing times such as their puritan ancestors experienced were again to occur they might be as staunch and true in meeting difficul- ties and overcoming obstacles as they were in their day (Applause.) They had had a very comprehensive pro- gramme of proceedings at Liverpool. The speaker then dealt with several of the most important features which had marked the Union meetings, including the opening sermon by the Rev. A. Rowland, 8.A., L.L.8., and the missionary and temperance meetings. They had of course, he said had something to say about Dis- establishment. He quoted a statement of Canon Jowett, of Dublin, who spoke from experience of the benefits which had accrued to the Irish Episcopal Church since its disestablishment. Mr. Crosfield said they had no desire to cripple the Church as some people said, but they desired to see the Church increase in use- fulness, and better able to do work for the Master, be- lieving that that would be best accomplished by her freedom from the control of the State. They had, he daresay, brought politics into some of the things that they had said and done during the week. But they found that they could not keep clear of politics They believed in the old saying of the quaker, William Penn, that it was the duty of every Christian man to see that his country was properly governed. So if they brought their religious principles to bear upon their national life they considered that they were doing their duty. (Applause.) They found that they were carry- ing out the principles that were laid dawn for their guidance by their puritan forefathers. After referring to the demands made from those in authority by Crom- well 250 years ago, which in some respects, he thought, had a likeness to the Newcastle programme, he said it was a pity that so many of those old demands should have to be again repeated at so late a day, but the fact that they had been striving for so many of those rights and that they were still hoping for them, and knew would ultimately secure them, that fact made it the more important that they should go on striving and working for those rights which their puritan forefathers demanded but did net get. (Applause.) Rev. J. D. JONES, M.A., 8.D.. of Lincoln, said he had been to Warrington before, but he now expressed his pleasure at making his first appearance in that church under the presidenay of Mr. Wm. Crosfield, who had long been their friend and was so still. The magic letters M.P. at the end of his name represented Warrington's loss and Lincoln's great gain. (Hear, hear.) If he were asked to describe the last quarter of a century he should ascribe its improvement to the people's power. During the last century England had passed through a great re- volution,which, he thanked God, had been bloodless and one ef peace. It had resulted in a transference of power from the titled nobles and barons to the mass of the people. He believed in the old motto" Trust the peo- ple." 'There was a doctrine held by certain classes of persons and preached consistently and persistently at all times ; a doctrine which he truly abhorred, viz.—that a man's love for his country was to be measured by the amount of prc petty he possessed and the quantity of money he had invested in his country's banks. Be , entirely repudiated the idea that a man possessed of many acres loved his country better than he (Applause.) The past session of Parliament had taught them that those men with such a large vested interest were prepared US do anything for their own advantage. Patriotism was not the exclusive prerogative of the titled, but was also to be found among the " sons of the soil." A man's right to have a voice in the affairs of his country did not come from his millions,bnt from his manhood and English blood. In that they stood equal. This was the day of the people's power, and the House of Lords might be compared to a piece of antique furni- ture which was of no use except as an ornament. When that House pitted itself against the people, there could be only one result. (Applause.) Kings, nobles, yeomen, and country gentlemen had had their opportunity, and right well had they used it, but now it was the people's opportunity. (Hear, hear.) He said there was nothing more deplorable than using such a power as they now possessed wrongly, and he ex- horted them ta use their power under the fear of God and their consciences. The French Revolution was cited by him as a lesson and example of power used wrongly. He also took a lesson from the fear of and faith in God held by Oliver Cromwell's " Tron- sides." It was not right, he thought, that men should squander in idle pleasures what they never earned. whde other men had to work by the "sweat of their brow "to earn their living and procure something to eat. (Hear, hear.) The rev, gentleman also said that the most press- ing danger to England's greatness arose more from her sins than from any outside halluence. In this he was supported by authorities as great as the president of the Trades Union Congress, who st.o,d that what they hscl most to fear was drink and gambling. The power of the people should be used to stay the evils of drink. Eirr- land's prosperity could never continue under the influence of so much drink and gamhlines He had heard that- to keep up the revenue they ninst I:een uo the drink traffie, hut if the revenue was to be kept no by the ruin of the lives of the people, then let the reverme perish. (Applause.) Ile exhorted them to use their power to mitigate these evils. The way to make England strong was to make England righteous, for righteousness exalteth a nation. .(teud applause.) MT. ALBERT STWER, M.P., said he had no claim for their forbearance unless it was that, having won the sympathy of a great constituency, he felt that he was brought into • touch with all constituencies where the people were duly represented. (Applause.) The mo- ment a man became the representative of a constitu- ency he had to give his decisions in the light, not of his own interests, although it might be to his personal dis- advantage, but he was hound, if he was acting in the light of his God, to look after the general interest of the people he represented. (Applause.) He wanted to help them to realise the great heritage to which they had fallen beira. He wished them to realise the obligations that rested upon them in connection with that heritage. He wanted them to appreciate the great power that en- abled them to take their share in connection with the government, and administation of that great heritage. (Applause.) His thoughts were led in that direction by a sentence he met with in a hook containing Dr. Fair. burn's lectures to workingmen in which it was stated that we lived in the generation that had witnessed a transit of power. That meant that we had called out our last reserves and on the skill and endurance of the people the issue would turn. He wished to know how they would act. It was some- thing to have seen as he had done a large part of the British Empire. He thought few of them realised what it meant and what our influence in connection with it meant. Let them take for instance that large depend- ency of India with its 200 millions of inhabitants for the government of which the Houses of Parliament were the court of appeal. He also referred to the colonies and stated that in many ways we in this country had pro- fited by the experiences of these new democracies. He mentioned those things not in any Jingo spirit but simply that they might apnreciate the position they had been placed in, and the influence they would exert for good if they would look at these questions aright. They were entrusted with a share of the government of this country. (Hear, hear.) Thar had received their power that they might exercise their influence to the advantage of their fellow men. They could find a duty for them- selves. If they could only get this idea implanted in the minds of the people, England wouldibe a brighter and happier place than it was to-day. (Applause.) If they</p>
      </div>
