      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>'HIE EXAMINER, SATURDAY OCTOBER 20 1894.</head>
        <p>'HIE EXAMINER, SATURDAY OCTOBER 20 1894.</p>
        <p>followed the golden rule " Do unto others as you would they should do unto you," they would hasten the day of happiness and prosperity for the country and empire they all loved so well. (Loud applause.) The Rev. W. J. WOODS in speaking on the question, Why are the people 'outside the churches? said Mr. Hugh Price Hughes had truly said that there were three millions of people in London who never went inside church or chapel and there would not be accommoda- tion in church or chapel if they desired to go in. The peNple were very largely outside the churches. That could not be the will of God and was not the will of Christ. One cause he thought was the class feeling in- side the churches. (Hear, hear.) There was no doubt that the church which was called the National church was a church of eminent respectability. there was no doubt at all that it had worked harmoniously with the squires in the parishes and with rich men in the towns. There was no doubt that its ministers were educated and university men, but it • was a church of the classes rather than of the masses. He said that in perfect fairness because he wanted to look at the whole problem. When they came to look at the chapels they .found the very same fault. Why had the people been out of the chapels ? They had got sound doctrine. They had eloquent preaching in their chapels. They had men who took a prominent part in local politics, and yet the people who would vote for these very men in munici- pal affairs refused to concert with them in the house of prayer. The reason he again said was the class feeling. ' (Hear, hear.) The church of the Lord Jesus Christ had been too much human and too little divine in its regard of the golden ring upon the finger. (Applause.) The people had not been permitted to sit in the best seats, but had been allowed a place somewhere in the gallery on hard benches. The people's manhood had been insulted in the very place where manhood should be declared equal, and they had therefore held aloof. There was another danger which deserved comment. It was that quality in the life of the churches which was described by George Eliot as their worldliness. Religion had come to be regarded as busi- ness, not of this life, but a business which concerned tself with the life beyond. This he contended was a allacy. He also stated that they would never reconcile ihe people with the churches by means of ritualism or freeds. The church that was going to win the people tack would be one that put in the forefront, character. c man's character was his real wealth. The character bhich a church must aspire to have was the spirit of rrotherhood. He thought he was a democrat. He temembered a speech by Theodore Parker who stated hat the difference between Christianity and democracy was that democracy said "I am as good as any other man," whilst Christianity said " Every other man is as good as I am." The church that was to win the people to it was to be one that :cultivated human brotherliness. which felt that " every other man is as good as lam." That church must have in it the divine life of God. They must let the people see that their character was brotherly. (Applause.) A vote of thanks was heartily accorded the speakers on the proposition of the Rev. J. Yonge.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. Week ending 6th October, 1894. During the week, which was the 40th of the current year, the following births and deaths were regist7ed Births. Deaths. Warrington Latchford 5 3</p>
        <p>Total 46</p>
        <p>The excess of births over deaths was 22. The death- rate from all causes was equal to 22.3 per 1,000, against 18.3 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 22.6 the corresponding week of last year. The death. rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 27 per 1,000, against 2.7 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 56 the corresponding week ot 1893. The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-60 60 up. 6 2 1 9 3 21 2 -- 1 3</p>
        <p>The deaths included-1 from diarrhcsa, 1 from whoop- ine• cough, 1 from scarlet fever, 3 from phthisis, 1 from diabetes, 4 from lung disease, 4 from brain disease, 2 from kidney disease. The following cases of infections disease have been reported during the week :—Erysipelas 1 case, scarlet fever 23 cases.</p>
        <p>Total number of patients in hospital, October 6th, —59.</p>
        <p>METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 47 Fah. and therefore 4•1 deg. colder than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 60 F., was recorded on the 3rd inst., and the minimum temperature 34 F., on the 2nd inst.</p>
        <p>The Rainfall measured nil. The general direction was E., the reading of the Anenometer denoting 700</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>' MORE NEGLECT OF CHILDREN AT WARRINGTON. TWO MONTHS' IMPRISONMENT FOR THE MOTHER. At the Warrington Borough Court on Monday James and Mary Turner, residing at Spencer's-yard, were charged with wilfully neglecting their chil- dren Mary and Joseph, on October 12, and at divers times during the last six months. Inspector McAteer said that he appeared on behalf of the N S.P.C.C. tc prosecute the two defendants for wil- fully neglecting their two children, and causing them unnecessary suffering. The mother had been addicted to drink, and, she frequently left her children for hours at a time. The two children were twins, and were only five weeks old. On Tuesday evening in the previous week, the female defendant left hcrne at 4.30, leaving the two infants behind, and sh 3 never returned until the following day at twelve o'clock at noon. Inspector Spinks and himself then went to the house and found her partly drunk. He remonstrated with her and told ' her what the consequences of her conduct would be. She made several promises, and said that she would give up the drink and look after her children. Notwithstanding this however, an hour afterwards, she. went out again, leaving the children behind, and never returned until nine o'clock the following morning. All that time the children were alone,without anyone to take care of them except some of the neighbours who took compassion on them. The father was in constant employment, earning £2 per week as a foreman mason, and every Saturday, he gave his wife the whole of the money to keep house with. The woman however spent the money with her dirty associates whom she found in Warrington, in the public houses of the town. There was no doubt that she was a drunken, had woman, a woman who was out at all hours, night after night, day after day, spending the money which should be for her children. He had cautioned her time after time. He asked that, with regard to the husband, they would deal leniently with him, as he had taken all possible care he could of his children, when he was not absent at work. He pressed the case, however, against the female defend- ant, and asked either that the fine of £25 be inflicted or six months hard labour. Some three months ago the woman came from Manchester, and left two of her children on a door step in Cairo-st., telling them that if they left the step until she returned, she would beat them. He (Inspector McAteer) found them there at eleven o'clock at night, and the mother had net then re- turned, and he took them to the workhouse. The follow- ing day, the woman was taken into custody by the police, but owing to her condition, she had to be taken to the workhouse. She would have been prosecuted at that time, but the matron of the Workhouse said that she was unfit to appear. Inspector Spinks said that what Inspector McAteer had stated was quite correct. The female defendant had always - plenty of money. She did not neglect her children when she was sober. She did not appear to know where she went when she was drunk. The male defendant said that it was all the drink that caused his wife to act as she did. If she could give up the drink he would have a good home. The magistrates decided to discharge the male defen- dant, but sent his wife to gaol for two months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>'MINOS HEARD AND SEEN</head>
        <p>'MINOS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>I am glad to notice that the work of completing the gates and arranging the plot of ground in front of the Town Hall has been recommenced. The gates are also to be painted forthwith, and soon we shall see them and the angels in full glory. By the way should not the opening of the gates be made the occasion of a grand ceremony? And a banquet, of course.</p>
        <p>--- Now that Alderman Greenall has announced his intention not to offer himself for the position of ' Mayor of the borough for the ensuing twelve months, I think that it is only fair that the honour should be offered to a Liberal member of the Coun- cil. There has not be a single Liberal Mayor since Alderman Crosfield in 1881-t2 and that.year was the' only break in a succession of Tory mayors for al- most twenty years.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyans of Latchford had a red-letter day on Thursday last when they met for the first. time in a chapel of their own. There was naturally much rejoicing at the event, and not without cause, for Nonconformist chapels are not too common in that district. The chapel itself is a fine piece of architecture, and is an ornament to the village. The attraction was, of course, the Pre- sident of the Conference. The Rev. Wal- ford Green holds a similar position in the Wes- leyan Methodist body, to that which the Arch- bishop of Canterbury holds in the Church of Eng- and with the exception that he only holds the office for one year,and he is elected. His visit there- fore was an event in the history of the Warrington , Circuit. The rev, gentleman was very genial and he quickly made himself popular. No doubt the Latchford people will avail themselves of the carli- lest opportunity of reminding him of his promise to come again.</p>
        <p>The magistrates at the Borough Court took a drag- tic step on Monday morning last when they fined a number of hawkers 10s. and costs for obstructing the thoroughfares near the market. These hawkers it appears not only block up the highways leading to the market with impunity, but place the stall- holders (who of course are obliged to pay the market tolls) at a disadvantage by the unfair com- petition. The streets of Warrington are far too narrow to allow any obstruction whatever in the shape of standing carts and handcarts,and hawkers must know that although they have perfect liberty to sell their goods in the streets they must do so without becoming a nuisance to the public. The magistrates no doubt took into consideration, in imposing the fine, the insolence of the hawkers to the police and officials when ordered to "move on."</p>
        <p>New Zealand is, from all accounts, an eligible field for young men abut to marry. They fare forth thither, they prosper, and they send over to England for the girls of their hearts to come and be married. This is nice for all parties including New Zealand. These young people are not of the lower classes. They are many of them people of position, of education, and of refinement, but are dependent on their own strength and intellect for making a way in the world. Young ladies, therefore, who are sufficiently old-fashioned to care about matri- mony should send their lovers to New Zealand. Anent the craze for inoculation as the universal panacea, an American paper says :—A Swiss physician is treating tuberculosis with injections of the serum of asses, and the treatment will, at least, be conceded the merit that the supply of serum cannot possibly run short.</p>
        <p>BABIES ON FIRE, BABIES BURNING UP, with itcising and burning eczemas and other torturing, disfiguring skin and scalp diseases. None but mothers realize how these little ones suffer when their tender skins are literally en fire. To know that a single application of the CUTICURA REMEDIES will, in the great majority of cases, afford instant relief, permit rest and sleep, and point to a permanent and economical (because most speedy) cure, and not to use them without a moment's delay is to fail in your duty. Think of the years of suffering entailed by such neglect, years not only of phyaical, hut of mental, suffering by reason of personal disfigurement, added to bodily torture. Cures made in infancy and ghildho,A aro speedy, eco.aominal, and per. moment.</p>
        <p>TowLE'SC HLORODYNE Ask for and take Bo other. T 0 WU'S C HLORODYNE , TOWLE'SCHLORODYNE RELIEVES rp 0 WLE'SCHLORODYNE COUGHS, TOWLE'SC HLORODYNE INFLUENZA, BRONCHITIS, TOWLE'SCHLORODYNE kdONSUMPTIO/P, T OWLET HLORODYNE ASTHMA, T OWLET HLORODYNE DiAnnuas, SPASMS, &amp;a. T 0 WLESC HLORODYNE 131 d. per Bottle. A. P. TOWLE, MANCHESTER.</p>
