      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>WARRINGTON WATCH COMMITTEE AND GAMBLING.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WATCH COMMITTEE AND GAMBLING.</p>
        <p>Councillor H. Roberts, J.R. presided at the weekly meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society on Sunday afternoon. He said they beard an address in that church on Thursday. evening from a minister of the denomination to which he belonged, which was a great inspiration to him. The text was the old puritan proverb that it is the duty of every Christian man to see that his country is properly governed. He wished to alter it and say tbat it is the duty of every Christian man to see that his.town is properly governed. There had been subjects, one of which had occupied the local public mind for some time past, and that was the action of their governors in the Town Council. He did not want them to run away with the idea that it was not their Town Council, because it was of their crea tion. They had a body of men who were interested in a trade which was a monopoly. It was a means of creating an immense amount of wealth and with that wealth it created an enormous responsi- bility. These men were quite prepared to take the wealth but not the responsibility. It was the duty of their governors to see that they did take the responsi- bility as well as the wealth. This Council of ours had a ' Watch Committee. He thought the term "Watch Cool- ' mittee" was entirely a misnomer as it was more of a ' publicans' protection committee. Instead of looking to the interests of the public of Warrington it seemed to have been a committee purely and simply for the pur- pose of protecting these few specially favoured citizens. It was a grand jury for the purpose of hearing eases in private which should be dealt with by the magistrates, and, where it could, it cot the bill. He wQuld draw no conclusions, but he would leave them to draw their own. It was a very remarkable thing when it was possible for that grand jury—the Watch Committee to hear cases against publicans and then to cut the bill. On the last occasion he (the Chairman) had the honour to preside at that P.S.A. meeting he stated that he would say something on gambling. It had been said that it was no use trying to put down the gambling spirit—that they would never do it. He had noticed this, that the men who would tell you that, were never the men who tried to put it down. They were men who never lifted their halals or voices to prot.t, but put every discouragement in the way of those who did do so. There was a time when men did without g,ambl- ling. He did not think there was any betting in the garden of Eden. If there was a time when men did not gamble he hoped there would be a time in the world again when they would not gamble. The material pros- perity of the great bulk of the people of this country had been marvellonsly improved during the last 25 years and every great measure which was being considered had only one object, and that was the welfare of the people. If they coold not prevent gambling they could prevent, houses being used for gambling and gaming purposes. That such houses did exist *as koown to the Watch Committee and the police, but he did not blame the police, as he did not think the police of Warrington were allowed to do' what they would like to do. There was a hoose within 40 yards of one of the four main thoroughfares of War- rington which was used solely for the purpose of betting and f.larnbling. It was a small house and not a public- (loose. There was no business carried on in this house except betting. September 12, was, he understood, St. Leer day, and from 11 15 to 11.55 a.m., twenty-one people visited the house, and from 12 30 to 230 it was visited by 77 people. It was a curious thing that 93 people should he going in and out of a house in the course of a single morning. What was it for ? It was St. Leger day, and the visitors were known to he racing men and racing women, and this honse was used for that purpose and in that way. The same house was visited on October 10.—Cesarwitch Day—from 12 30 to 2.0 o'clock by 101 men, 8 women, 10 youths and 2 girls, total 121 in one hour and 40 minutes. He asked was it fair, was it right, was it just, that these facts should be known to the proper authorities I Rnd yet that the thing should go on without the slightest attempt being made to stop it. He might tell them that he did not care if he was alone on this subject ; as long as he had strength Ile would protest against the law of the country being put en one side while such a state of things was allowed to exist. The bulk of these betting men could no more afford to lose their few shillings than he could afford to lose £l,OOO. The women and children were being robbed of their necessities whilst a few people were pro- fiting on the loss of hundreds of others. In their common self interests it was time this was put a stop to. He asked them to recognise the facts and do what they could to make this town a little better than•they found it. (Applause.) The Rev. R. SMITH, of Lancaster, afterwards delivered an earnest address.</p>
        <p>Miss COUBROU(111 was the soloist and she sang exceed- ingly well "The Chorister." The meeting was also taken part in by Mr. Thos. Wallington (secretiry), and Mr. A. Birch.</p>
        <p>Lord Dunraven has not yet formally sent another challenge for the America Cup, but there appears to be no reason to doubt that he has decided to do so, and that the forthcoming year will see another English yacht competing for the trophy in American waters. It was a short time since announced that it was probable that Lord Dun- raven's new boat would be much smaller than the Valkyrie—only about seventy feet on the water-line —but it is now stated that the new representative yacht will be of about the same size as his lordship's last ill-fated vessel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>ASTHMA PREVALENT.</head>
        <p>ASTHMA PREVALENT.</p>
        <p>OVER 500 CASES IN WARRINGTON. A NOTED PHYSICIAN'S VIEWS ON THE SUBJECT.</p>
        <p>Asthma has hitherto baffled the skill of our best phy- sicians. Within a comparatively recent period Dr. R. Schiffmann, the distinguished Specialist, who has de- voted many years of study to the treatment of Asthma, Bronchitis, Hay Fever, and Croup, has prepared a remedy for these affections which is meeting with great success wherever introduced. With this remedy there is no waiting for results. It is not a nauseating com- pound to be swallowed, but is used by inhalation, thus reaching the seat of disease direct. Its action is immedi- ate and certain. Acting directly upon the mucous membrane of the air passages, it relieves the spasm and constriction instantaneously, facilitates free and easy expectoration, and insures comfortable rest to those otherwise unable to sleep except in a chair. All Chemists have this remedy in stock, but in order that its efficacy may be tested by every sufferer who has tried all other means of relief in vain, one free trial package will be given, as an advertisement, to everyone who applies within the week following this announcement at J. R. Young's Drug Store Sankey-street Warrington thus con- vincing the most sceptical. Persons living at a distance who wish to test the merits of this remedy should address R. Schiffmann, M.D., No. 34 M. J. Gray's Inn Raod, London, W.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WARRINGTON LITERARY AND PHILOSOPHICAL SOCIETY. --- LECTURE ON LOCAL ANTIQUITIES.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON LITERARY AND PHILO- SOPHICAL SOCIETY. --- LECTURE ON LOCAL ANTIQUITIES.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the above society for the session 1894-5 was held on Monday evening, in the Museum Lecture-room. There was a moderate atten- dance, over which the President (the Rev. L. J. T. Darwall) presided. The HON. SECRETARY (Mr. J. Rymer Young) said that in the construction of the programme, a copy of which all the members had received, they had endeavoured to make it as general and as all round as possible. The committee were aware that the society consisted Of people who had literary tastes and of others who had scientific tastes, and those who did not seem to have much taste either for one or for the other. These members usually absented themselves from the meetings of the society. (Laughter.) Mr. Young concluded by a reference to the papers to be given during the session. The PRESIDENT said that night Mr. May would give them a paper on stone implements. That was a good foundation, and they trusted the more airy themes of electricity, holidays, and birds would form a not un- worthy superstructure. (Applause.) Mr. May had lately come to Warrington, he believed, but he seemed to have gravitated by a natural law to the society. Mr. THOMAS MAY, F.E.1.5., then proceeded to read a paper on "Ancient Stone Implements in my possession." A large number of specimens, to which frequent refer- ence was made during the reading of the paper, were exhibited on the table at which he stood and on the one immediately below. He observed that the exhibition of the relics of the Manchester Ship Canal in the neigh- bourhood of Warrington was an after-thought, and had been rendered possib`e by the kindness of the local staff. The resident engineer, Mr. William Burch, had been good enough to lend for the purpose of that evening's paper the Bob's Bridge skull and the principal Roman relics. An occasion like this was not suitable for criticizing the proceedings of the Museum Committee, but he mi gist say that in view of their treatment of the old canoe, they were not likely to be troubled with valuable antiquities until they had made better provision for housing and showing them. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) The cause of the difficulty was, of course,the want of funds. The principal Roman remains before the audience, namely, the iron spade, the fluted vase, the segment of a handmill or quern,and fragments of an amphora, salinum and ampulla, were from the Ship Canal cutting near old Twenty Steps Brid.ge,where the site or Roman station called Veratinum had been crossed. The iron spade was found in May, 1893, on the surface of the red sandstone rock more than 30 feet below the natural level of the vicinity. Though not of very precious material or beautiful design, it was venerable and interesting on account of its wnquestion- able antiquity. The fluted vase, which is of fine paste and very elegant outline, was discovered at less depth in the sand near the same spot. The traces of gold with ' which it was gilded could still be seen when it was care- fully examined, though they had been partly removed by the cleaning which it underwent. The segment of a quern was interesting because it was composed of the porous volcanic tufa obtained at Andernach on the Rhine, and because it was pointed or dressed on the scientific principle still followed by the miller of the present day. The Bob's Bridge skull was lent for exhibi- tion by Mr. William Iturch. resident engineer of the Warrington division. The skull was found in red sand at a depth (verified by actual survey) of between 18 and i 20ft. below the surface, at Bob's Bridge, near Randal's Creek, half way between Warrington and Runcorn. The Bob's Bridge skull might possibly belong to the period of the later Danish invasion of this country, in the 9th century of our era, but the circumstances seemed to show that it ought rather to be attributed to the period which was supposed to centre in about 500 8.C., when bronze was the material chiefly used for cutting imple- ments. It was that of a young man of great muslular strength, and Mr. May gave the dimensions. The audi- ence would notice on the skull that the teeth were much worn by grinding vegetable food, which was probably gritty owing to the rough manner in which it was pounded and cooked. They were worn more on the left side owing to the tenderness caused by caries in several of the mnlara on the right. The fattened occiput and cubical figure of the skull were the peculiar cranial char- acteristics of the Finnish or Danish of the present day, and still more markedly of some of the bronze aged skulls teaovered from the round barrows of this country and Scandinavia. The Arpley cutting skull was dredged from the sandy sill or diluvian in the cutting for the Mersey river diversion at Arpley, at some 18ft. from the natural surface, after removal of 12ft. by dry cutting and eft. be the dredger. Its principal dimensions were given which' correspond closely with those recorded by Profes- sor Daniel Wilson with reference to skulls of undoubted Celtic type in the Edinburgh Antiquarium Museum. He next showed a Roman sestertius of the first brass, which was a kind of coin current in Veratinum, now Wildersponl, in the second centers', and which by a curious coincidence he purchased there a few days since from a person who could tell nothing of its history. There was on the obverse a laureated bust of Hadrian, who was emperor from A.D. 117-138, and on the reverse an allegorical draped female figure holding in her left hand the 'costa pure, and with the right hand extended, seated on a shield or helmet—a figere ere itly resembling the Britannia in our own bronze coinage. Veratinum was the station mentioned in the Antonine Itinera next to Deva, or Chester, on the road from there to Manches- ter. Fladrian may have often plssed through Wilders- pool across the Mersey ford, half way between wilderspool and the Walton Lock, and so through Warrington. The larger of the two dug-out or singde log canoes now in the Warrington Museum—' or rather in the back yard of that Museum—was dis- covered in sandy silt, close to the west end of the new lock at Walton, which connected the Ship Canal with the river Mersey. The canoe lay upside down, over 18 feet below the original surface, two feet below the ordnance datum and only three feet above the till—a hard sub-glacial clay containing boulders in which the foundations of the lock walls are sunk. It was more than 20 yards from the north bank of the river, and above it were three feet of clean sand, six feet of sticky diluvial clay, and nine to twelve feet of soft, sandy silt. In the sandy stratum, a little higher and a few yards westward, a perfect skull of the great wild ox, or urus, was met with. This trophy had been sent to Mr. Jackson, and being suitably mounted. adorned his residence in London. The probable age of this canoe was an interesting problem. The piles sur- reranding it might or might not have any relation to tfbe canoe, as their pointed ends reached to about the same level. Its regular design, steeng,thening ribs, and ends, smooth pegs and peg holes, and the marks upon it when first recovered, indicate the use of metal tools in its construction. It was quite possible that the Romans who established the neighbouring station of Veratinum were responsible for the overthrow and des- truction of the habitations of the owners of these canoes. The other canoe, which west in the Museum, but not yet dry enough to be unpacked, was dredged from a depth of 18 feet in the cutting of the Aroley diversion and the skull from the same locality might be that of one of the unfortunate victims of that over- throw. Nor WWI this surmise altogether fanciful. The Lading of two canoes was a plain fact indicating the former existence of a pile-dwelling in this very suitable locality. Mr. May alluded to the lake dwellings of the stone age; and, in conclusion, observed that the ladies Present might congratulate themselves that their lot had been cast in the present and not in the "good old times." In his opinion, the occupation of a separate self-contained dwelling by each separate family was the foundation of our freedem,prosperity and mnrality.just as our churches and chapels were the foundations of our social worth. (Applause.) Captain EDELSTEN proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. May for his able and valuable paper, and Mr. ROBERT DAVIES seconded the motion, which was heartily agreed to.</p>
      </div>
