      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <p>THE LONGFORD DESTRUCTORS.</p>
        <p>PAPER BY COUNCILLOR BEAMAN.</p>
        <p>In the Lecture Hall of the Free Library, William Brown-st.,Liverpool,on Saturday evening,Councillor S. J. Beaman, of Warrington, a member of the Health Committee of the Corporation, read a paper before the Liverpool Sanitary Inspectors' Associa- tion, on "Town Refuse and Refuse Destructors." Dr. F. Vacher, medical officer of health for the Cheshire County Council, presided. Councillor BEAMAN contended that the disposal of town refuse was a very difficult and complex question, and the difficulty necessarily increased in proportion to the grov,th of the population. Mr. Beaman then al- luded to Mr. Alfred Fryer's patented " destructor." There were no such things as destructors, scientifically ' speaking, for it had been demonstrated over and over again, and the truth of the principle firmly established, that matter was indestructible, that it was as easy to create a worid as to destroy an atom. Fryer's furnace, though very far from perfect, and designed without having any regard for sanitary or scientific principles, was in advance and was an irnproveinetst on the primi- tive furnaces that had preceded it. Lancashire, always to the front in sanitary matters, was amongst the first to adopt the system. Mr. Beaman alluded at consider- able length and in an able manner to the progress made step by step in sanitary sckntific matters, to several inventions which were brought out, after which he proceeded to review the furnace which was now engag- ing the most profound interest and attention in those quarters where the generation of steam from the burning of town refuse for electric lighting, sewage works, and any ulterior purposes was of such vital importance. That furnace, it was scarcely necessary to remind them, was worked with forced draught propelled into the closed ashpit beneath the furnace bars by means of a fan,and in addition to the fan blast there was also a fine ,let of steam introduced, which not only tended to pro- long the lifetime of the fire bars by keeping them cool, and preventing the adhesion of the clinker upon them, but also served to increase the volume of gases for steam generating purposes. The sharper the draught the less was the quantity of air required for dilution,the higher the temperature of the products of combustion, and the less was their velocity. The economy was due to the high temperature of the furnace, which ensured such rapid and efficient diffusion and combination of the gases that they could not escape from the furnace unconsumed. The formation of carbonic oxide and the discharge of smoke from the chimney shaft was also prevented. They would concur with him that forced draught had many advantages to which natural draught ; could lay no claim. It would be seen from a diagram that the furnace had been designed upon lines very dis- similar from those of any other in operation, not only in the position of the doors for drawing on the charge to refuse to be burnt, and for extracting the clinker, but there was introduced quite a new feature in the form of a fire bridge,a very important factor in all reverberatory furnaces. That important factor had been ignored, and in every other type of destructor furnace was entirely wanting. Another important feature was the combus- tion chamber, wnich was formed between the fire bridge ' proper and the perforated screen or additional bridge situated nearest to the flues. Into this chamber hot air was introduced at the back of the fire bridge, through small apertures communicating with the cavity in the said bridge, in which the air was heated. The air thus heated, on coming into contact with the heated gases distilled from the refuse, entered into the most perfect combustion, producing a large volume of flame and an intense heat, which was transmitted direct to the heat- ing surface of boiler. Another feature peculiar to the Beaman and Deas furnace was that the reverberatory arch extended along the whole length of the structure, including the drying hearth, furnace bars, fire bridge, and combustion chamber. Another peculiarity of this furnace was that the distilled gases were retarded and held in check by the conflicting currents which were set up inside the furnace by the forced draught through the intervention of the fire bridge sufficiently long enough to destroy all fumes and organic - yapours. Opinions might differ as to the temperatiil.e at which these gases were dissociated from the refuse, and as to the necessary heat for their complete destruction and cremation. Mr. Boulnois,who paid a visit to Warrington with Mr. Broadie on the 20th December, 1893, inspected the Beaman and Deas' furnace and saw it in full operation. He stated that such a heat as he saw generated was quite unnecessary, and that the furnace would soon burn itself away. As a matter of fact the furnace was just as good to day and as fit for work as on the date and occasion of the President's vi3it. The temperature which these gentlemen termed "intense," and which was to bring about such disaster to the whole structure, was totally inadequate to fuse a fire brick of average quality. The low calorific value of town's refuse compared with that of good bituminous coal precluded the possibilitv of producing the same amount of heat with one as with the other class of fuel. The chief alleged objection that could be urged against this destructor furnace was the very point that stamped its superiority over others, namely, the high temperature obtained, and which was so necessary and indispensable for the economic burning of town refuse for the genera- tion of steam. He did not object or complain of the zeal with which these objections were put forth ,but he some- times felt compelled to question the motive. Sra far as the value of the furnace was concerned, he confessed i that he felt no fear as to what might be the result I of such criticisms as those indulged in by parties interested in other types of furnaces. Instead of depre- cating the fullest inquiry, and the collection of all the evidence and information possible respecting the matters in question, he urged and invited the fullest investiga- tion into and careful perusal of the statements contained ' in the paper which had been read that evening.. The lecture, which was illustrated by numerous dia- grams and sections of the destructor, proved highly in- teresting, and at its conclusion a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Beaman, on the motion of Mr. Brough- ton (Garston), seconded by Mr. Short (Wigan). The proceedings closed with a vote of thanks to the Chair- man on the vote of Mr. Coates, seconded by Mr. Bea- m an.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>GOING DOWN HILL.</head>
        <p>GOING DOWN HILL.</p>
        <p>An nc3ount is going the rounds of the press of an aged men (74 yea,ts), who for more than twenty years was sainted with the painfully characteristic symptoms, of inflammation of the bladder—)ravel,sca,lding, retention, and acute pain—a weakness particularly prevalent among thoe in advanced years. His experience has ben so remarkable that we feel justified in giving it prominence. He says : "I am 74 years of age. My life has been active. lam well known in this town, and what I.say will not be cinuhted.by anyone who knows me. In addition to the symptoms above described, pain deueloped in my loins and extended up the back to the shoulder, finally affecting the right arm, making it painful to even raise it. These pains had gradually increased during all these tweoty years, although I tried many remedies in hopes of obtaining relief. A few months ago I saw an adver- tisement of Warner's Safe Cure and read the testimonials of the cures it had wrought in similar cases, and deter- mined to give it a trial. My liith was not great, as you may infer, after so many failures : and after taking six bottles it completely cured me ; the pains are gone, I fed as sound and well as a boy and as ever I did in my life. It is now three months since I took the last bottle, and there are no signs, whatever, of a return of this complaint." There is no doubt but that this is a true recital of the case of Mr. R. Tanner of Marine Villa, Newuham-on-Severn, Gloucestershire, and it should be an encouragement to similar sufferers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>MUSIC HATT" CHARMS TO SOOTHE THE</head>
        <p>MUSIC HATT" CHARMS TO SOOTHE THE</p>
        <p>SAVAGE B(II)EA.ST.</p>
        <p>Plagiarised. from Mr. W. Shakespeare aad Bill Adams, Esq., in collaboration.) On a downy couch they sat together, The lamplight burning low and dim ; The old folks had retired to slumber— 'Twas eleven-forty-five P.M. Their tales of love and sweet canoodling, Ten seconds they'd left off or so ; Their hearts felt poised 'tween love and sadness, Because—twas time that he should go. He fidgeted, in mild distraction, As if he'd something in his mind He lacked the enterprise to utter. Like a lump of lead tossed in the wind! At last he mustered up some courage, And said," my Sweetest Gwendoline, I'm to request a little favour Which you I trust will not decline." Sbe blushed a sea-green jet carnation, That almost rivalled Sankey Brook, Or Mersey's pale pellucid waters, Where ardent anglers" fish for fluke "-- And said, in love-struck agitation, My dearest Adam, well know you There's nought within my power, in reason, For you I would not gladly do." " Thanks,thanks, my precious rose-eyed darling— My trouble is like this, you see : I know the big black dog so savage, Out on the lawn's awaiting me. Would you please sit at the piano, And once more try to sing and play That latest song of yours 4 Ta-ra-ra '?— I want to skeer the brute away " ! ADAM NEWSENSE. Warrington, October, 1894.</p>
