      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, OCTOBER 20, 1894.</p>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN has, for Once in Another Pro- gramme. a way, got rid of his familiar bogey the Irish Question. It is true that in his speech to his constituents at Birminghatniat the end of last week he could not entirely refrain from paying Irishmen, and especially the Irish members, some of those left:handed compliments of which he has produced a copious supply during the last few years. The special fault of the Irish people which Mr. CHAMBERLAIN now complains of is that they are not in the habit of speaking well of each other. This, he says, was also the opinion of the great Dr. JOHNSON, but as a matter of fact Dr. JOHNSON did not blame the Irish for not speak- ing well of each other but rather praised them for it. It was, he considered, better than the habit of the Scotch, whom he roundly accused of a disposi- tion "to tell lies in favour of each other." But then Dr. JOHNSON disliked the Scotch just as Mr. CHAMBERLAIN seems, to dislike the Irish, and the wholesale blamEi is as little deserved in the one case as in the other. However apart from this little preliminary sally, Mr. CHAMBERLAIN left the Irish question severely alone, and his audience was no doubt grateful for this little consideration. After the tirades of last eight years he could not possibly have had any ob- servations of an original character to make on that subject, and certainly none that would add to the enlightenment of the country. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S min theme was that favourite one of his, programme-making. He stands first amongst the politicians of the day in the construction of un- authorised programmes. This is Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN'S hobby and he seems to be not a little jealous of others who attempt the same perform- ance. Of the Newcastle programme, for instance he can claim no part of the authorship,. and he has, therefore, nothing but abuse for it. There are, it appears, only two ways of building up a programme, one of which is right and the other wrong. The Newcastle programme has, of course, in the opinion of. this infallible politiean, been con- structed on the wrong plan. Its compilers have "collected from every corner every possible pro- ject,good, bad or indifferent which has succeeded in securing any Measure of support and any number of votes," and have combined them together in a "heterogeneous mass." Now,. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN's. own way of making a programme is different, and it is, of course, the right way. He has surveyed the whole field of politics and he has selected those points which are the most ripe for practical legis- lation and which command the largest amount of general support. Yet, when. we come to look at the items of this programme they have a wonder- ful resemblance to those in the programme with which the Liberal party fought the last general election, and which they have since endeavoured to pass through Parliament. There is, for example, temperance reform, improved dwellings for the people, old age pensions, industrial arbitration courts, reduction of the hours of labour, and other kindred questions. Where is the novelty of pro- posals. of this description to Liberals? In fact when Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S vaunted programme is analysed all that is original in it is seen to be very little indeed. But taking Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S scheme as it stands, how does he propose to carry it out apart from the Liberal party? Has he converted or has he any expectation of converting the Tory party into full-pledged reformers ? We should have thought that even Mr. CHAMBERLAIN blinded as he i by his bitter prejudices, would have been convinced by this time that the Tories have no intention to suddenly change their whole political complexion. In spite of all that he says the Liberal party is still the great instrument for the effecting of reforms in this country.</p>
        <p>ONE of the assets of the notorious A Mnt e r Liberator companies is an enormous Hoote . but incomplete building on the Embaßkment in London. This was one of the enterprises of" Hobbs and C 0.," and was intended to be a monster hotel. When JABEZ BALFOUR and his colleague were relieved cf the responsibility of conducting the business they had so dishonestly mismanaged this huge pile of masonry fell into the hands of the Official Receiver. The problem which this official had then ,to solve was, what to do with it. An immense sum of money had already been spent, yet as the building stood, unfinished and unfurnished, it was comparatively valueless! A bold course was adopted. The Official Receiver decided to compleLe the hocl, and, if need be, to</p>
        <p>carry on a business as hotel keeper in the interests of the defrauded investors. Already the announcement is made that the place will be opened early in 1895. It will certainly be one of the largest hotels in the world It will cover two and a quarter acres of ground and will contain 1,200 rooms, including 700 bed- rooms. There are from the cellar-to the roof no fewer than twelve floors, but this does not mean that visitors who may be located in an upper storey will have to travel an interminable series of stairs. The building is to be fitted on American principles, and in its equipment is a lift which will travel from top to bottom in ten seconds.</p>
        <p>, THE Birkenhead election is not The Birkenhead a Liberal victory in the sense Election.</p>
        <p>that the Liberal candidate has been returned, but on the other hand it is not a defeat that Liberals need lam ent over. The Tories boast that Birkenhead is a " virgin " constituency, in other words, that it has never yet returned a Liberal to Parliament. Another such assault as that which Mr. LEVER has made upon it during the last few weeks and the long record of Tory successes will probably be broken. In 1885 a Liberal candidate was left in a minority of 1,173 votes; on Wednesday last that minority was reduced to 106. It may be said that in Mr. LEVEE the Liberals had a particularly strong candidate, and this must be conceded. But it is worth remembering that Mr. LEVER was also the candid- ate two years ago and that the majority against him was then over six hundred. The figures, therefore, point to a gradual and by no means slow increase of the number of Liberal voters in the "City of the Future." From a general point of view the election is satisfactory. As far as it goes it indicates that Toryism is not progressing but is receding in the country. It indicates also that an outspoken candidate with an advanced social programme can make a formidable bid for success even under the most unfavourable circum- stances.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER is now supplied with A Large Water Scheme, water from a Cumberland lake. The distance from Thirlmere, the source of the water supply, to the great Lanca- shire town where the water is to be consumed, is almost exactly ninety-six miles. In order to get the water through the taps at Manchester the engineers have had to provide fourteen and a hale miles of tunnelling, thirty-six miles of what is known as "cut and cover," and forty-five miles of piping. Twenty-three bridges have been made, including one over the Ribble of 231 feet and another over the Lune of 350 feet. An immediate supply of ten million gallons a day has been procured, but this quantity can be raised,. if need be, to fifty million gallons per day. The cost up to Saturday last had been two and a half million pounds,which will be raised to four and a half millions if the whole fifty million gallons should be required in the , future. It is claimed that Manchester now stands first amongst modern cities in regard to. the length of its water conduits. The second place is claimed by Marseilles in France which conveys its water for a distance of sixty miles ; while New York has an aqueduct on stoue arches forty miles long.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>SWING BOAT FATALITY AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>SWING BOAT FATALITY AT WARRING- TON.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, a young man named William Jones,. 22 years of age, who was employed as a labourer at the Mersey Rubber Works, died from the effects of a fall on Saturday evening from a swing boat in Howard-st.—Mr. F. A. Sones, deputy coroner, held an inquest upon the body of the deceased at the- Parade-room of the police station, on Wednesday afternoon.—Hannah Jones, the mother of the deceased,. said that her son did not make any statement with regard. to the accident to her.—William Henry Egerton said that he was with the deceased on Saturday night a little after eleven o'clock at the fair in Howard-street. They both. had a swing, and deceased was sober. They had been swinging for about five minutes, and deceased was standing up, when he appeared to become "giddy," and fell to the ground on to his back. Witness got out to his assistance and he was- unconscious until he was taken home. He was suffering trona a wound on the back of the neck. They had been warned by the owner. of the swings not to stand up.—Frank Wilson raid that he was in charge. of the swing boats on Saturday night. He told deceased. and last witness not to stand up when they entered the boats, and they promised they would not. He had to stop them three times, however, in consequence of their standing up. After some persua- sion he allowed them to commence swinging a fourth time, and deceased, got up again, this time falling out. He would have stopped them altogether but he was afraid of being ill-used, as he had been previously when he stopped some other persons.—The coroner said that it was evident that the k-eper of the swings was in no war to blame for the accident,but that it was deceased's own. fault, swing to hire standing in the swing boat.—The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental Death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SHIP CANAL</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL</p>
        <p>TRADE ON THE CANAL. A HOPEFUL REPORT.</p>
        <p>A report on the Manchester Snip Canal has been corn-. municated to the Board of Trade. The actual working of the canal,. says this report„has been very satisfactory. The imports include manufactured iron,iron ore,timber,, cotton, dyewoods, wood pulp, wool, sugar, rice, fruits, oils, butter, cheese, lard„ grain, hides, margarine, con- densed milk, wines, spirits, and minerals. Coal has. been exported, also cotton goods, cotton yarns, machin- ery, chemicals, kc., and the coasting trade is developing steadily. So far the timber trade has taken the lead. There is accommodation at Trafferd Wharf for the storage of 50,000 standards of timber, and an extensive pond is provided at Wilderspool. near Warrington in the old river bed for floating timber. A good commencement has been made with the green fruit and vegetable trade,. large quantities of oranges, lemons, grapes, tomatoes, melons, and unions having been received from the Medi- terranean ports, Alexandria, Valencia, Rotterdam, and Bordeaux. The Mediterranean trade has opened out so well that additional steamers are about to be put on. Forty-one of the principal exporters from Manchester to China and Japan have sign el and forwarded to the China Steamship owners' Confe-ence a memorandum stating that they propose to ship their consignments direct from Manchester d( cks, and asking to be supplied with regular sti amers. In reply, the canal company have quoted inclusive rates, offering a release from ship dues to the end of 1895. Special attention is also being paid to the Eastern trade. A COM- . mittee. of merchants connected with the Australian trade has been formed, and as soon as the cold storage is ready a line of steamers will run between Manchester and the Australian ports. The important question of cotton shipments is getting nearer to a solution with the arrangement of the through railway rates, The present saving to the cotton trade brought about by the canal is at least £150,000 a year. The foreign animals depot to be erected on the banks of the canal, close to the docks, by the Manchester Corporation, is to be commenced at once. Complete provision is to be made ultimately for 3.000 head of cattle, but the work has been divided 50 that accommodation for 1,000 may be ready by next summer. The Manchester Chamber of Commerce has appointed a shipping committee which ie actively deal- ing with all quessions suggested by the members, and the shareholders have formed an association and are also organising a conference for the investigation of plans and proposals for the development of traffic. The Old- ham Chamber of; Commerce has also appointed a ship- ping committee. The Manchester docks are in direct railway communication with the whole railway system of the country, end through rates have been arranged. for all classes of tr..ffic to all places.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>WARRINGTON ART SNIOLARSHIPS.</head>
