      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>-- - ST. EDWARD'S LEAGUE OF THE CROSS, RUNCORN. On Sunday evening the members associated with this organization met in the rooms, Irwell-lane, to listen to an address frsm the Rev. Father Cross on the drink question. The rev, gentleman expressed a hope that some scheme would soon be formulated by Government to regulate the sale of drink, and thus bring their prss- pects of prosperity and happiness closer to them.—The meeting terminated with a vote of thanks to the Rev. Father Cross. LECTURE AT RUNCORN ON BURNS AND HIS POEMS. On Tuesday evening a meeting of the members form- ing the Literary and Social Class connected with the Bethesda Congregational Church was held in the Lecture Hall, Runcorn, when the subject of the lecture was the Scotch poet" Robert Burns." Brief essays on the merit of Burns' works were read by several members, and criticized by the Rev. John Simpson, The rev gentle- man after concluding his criticisms of the various essays entered upon an analysis of the man and of his literary productions. COLLIER'S sell the best quality goods at lowest possible prices.—Advt. ST. EDWARD'S CATHOLIC CHURCH, RUNCORN. Services were celebrated at this place of worship on Sunday in honour of the Feast of Sit. Edward, the in- gathering of the harvest, and the school anniversary. As in former years the interior of the sacred edifice was handsomely decorated by the lady members of the con- gregation. The Rev. Father Cross was the preacher both morning and evening. High Mass was celebrated in the morning and Benediction in the evening, the offi- ciating clergy being the Revs. Father Fells, S.J., Dr. Gastaldi, Father Steir, and Father Chapman (Widnes). The offertories, which realised about £9, were in aid of the day schools. ANNIVERSARY OF BETHESDA CHURCH, RUNCORN. I Anniversary services in connection with this church were conducted on Sunday by the Rev. A. Mines, 8.A., of Rock Ferry, and Chairman of the Congrega- tional Union. Large congregations assembled, and the remarks of the rev, gentleman were much appreciated. The choristers rendered special musical selections at each service, and Mr. C. C. Le Couteur presided at the organ with his usual skill. The church funds will be benefitted by the collections. ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH, RUNCORN. The choir held their annual social evening on Tuesday. An excellent progamme was afterwards gone through. The pastor, the Rev. J. P. Williams occupied the chair and delivered a short address. Mr. M. Thomas led off in style with the song "Hearts and Homes," followed by a pianoforte duet by Misses Harrop and M. Jones. A reacting entitled "The Chameleon," was then ably given by Mr. James Richardson. Miss A. Dobson sang very creditably" The Crossing Sweeper," and Miss Warburton rendered very effectively the pianoforte solo" Falling Leaves." "Rocked in the cradle of the deep" was next sung by Mr. C. Leathwood with his usual ability, then Miss Rowlands who has a sweet, well-trained voice,sang "My Fiddle and I," after which a selection by the mandolin and guitars was given in a stirring manner by Mrs. Burn arid Mrs. and Miss Warburton. Mrs.C. Leath- wood who was in good vsice sang with feeling" Alone on the raft." A recitation entitled "Trouble in the Amen Corner," was admirably given by Mr. F. Littler, and was folowed by Miss Marion Jones with a pianoforte solo tastefully rendered. Mr. Warburton next sang in his usual style "Right over wrong," after which Miss Hough rendered the song "I dreamt that I dwelt in marble halls." Messrs. T. Parry and C. Leathwood rang the" Larboard watch," and Messrs. A. Robinson and T. Charleston sang " David "and "Goliath," both of which were well received. The accompanists were Misses Warburton, Pope, and M. Jones. Fruit was distributed during the evening. The Doxology concluded the meet- ing. The catering was in the charge of Mrs. F. Jones, assisted by thc lady members. COLLIER'S is. BUTTER is tha gem in the market.—Advt. Two JUVENILE THIEVES AT RUNCORN. At the Runcorn Police Court on Thursday morning before Messrs. Philip speakman, C.C.,:F. J. Norman,and Thomas Handley, two boys named respectively John Bowdon (12), and Enoch Shaw (10), were charged with stealing an indiarubber door mat, the property of Mr. Luke Holme, landlord of the "Holyhead Harbour,"High- street, Runcorn.—The l.ench ordered that each of them should receive six strokes with the birch-rod. COLLIER'S sell everything for the smallest possible profits. Advt.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING PRESENTATION AT RUNCORN. The Sunday school teachers and elder scholars con- nected with the Bethesda Congregational Church, Run- corn' gathered together on Monday evening in the Lec- ture Hall for the purpose of discussing certain business relating to the welfare of the school. The Rev. John Simpson was in the chair, and during the proceedings light refreshments were handed round. The ordinary business being at an end, the Chairman announced that he had a most pleasing duty to perform. It was to pre- sent Mr. Harold Chadwick with a handsome writing desk, and Mrs. Harold Chadwick with a pretty work- box. There were tokens of the good feeling and hearty wishes for their future happiness, and also as slight memorials of their work in the school.—Mr. Chadwick, on behalf of his wife and himself expressed his gratitude for the kind wishes and tokens. He hoped to be long connected with the Sunday school and its work, from which he had ever derived much satistactio4n. COLLIER'S FRESH IRISH DAIRY BUTTER, 10d. per lb.is excellent. —Advt. WIDNES SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly ene;tiiiii of the Widnes School Board was held on Tuesday afterncon, at the Widnes Town Hall, under the presidency of Mr. H. Wade Deacon, J.P., C.C. The District Education Committee reported that the following children had been sent to the under-mentioned schools :—May Mulvey to the Deaf and Dumb School, Boston Spa; Alfred B. Dalton to the Deaf and Dumb School, Liverpool • and Daniel Mullen to the Blind Asylum, Brunswick-road, Liverpool. Major Waring, in moving the adoption of the committee's minutes, said the duty of seeing that the deaf, dumb, and blind were provided with education formerly devolved upon the guardians, but under recent legislation this duty now devolved upon the school boards. The minutes were adopted.-1 he financial statement for the half-year was submitted. The receipts, including a balance of £702 17s. from the last half-year, amounted to £6,670 is. 10d., and the expenditure amounted to £5,988 16s. 7d. leaving balance of £6Bl ss. 3d. The report was adopted. COLLIER BREAD is the best money can buy.—Advt. SPECIAL MEETING OF THE RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD. A special meeting of the Runcorn School Board was held in the Board-room on Tuesday, for the purpose of examining and passing the annual accounts of the Board. There were present, Mr. J. Pritchard, Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Mr. T. Handley, J.l'.' Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, Mr. E. Imibon, Mr. S. Taylor, J.P. In submitting the accounts, the Chairman stated that the year began with a balance of £466 12s. 6i. at the bank and ended with a balance of £543 14s. 4d., showing that the exnen diture had been kept und-r the estimates by about £77. Com- pared with the previous year there was a small decrease in every item of expenditure excent teachers' salaries, Which showed an increase of £127. Part of this was caused by the opening of an evening school for girls,and the remainder yr4s attributable to the growth and improvement of tie day schools. It was satisfactory to find that the increase was more than covered by increased Government grants, amounting to £135 as compared with last year. The amount drawn from the rates was £l,OOO, being £5O less than last year, and is equivalent to a rate of 4d. in the pound on the rateable value of the township. The accounts were examined and passed, and signed by the Chairman. This was all the business. COLLIER'S BLAcic BLENDED TEA. 20. per quai ter lb.—Advt A SAILOR IN TROVELI AT RUNCORN. James Clarke was charged on Tuesday morning at the Runcorn Police Court before Messrs. F. J. Norman and S. Taylor with having been on enclosed premises for an unlawful purpose on Monday evening.—Superintendent Atherton said that the def4ndant was found lying among some bedding in the goods-warehouse at the Run- corn Railway Station. He was in a very maudlin state and nothing could be aot out of him when locked up. That morning, however, he was sober and said he was a Sailor who had just been paid off. The Superintendent continued by saying that under the circumstances the defendant might have become influenced with drink to such an extent as to ramble into the warehouse with no felonious intent.—Evidence having been given, the defendant was allowed tbe benefit of the doubt and dis- charred with a caution. HARVEST FESTIVAL AND SOCIAL EVENING AT RUNCORN. In connection with the Runcorn branch of the Mersey ,Mission to Seamen, harvest thanksgiving services were tkeld on Sunday in the Seamen's Institute. The in- terior had been tastefully decorated by the ladies con- ilected with the Mission, and they are to be congratu- lar don the result of their exertions. The gentlemen 'Who respectively occupied the pulpq, morning, after- boon and evening, were the Rev. A. J. Jameson, Mr. B. 'Bfnith, and Mr. Coleman, of Bootle Institute. Large congregations were present, and in the evening the Run- corn Volunteer Band, under Bandmaster Oathwaite, Z,ceninpanied the singing. The proceeds were in aid of Ipe Seamen's Distress Fund, and the Sailor's Monument und. On Tuesday evening the first of the series of 'Winter concerts was held in the Institute, Mr. Wrn.Shaw Presiding. There was a fairly large audience present, ..arld a programme of songs, recitations, and Lancashire ;eadings was admirably sustained by Misses Douglas, 41Ien, and Polly Brockley, Messrs. Wm. Byfield, John 8h aw, Paul Green, and others, Miss Bowyer actin :g as :9companist. The concert was succeeded by a supper, ter which various games were indulged in and a large 141:Entity of fruit was sold. The amount realised from ~e sale of fruit will also be devoted to the Seamen's 'tress Fund. 480AKINO RAIN PROOF OTFRCOATS, yet poron.. 555., C f,„e"ON HOLMES, General outfitter, Market (*ate, Warring</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <p>. ,</p>
        <p>ACTION FOR RECOVERY OF TUITION</p>
        <p>FEES. INTERESTING C kSE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge _Wynne Ftoulkes, the Misses Rimmer, 23, Wilson Patten-st., sued Dr. Fox, Rylands-st., for three guineas, being damages in lieu of a term's notice of his intention to take away his daughter from their tuitien. Mr. Arthur Browne represented the plaintiffs, and Mr. Percy Davies the defendant. Mr. Browne said that the action was brought to re- cover three guineas from Dr. Fox for a term's fees in lieu of notice which had not been given by Dr. Fox before taking away his daughter Winifred. The facts were that in January, 1893, Mrs. Fox arranged with Misses Rimmer to send her daughter to school half time, as she was only five years of age, the fee to be two guineas per term. Subsequently the Misses RiMmer handed to Mrs. Fox a prospectus on whith was subscribed "six weeks notice required previous to the removal of pupils in discontinu- ance of any subject, the notice to terminate on the ex- piration of the term." They subsequently sent Mrs. Fox another prospectus which she asked for a lady friend of hers. After this,in January, 1893,arrangements were I made for the daughter to learn music at two guineas per term, and she was discontinued at school, Dr. Fox pay- ing three guineas each term until Christmas, 1893. On all the accounts sent in to Dr. Fox were similar words printed in large letters to What were inserted in the prospectus, as to notice. No objection whatever was made by Dr. or Mrs. Fox to those terms, and when the school re-opened on January 23, to the surprise of the plaintiffs, the little girl did not attend. They sent to make inquiries,but could not ascertain an thing definite until Mrs. Fox called along with her daughter on Feb. Bth, and she then explained that the little girl had had deilation of the pupil of the left eye. The doctor had thought that she had been run down from over- visiting by coming from heated room to heated room. The doctor thought she would return on Monday. Instead of the child attending the school, however, a letter was written by Mrs. Fox in which was enclosed a cheque for L 3 ss. 7d., the fees for the past term, and saying that they had decided to send their daughter to Blackpool . Miss Rimmer replied the same day that she must call their attention to the rule in the prospectus and bills,and expressing the wish that the little girl would soon be able to return to her studies. No notice was taken of this,and the little girl never came back, but was sent to seMool at Blackpool. At the end of the term the account was sent in for three guineas,and Dr. Fox replied that he objected to pay it but was quite willin to pay one guinea. The guinea was refused by the Misses Rimmer, and that action was commenced. Dr. Fox on Monday last paid a guinea into the court with proportionate costs. Miss Phcebe and Miss Catheriae Rimmer were called, and corroborated the opening statements of Mr. Browne. Mr. William limber, school attendance officer, and Inspector Norman, nuisance inspector, also stated that the accommodation of the school, according to the Edu- cation Code, was for 50 pupils, and the Misses Rimmer had only 24. Mr. Davies for the defence, said that the contract was unreasonable as to notice, and called Dr. Fox to give his reason for refusing to pay the three guineas, on the ground that he considered that one guinea was sufficient for the notice.</p>
        <p>His Honour, however, came to the conclusion that in- asmuch as Dr. Fox had received the bills with the sub- scription of the terms of notice, be must hold that thera was a contract agreed on those terms, and he could not hold that it was unreasonable inasmuch as the Misses Rimmer on commencing an extra subject had to arrange at the beginning of the term with the masters, and it was only reasonable that they ohould have notice of the discontinuance of the subject. He must, therefore,give judgment for three guineas, including the guinea paid into Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>BRITISH AND FOREIGN BIBLE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>BRITISH AND FOREIGN BIBLE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>MEETING- AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the annual meeting of the Runcorn Auxiliary ot the British and Foreign Bible Society W5B held in the Green way-road Primitive Methodist new schoolroom, Mr. Joseph Pritchard presiding. There were also on the platform the Revs. Dr. Preston, J Symonß. P.J.S. (deputation), T. Brackenbury, W. George, John Stafford and Mr. Kirkham . The Secretary, (Dr. Preston) stated that the following amounts had been collected during 1894 :—Trinity Cnurch, by Miss Preston lnd Miss Warner, £2 ss. ; Wes- leyan Church. by Mrs. A K. Norman and Miss Norman, £l2 2s. BJ. ; Bethesda Church, by Miss Hea therington, £3 9s. ; Primitive Methodist Church, by Mrs. Bramall and Mr. James Stubbs, £1 Bs.; Methodist Free Church, by Mrs. Stafford, £1; Halton Church, by Mr. Buckley, 13g.; Halton Wesleyan, by Miss Ga,mon, £1 7s. The collections had not come to hand from the Runcorn Parish Church and St. John's Presbyterian Church. Mr. Simpson, the treasurer had reported that £27 7s. 4d. had been sent, in free contributions, to the parent society for 1893. The Secretary went on to urge all the supporters of the society to stand shoulder to shoulder in their efforts to circulate the Bible. _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairman then delivered an address in the course of which he mentioned that this society, which was one of the most noble and useful institutions existing, was founded in 1804. He gave a history of the societies which existed from 1611 until the founding of the present one. The Rev. J. Symons gave statistics relating to the wide circulation of the Bible and appealed strongly for help. The Rev. W. George proposed and the Rev.J. Stafford seconded,a vote of thanks to the Rev. J. Symons, for his address. The proposition was unanimously carried, after which a similar vote was accorded to the Chairman on the motion of the Rev. T. Brackenbury, seconded by the Rev. Dr. Preston.</p>
        <p>:HARVEST FESTIVALS. ELLESMERE-ST. WESLEYAN MISSION.</p>
        <p>The annual harvest thanksgiving services in connec- tion with the above mission were held on Sunday after- noon and evening, when there were very large congregations at each service. In the afternoon, there was a musical service given by members of the mission. Solos were efficiently given by Miss Gunton, Miss Raw- ling, and Miss Wheatley, and an address suitable to the occasion was given by the Rev. J. Kirtlan. In the even- ing, the pulpit was occupied by Mr. H. E. Roscollor who preached from the text, "He shall dwell on high, his bread shall be given, and his water shall be sure." The mission was decorated very tastefully with fruits, flowers,and vegetables,the work of which was done by the lady members of the congregation.—On Monday even- ing, a fruit banquet was held in the Mission, presided over by Mr. Levi Richardson. The programme con- sisted of songs, recitations,and addresses,and the follow- ing took part,Messrs. Osborne, Swindells, Philips, Whit- field and Miss L. Bate. Mr. Whittaker also gave an ex- hibition upon the concertina, causing much amusement by imitating the voices of various birds, bells, and 'buzzers." He also gave some clever selections upon the concertina. During the meeting those present were regaled with fruits and cakes, and at the close, all the remaining things were put up for sale. STRETTON CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The annual harvest festival in connection with this church was held on Sunday, a large congregation being present at each service. The anthem in the morning was taken from the words " The eyes of all wait upon Thee, 0 Lord," and was well rendered by the choir. The Archdeacon of Chester occupied the pulpit both morn- ing and evening. The names of the decorators are— East end, Miss Dodd ; screen, Mrs. Dodd and Mr. J. Ben- nett; pulpit, Miss Helen Dodd; eagle, Miss Perry Ays- cough*; pillars, Mr. Parkinson; windows, Misses Darbyshire and Parkinson; end windows, Mr. Nichol- son and Mr. Henry Dodd; font, Mrs. Fletcher. Mr. Ashton Hayes and a friend sent two large loaves and Mr. Fowles a miniature stack. Vegetables, fruit, &amp;c., were contributed by the parishioners and kind friends. The collections which were in aid of the choir fund amounted to over £lB. Mr. Parkinson ably presided at the organ. ORFORD CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Harvest thanksgiving services were held at Orford Church on Sunday. The interior had been beautifully and artistically decorated for the occasion, and fruit, flowers,and vegetables had been used to the best advan- tage reflecting great credit upon the ladies who had en- gaged in this work. The following is a list of the ladies who had assisted in the decorations together pith the particular portions of the church they had embellished, namely—chancel. Mrs.Hartwright and Miss Thompson ; screen, Mrs. Charles Grounds; reading desk and pulpit, Miss Pollitt ; font and lectern,Mis;s Grounds ; north side and west end of church, Mrs. Beamont ; windows, Miss Maggie Richardson and the Misses Fisher ; gasaliers, Miss Woods and Miss Woolmer ; and the remainder of the church, Mrs. Watson, Misses Wilson, Melbourne, Monks, and Povey. The pulpit was occupied at the morning service by the Rev. W. R. Hartwright, curate- in-charge. The anthem, which was creditably rendered by the choir, was "Praise the Lord, &amp;c." At the even- ing service the Rev. A. B. Bater, of the Warrington Training College, was the preacher. The anthem was "0 Lord, how maniford are Thy works," and Mr. Wm. Richardson sang the tenor solo with good effect. Both services were well attended, the congregation in the evening being very large. Offertories were taken during the day towards a fund for the purchase of a new American organ, which is much needed, and amounted to £4 16s. 4d.</p>
        <p>P:S.A. AT THE Y.N.1.0 A.—OPEN MEETING. The quArterly open meeting of the at the Y.M.C.A. was held on Sunday afternoon. Mr. J. W. Hayward, J.P., was in the chair. Mr. Watson, of Warrington. was the speaker, and he chose for his subject, "The likeness of God.' The soloist was Mr. W. H. Ireland, of Penketli, and Hr. F. Hickman was the accompanist. Messrs. Stewart and Gregson also took part in the meeting. Prizes were distri- buted by the chairman to those who had made the reqnisite number (12) of attendances during the quarter. There was a large attendance and the fair sex was well represented. We are pleased to say that the roll of membership continues to increase. TFNNIS VEQUISITE3.--GARLSND, Bridr-streer; War- rington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>- ••••• - V</p>
        <p>THE • EX A MINER, S A TURT) AY OCTOBER 2O 1894. THE 'BUS COLLISION AT ST. HELENS. NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN. -- ACTION AT WARRINGTON COUNTY • COURT. The hearing of the case at the Warrington County Court, for £3 155., damages to a trap belonging to Mr. Massey, Wilderspool-causeway, by a collision, which took place near St.llelens on June 23rd,with one of the 'busses belonging to Mr. Edward Connor, Sankey-st., Warrington, was continued on Thursday before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes. Mr. Arthur Browne again appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. Percy Davies for the defendant. It will be remembered that the plaintiff alleged that on June 23rd, a driver of Mr. Connor, named Perdue, was driving a 'bus from St. Helens to Warrington, and when going down an incline, he ran into his (plaintiff's) trap, doing a considerable amount of damage to it. It was also alleged that the driver was drunk. The case was partly heard at the meeting of the County Court,on Thursday, October 4th, and was adjourned until last Thursday. Mr. Percy Davies said that it was usual in cases of this kind for the defence to attempt to prove contri- butory negligence on the part of the plaintiff. In the present case, he was instructed that the negligence, if there was any negligence at all, was entirely on the part of the plaintiff. If a man had a jibbing horse he must take the consequences. The plaintiff's horse un- doubtedly jibbed on the present occasion, and that caused the accident. If the horse had not jibbed, the accident would never have occurred. It had been sug- gested that the driver, Perdue, was drunk at the time, and as his character was brought into question that was of some importance. As a matter of fact, the driver had on the occasion referred to driven a team of four horses with a chair bus weighing three tons, ten cwts from Warrington to St. Helens, a distance of eight or nine miles. The road was a very difficult one as there were so many abrupt turns in the road. He submitted that if the driver had been drunk, he could not have driven the 'bus along the road. The driver had been in the employ of Mr. Connor for nine years, and he must therefore have been a man of good character. On June 23, he left Warrington at two o'clock in the afternoon, and arrived at St. Helens at 3.30. He was engaged to drive the League Hall Band to play in a contest. He left for home again between nine and 9.30 p.m. He had lit the lamps, and on going down the decline he saw Mr. Massey's horse near the bottom for a long time before he got to it. He noticed the trap stop, and just as he was passing—there being plenty of room to pass—the horse in the trap commenced jibbing and backed the trap into the bus. The horse in question had previously backed into a plate glass win- dow in Bridge-street, SO that there was no doubt of its jibbing character. Thomas Perdue was then called, and said that he was 39 years of age. He had been driving almost all his life. He denied that he was driving at a great speed down the incline, but was going at about seven miles an hour. He was sober, and had had nothing to drink during the journey. After the accident occurred, witness said "Thank goodness that we are not all drunk to-night."—His Honour: "Do you never take an after- noon tea ?" "No, sir." (Laughter.) Samuel Molyneux, manager of Messrs. Dixon, Fletcher and Co., St. Helens, said that he looked after Mr. Connor's horses when they were at St. Helens, just as Mr. Connor looked after his horses when they were in Warrington. He saw the driver Perdue when he set out for his journey to Warrington, and he was sober at that time. Similar evidence was given by a large number of witnesses. The case was further adjourned until Nov. Ist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
