      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <p>_ .</p>
        <p>ST. ANNE'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest thenksgiving services were continued at the above church on Sunday. In the morning the sermon was preached by the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, 8.A., (vicar of Holy Trinity). An anthem "Ye shall dwell in the land" was effectively rendered by the choir. At 2.30 a service for the young people was held and the address was delivered by the Rev. J. Davies (pastor), on Ruth, "a bible gleaner." The evening service was conducted by the Rev. H. V. Pigot, M.A., (vicar of St. Paurs),who chose as his text the 11th of St. Luke, 41st verse, "But rather give alms of such things as ye have; and behold all things are clean unto you." There was a very large congregation and the offertories from all the services amounted to ever £lO. which will be devoted to paro- chial purposes. The decorations inside the church were very tasteful and due to the efforts of the following : Mesdames Plunket, Dunk, Taylor, Glover and Shaw the Misses Davies, Evans, Rigbey, Jefferson, Wheatfield, Hawley, Bunch, Barker, Bailey and others; Messrs. Rigbey, T. Gorton, J Evans, F. Taylor, W. Brown, Gregory, Daniels,Plunket and Q. M. S. Taylor. A large number of gifts of fruit, flowers and corn were given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>SANK-EY BRIDGES WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>SANK-EY BRIDGES WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>The annual harvest festival in connection with the above chapel, was held on Sunday morning and even- ing. The chapel was very artistically decorated with farm produce for the occasion' and conspicuous among the decorations was a largo loaf of bread, and a minia- ture haystack. There were large congregations at the services, especially in the evening. The morning preacher was Mr. S. B. Stringer, and the evening preacher, the Rev. J. Kirtlan who selected as his text, "Behold 1 stand at tho door and knock." During the evening service the choir efficiently rendered the anthems." All Thy works praise Thee," " The Lord is my Shepherd," in which the duet portion was taken by Mrs. Bailey and Miss Hannah Brough ; and" Thou crownest the year," Mr. and Mrs. Bailey taking the duet. Collections were taken at the services in aid of the circuit fund. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>MISSION. BANK QUAY.</head>
        <p>MISSION. BANK QUAY.</p>
        <p>On Sunday, the harvest thanksgiving services in con- nection with the above place of worship were held when two sermons were delivered in the morning and evening by Mr. Amos Kniveton, of Leigh. There were good con- gregations at each service. In the afternoon' a service of song entitled " Joyful Harvesters," was well rendered by the choir, assisted by Leh-wreet Primitive Chapel choir. Mr. William R thefts performed the duties of reader. Solos were given by Mrs. Monks, Mr. S. Hassall, Spruce, etc. Mr. James Mills efficiently per- formed the duties of leader, and .Mr. F. Atkinson presided 3.t the organ with his ivm,il ability. The chapel was tastefully decorated for Ow occasion by the follow- ing lady members of the congregation :—Mrs. T. Ashley, Mrs. Monks, Miss Ashley, Miss J. Birch, and others, to whom great credit is due for the manner in which they had done their work. Plants more kindly lent to the mission by Mr. John Crofie'd, J.P., and Mr. K'Pl3g. his gardener. Collections were takEin during th e day in aid of chapel expeases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>01J1t LOCAL CHURCIIES-WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>01J1t LOCAL CHURCIIES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>At this church, Winwick-street, on Sunday evening, the Rev. J. Warnock took for the subject of his sermon Remorse, or the love that comes too late." He chose as his text Matthew xxyii., 3-4, " Then Judas, which betrayed Him, when he saw that He was condemned re- pented himself, and brought back the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and elders,saying,l have sinned in that I have betrayed innocent blood. But they said, What is that to us ? See thou to that." The preacher said one could not help wondering at times how Judas ever came to be an apostle of our Lord. His name was now the synonym for a character which was universally loathed and detested. He urged his hearers not to envy the success which came by unjust means. There was noth- ing more certain than that happiness could never be theirs unless they had a mind and heart at peace with God. _ _ .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>LATCHFORD BAPTIST CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD BAPTIST CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>The autumnal sermons in connection with the above place of worship were preached on Sunday by the Rev. Charles Cole, ot Bolton. In the afternoon service the rev, gentleman chose as his text, Isaiah 55, last verse, "Instead of the thorn shall come up the fir tree, and instead 0.,f the brier shall come up the myrtle tree," and at night he preached an excellent sermon ell "The zeal of thine house, etc." An anthem was sung by the choir at each service. The congregations were good and the collections in aid of the church funds amounted to £lo3s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>CAIRO-ST. CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>CAIRO-ST. CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening, at the Cairo-st. Unitarian Chapel, the Rev. W. H. Drummond, preaching from the words When he was yet a great way off his father saw him and had compassion, and ran, and fell on his neck and kissed him" dealt with an article of Mr. Gladstone's on "The Atonement." He said Mr. Gladstone's objet was to exhibit the doctrine of the atonement in such a light that so far from being a breach in the moral order of the world it might be seen at its fairest illustration. They could find in his purpose much to admire and be thank- ful for, and they recognised the moral hssi...;ht and en- thusiasm with which it was presented to them. There was a further real admission that the doctrine of atone- ment as commonly taught and accepted needed modifi- don, or at least change of emphasis in order to meet these requirements. Three points appeared to him specially noteworthy : (1) The one object was to work a reformation in men ; not to reconcile God to them,but to bring them back into fellowship with God. (2) There was an absence which he could not but conside r remarkable of sacrificial terminology which, borrowed from the least lovely and spiritual part of the Old Testament, had usurped such a prominent place in Christian theology and devotion. (3) There was no reference except in a few guarded words to original sin and human depravity as making men incapable of good except through the merits of Christ, He (Mr. Drum- mond) thought they might deduce from these argu- ments that God accepted men not for the sake of Christ, His righteousness covering their sins, but for the real love and goodness which were in themselves. There were acme minds who delighted to enlarge upon the distinctions of the Divine Nature, the prerogatives of the Eternal Son, and the necessity of His atoning sacri- fice. On matters such as these he frankly confessed his complete ignorance. They might interest him as subjects of human speculation,but they did not touch his</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>SERVICES FOR THE PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>SERVICES FOR THE PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>The usual People's Service was held in Bold-st. Wes- leyan Church on Sunday afternoon. The service Was opened by the band playing an Andante by Neir ; and the hymn " All hail the power c,f Jean's name," having been sung, the Rev. G. D. Mason read a portion of scripture. Mr. Dile engaged in prayer, after which the Rev. G. D. Mason heartily welcomed the congregation that afternoon to the service. They sometimes heard it said that the reason people did not attend such services was because they were not made welcome. His own ex- perience was directly opposite to that, for the people who did appreciee services of that kind were always ready to make others as welcome as they possibly. could. Too many loved darkness rather than light, because their deeds were evil. Mr. Podmore then played " With Verdure Clad " from " Creation," in a manner which proved him to be an accomplished flautist. Mr. Albert Perkins (Joyful News Evangelist), late of Wirrinqton, then delivered a brief address. He said when Mr. Mason asked him last Thursday if he would give an address there lie felt placed in a difficulty, as he had never at- tended a service of that kind before, and did not know what sort of ari address would suit. Life was a great harvest field. What they sowed in their young days they reaped when they got old. Let them try, tnerefore, to sow good seed, so that they would reap good, and that they would all be present at the last great harvest home. Mr. James Bennat then rave a creditable rendering of "Jerusalem." and the service was brought to a close by the band playing "See the conquering hero comes." ANNIVERSARY OF THE WARRINGTON CHURCH</p>
        <p>NOTES FROM LIVERPOOL AND DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>(BY _A LIMB OF THE LAW.)</p>
        <p>The Rev. C. F. Aked, of Pembroke Chapel. Liver- pool, has again been preaching what is termed a political sermon. The rise of Aked in Liverpool has been somewhat phenomenal, and he is to-day looked upon as the most popular Nonconformist preacher in this district. He is never afraid of deal- ing with public questions, and the consequence is that the public go to hear him. It is difficult at times to procure a seat in his chapel,and on the last occasion I went I was glad to avail myself of a seat in the aisle. Aked is gifted with a splendid memory and a good command of language, though at times he comes out with statements that startle his hearers. He is in religion a Baptist, in politics a Liberal, and in temperance, a staunch teetotaller. In the immediate neighbourhoud of his chapel pra,tical good is being done, as over 1,000 poor children are prior to going to school provided with a breakfast, the expense of which is derived from the worshippers at his chapel.</p>
        <p>Last Sunday morning his sermon was on Mr. Gladstono's, Mr. Chamberlain's, and the Bishop of Chester's lows with regard to the drink question. His sermo,i was an adverse criticism of the Gothen- burg system and an appeal in favour of the direct veto. Per., Ma Ily, I don't think any Bill will pass Parliament where a majority will be empowered to prevent a minority from being supplied with alcoholic liquors. The best solution of the problem would be to municipalise ;lie public-houses and let the number of them be limited according to the population.</p>
        <p>The announcement that the Ship Canal Company has made accommodation for 1,000 cattle at a lair- age has aroused some interest in Birkenhead. The killing of cattle for domestic purposes is one of Birkenhead's chief trades and hence the apprehen- sion that Manchester may take a portion of this away.</p>
        <p>Mentioning the Ship Canal Company reminds me that nearly £4O was spent in cigars, wines, and spirits on the occasion of its opening. Here in Liverpool at one time our city fathers were given to weaknesses in dining at the ratepayers' expense, and some of them were fond of " ham boiled in champagne." It is remarkable how easily people will spend other persons' money, even if they are councillors or aldermen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The anniversary of the Warrington Church Society was held on Thursday, when a service was held in the Parish Church at which the Rev. M. F. Binney, vicar of Sutton, St. Helens, was the preacher. There was a small congregation. After the service, the annual meet- ing was held at the National School,Church-street. The chair was occupied by the president, the Rev. F. W. Willis (rector). The hon. sec., Mr. Herbert Woods, in giving the annual report of the society, said that the work had chiefly been in providing lectures. A reading union had also been established under the auspices of the society, the object of which was to promote a systematic study of the Holy Scriptures. They com- menced the last year with 71 members, and during the year 23 members had joined and seven resigned owing to their leaving the district. There was therebre a meteßrship at present of 87. He regretted the loss during the year of the Rev. Canon Heron, who though not a member of the society had given great assistance, and expressed great sympathy with its objects.—The treasurer Mr. Robert D,bson said that the total expenditure of the year amounted to £2O is. 21. which left a balance doe to the treasurer of £4 14s. id. This with a nn. her outstanding liability of £1 3s. made a total of £5 17s. id. The adverse balance last year was £6 is. The reports were adopted on the motion of Mr. Westbrook seconded by the Rev. W. R. Hartwright. The following officials were elected for the ensuing year : president, the Rector; treasurer Mr. R. Dobson ; hon. secretary,Mr. H. Woods ; committee Revs. Morley Stevenson, and W. R. Hartwright, Messrs. C. S. Burgess. J. D. Buckton, F. M. Appleton, H. J. West- brook, J. Hesketh, J. Macgregor, and .1. D. Sekker. A vote of thanks was passed to the preacher. the Rev. M. F. Binney, on the motion of Mr. C. S. Burgess, seconded by the Rev. W. R. Hartwright, after which the proceedings terminated.</p>
        <p>OWD BUNDLER SWOPPIN' CREEDS."</p>
        <p>CIRCUMSTANCES AWTERS CASES.</p>
        <p>"Aw oncA wuz a Churchmon—aw'm summut else neaw, An' why? Cos my Boss is the same; Re's the piousest chap aw reaund here, shuz heaw,</p>
        <p>An' to follow his leeud's no shame. "On Sundays aw send one o'th wenches ereaut, To watch when he cum to his pew ; An' ez soon ez boo sees him hoo eies me a sheaut— " Be off to yore worshuppin', do !" " Behnwd me i'th' front, io the nicest o' suits, (Bow't at Lewis's—cost thirty bob—) Wi' a bonny red necktie, an' weel polished boots, An' a " three-an'-six " hat on my nob. "Then aw skyeaut slyly reaund w.n aw get i' mi' place, To see if he's cum in at th' door; An' if aw con just see a smile on his face, My promotion's not far off, aw'm sure. Wot a angel he look, ez he howcisde two his yed, An' gies thanks fer his bed an' his tr;ar 1 ; Fer he favvers ez if—ez aw've offen heerd Fed— _</p>
        <p>He wuz just " shakin' hoods wi' the Lord !" " Befoor he coom, aw to church never went Moor than two er three times in a year; But neaw,—wen he sees me,—my time's alias spent, Singin' " 0 but its good to be here." " Heaw quare it duz look, wen his holiday's on, An' he'z gone to a big rowlin' tide; To find quite th' tone hauf o' nz worshuppers gone, Annywheer but to Zion's inside! "Which it makes me remark, wen Eaur Boss ez in tin To a glorious mansion i'th' sky, They'll hey to discover sum fillin up plan,</p>
        <p>Er the parsons may aw say Good-bye. - " But aw hope aw'st o Cost, fer aw wudn't backslide, WiT th' Owd Veteran's thPer to see ; Solwen th' bey thebearer cums, may he let th' Owd Chap bide, - Till he's made a full settlin' wi' me !"</p>
        <p>TUM 0' PEGS.</p>
        <p>Rose Bonk, Oct. 19, 1894.</p>
        <p>..,-.-....---.</p>
        <p>According to Galton the patterns on the finger-tips are not only unchangeable throughout life, but the chance of the finger prints of two persons oeing alike is less than one chance in 64,000.000,000. STOCKTON HEATH-.</p>
        <p>NARROW ESCAPE FOR 'Bus.—On Tuesday afternoon, a very exciting scene was Witnessed at Stockton Heath. The 'bus which runs between Warrington and Stockton- Heath, had finished its journey at the last-named place, the passengers had alighted and the heads of the horses were turned in the direction of Warrington. a little rest being given to the animals before commencing the re- turn journey. All at once, wit hoot any apparent cause, the horses became alarmed and nil off in the direction of the Ship Canal swing-bridge. The bridge had been thrown open to allow a steamer tl pass, and the gates had been closed against road traffic. The affrighted animals dashed through the gates and would have been precipitated into the water below but, fortunately the huge bridge, whinh was bPinz closed, was swung to ia t,</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</head>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>I wonder what the pessimists, who declaimed so loudly, about the white elephant, which now bears the name of "Town Hall," think as to its tisefulness. They appear to have sunk altogether into oblivion, and as their tirades against the " folly," as they called it decreased, so has the popularity of the Town Nall increased. Certainly a long felt want (to use a hackneyed phrase) has been supplied, and the Earlestown people recognise that they have now got in their midst a most useful building. The indoor amusement season has only just commenced but already I learn that the hall is booked f6r en- gagements for a long period in advance. This week for three nights we have had a .fairly good troupe of minstrels with us, and as proving that the Hades- town people are not i“ to resplactanle amuse- ment, I may say that the performances have been remarkably well attended. Concerts and balls are promised in galore during the winter, and at this rate I am thinking the ratepayers will not be troubled very much about paying for the Town</p>
        <p>At the beginning of the football season. I believe I expressed the opinion that owing to the Earles- town club having severed their connection with the Liverpool and District League,the public would not patronise the games on the Mesnes quite as much as they formerly did. Of course this was only an opinion, but from what has transpired so far I am inclined to admit that I have been wiong. Only three or four matches have been played at home, and each of them has been exceptionally well- attended. lam glad of this, because the coffers of the clib will largely benefit thereby. The spectators are receiving plenty of excitement, as Earlestown are playing remarkably well at present, and bear very well the honour of being so far unbeaten dur- ing this season.</p>
        <p>I trust I shall be excused for again trotting out the District Council question before your readers, but really there is nothing else very exciting to write about. Since I last broached the matter one candidate, who as not been selected by any com- mittee, luta placed himself prominently before the public, or should I say more correctly, the electors of Newton Ward. Now the Newton Ward is at the other end of the township, and although it is in- cluded in Newton-in-Alakerfield, it might be in the antipodes for all the interest the Earlestown folks manifest in its proceedings. The Newton people are to a great extent themselves responsible for this, as they have, on this occasion especially, acted entirely independent of the other wards, and have sought advice from nobody outside their own ward.</p>
        <p>This is capitally illustrated by the fact that the aforementioned candidate has issued an address to the electors,and has the honour of being first in the field. His appeal is addressed chiefly to working- men, and intimates his intention of going in for the Council meetings to he held after six 0 clock in the evening. He has paid rates for 17 years, and in addition to giving attention to the water supply (for Newton) he will go in for more efficiency in the removal of the house refuse and nightsoil. (Efficiency badly wanted). He is great on the post office question, and will tight tooth and nail against Earlestown and anywhere else in the district hav- ing a post office erected out of the rates. " Them's my sentiments," but is it seriously intended to do anything of the kind. We want a central post office, and a central office we must have, no matter who pays for it. 1 &amp;hould like to get hold of a few more addresses, because when complete, I think they will form a most interesting volume, if nicely bound, and after the election it will be curious to see how far the councillors abide by their promises.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>DUKE OF CAMBRIDGE ON THE "LOWER ORDERS."</head>
        <p>THE DUKE OF CAMBRIDGE ON THE "LOWER ORDERS."</p>
        <p>The Duke of Cambridge has been opening a technical school at Maidstone, and he has says the "Daily Chronicle," been favouring us with his views on things in general and progress in particular. " All progress." says the Commander-in-Chief," must be gradual," and he is afraid "that we are too apt to think the old institu- tions are no longer valuable." " Unfortunately," says the Duke, " we all get older, and I am very less able to discharge my duties than I was formerly." This is an opinion from which most people will not dissent. And then the Duke goes on to pour out his wisdom on the "lower orders." He hopes the Maidstone school will " lead to a wholesome feeling in the lower orders." "Depend upon it, unless we can get on' nowadays with- out strikes, we shall never progress in the world. Pro- gress is somewhat dangerous. We must take care that the education we give is suitable to the individual who receives it. Too much learning is a dangerous thing. (a new reading, this.) We hear of history and geography, and I don't know what else, being taught to all manner of people. But what good is it ? It only makes them discontented." Really these sparkling paradoxes almost make UR believe that the Duke must know soniething about the authorship of the " Green Carnation." One of his royal ancestors also thought slightingly of "boctry and bainting," while another asked, " Was there ever such stuff as Shakespeare ?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>ALLEGED BIGAMY AT ST. HELENS,</head>
        <p>ALLEGED BIGAMY AT ST. HELENS,</p>
        <p>At St. Helens on Tuesday, before Alderman M‘Bryde, Mr. B. B. Glover, and Mr. W. J. Thom- son, John Howard Pearson, a stoker, living at 97, Brynn-st., was charged on warrant with commit- ting bigamy. Margaret Anders, a middle-aged woman, stated that about three years ago she worked at Sherdley Colliery, where prisoner was also working. At the beginning of 1893 she was for some time an inmate of Whiston Workhouse, but in April, while lodging in St. James's-st.. she again met him, and he said he was a widower and wanted to make a home for himself. He asked her to be his wife, and after he had visited her and walked out with her a few times she consented to marry him. On the 22nd of July, 1893, at the Parish Church, St. Helens, she went through the form of mar- riage with him. He then described himself as a widower. John William Foster and Mary Ann Forster, with whom she was then lodging in Corporation-st., were witnesses. In March, 1894, a child was born. Last month he deserted her, and she obtained a sum- mons against him, but when the case caine on his wife presented herself in court, and no order was made. Prisoner was unkind to her while she lived with him, and he turned her out.—Prisoner denied this, and told witness to "own to the truth."—Mary Ann Foster said that when prisoner came courting Margaret Anders he came dressed in black and wearing a black tie. He said he was in mourning for his wife. She witnessed the marriage of prisoner and Margaret Anders.—Thos. Tinsley, glassmaker, 15, Grafton-st., proved a previous marriage on the 13th July, 1884, with Hannah Glover. Witness saw prisoner's wife (nee Hannah Glover) a fortnight ago, and had since seen her frequently. He spoke to her on Monday.--Police-constable Holden said he arrested the prisoner on Monday. On hearing the warrant read he replied. "It was reported out twice that she was dead, and if I had thought she was not dead, I should never have married again. When she came over to St. Helens from Wigan she said she would not inter- fere with me in no way whatever. She said I could' please myself what I did, and where I went and get married again if I had a mind. I took this Margaret Anders expecting she would make me a comfortable home. She knew all about it."—The prisoner was com- mitted to the assizes for trial.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN" A" v. Nsw BRIGHTON" A."—These teams met on Satutday afternoon on the Now Brighton en- closure. The respective players were :—Runcorn : Jolley, full back; West, Antrobus, Lightfoot, and , Gregory, three-quarter backs; Nickson and Wcodward, half backs; Stubbs, Clarke, Moores, Tatlock, Shaw, Darbyshire. Walker, and Jeacock. forwards. New Brighton; Dawler, full back; Lewis, C. Tomlinson, Dodd, and Hannay, three-quarter backs; Tomlinson and Smith, half backs; Hind. Harvey,J. McWilliams, J. W. McWilliams. Seddon, Nicholas, Miller and Walker. Antrobus kicked off for the Runcornians who immediate- ly besieged the homesters. Dodd cleared but shortly afterwards the oval was returned and West succeeded in registering the first try for the visitors. The hotnesters had a short look-in but could not score. Two more tries were secured in quick succession, no goals resulting from the kicks. Moores was subsequently disabled and was taken from the field. His absence materially weakened the visitors who were placed on the defensive for some time. However, they managed to not only prevent their opponents scoring but gained two additional tries and at half-time were leading by five tries to nothing. In the second portion of the game some good combination pla.v was seen on part of the Runcornians, who before the call of full time increased their score to on' : goal and six tries, thus winning a brilliant victory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>BAPTIST BAZAAR AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BAPTIST BAZAAR AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The little schoolroom attached to the Baptist Chapel in Golborne-street, wore quite a gay appear- ance on Wednesday afternoon, when a bazaar was opened in aid of the funds of the church. The decorating and arranging of the various stalls bore evidence of the pains-taking care of a willing band of helpers, who had been for some time engaged in the work. The stalls in the schoolroom numbered five and they were draped with material of the striking colours of pink, yellow and green. This material had been kindly lent for the occasion by the Vicar of Padgate (Rev. J. J. Longharn). The young men had rigged up a stall along the lengthy entrance to the schoolroom which was covered in with stout canvas. On this stall and on the ladies and Sunday school stalls were exposed for sale numberless articles which come under that very inclusive phrase "useful and orna- mental." In addition there was an advertisement stall which, as its name implies, advrrtised several manufac- turers' commodities, and the caretakers of a flower stall were quite ready to supply pretty buttonholes. The "inner man" was catered for at a w ell-stocked refresh- ment stall, and there was also an ice-cream stall. In addition to these stalls there was plenty both to amuse and instruct the visitors. A fish pond had been pre- pared and this was in charge of Miss Mabel and Hettie Balmford and Miss Hall. In a miniature museum there was exhibited a wood model of the chapel building. admirably executed by Mr. Fry. Mr. Morton, electrical engineer. Sankey-street, undertook the charge of an electrical battery, and Professor Betts, of Liverpool, conducted phrenological examinations. Added to these, the phonograph and telephone, were brought into requisition. The following assisted at the various stalls : Ladies' stall : Mrs. Benjamin Few, Mrs. John S. Green, Mrs. Balmford, Mrs. Hightield, Mrs. L9ngshaw. Mrs. Joseph Hall, Mrs. Fry, Mr,. Bukhy, and Mrs. Freeman ; Sunday school stall : Mrs. Fernley, Miss Fieldhouse, Miss Gemmel, Miss E. Fieldhouse, Miss Jackson, Miss Ward, and Miss Freeman; flower stall : Miss Few,Miss Hickman, Miss Richards, Miss A. Balinford ; refresh- ment stall ; Miss Potter, Mrs. and Miss Patrick, Mrs. Frain, Mrs. Hickman, Miss Hamer, Miss Waring and Miss Buckley ; young men's stall: Messrs. H. Buckby, W. Patrick, H. Walker, F. Pennington. G. Patrick, J. Fry, and J. Hamer; advertisement stall : Rev. D. W. Roberts, Mr. G. Hall. and Mr. David Patrick ; ice cr?am stall : Miss Martin. The secretaries upon whom devolved a considerable amount of work were Messrs. F. Hickman and B. Crowder.</p>
        <p>At the opening ceremony there was a good attendance amongst those present being the Revs. D. W. Roberts (pastor), J. Yonge, J. S. Hughes, and E.T. Reed, Messrs. J. Grippe, J.P., (Liverpool), J. W. Hayward, J.P., W. Hickman, John S. Green, G. Hall, Buckby, Fry, and others. After the singing of a hymn and prayer by the Rev. J. Yonge The Rev, D. W. ROBERTS briefly set forth the objects of the bar, tar. Ho said the proceeds were in aid of churth funds. At first they thought to have a bazaar in aid of new schools but as time went on they found that they could not go on with new schools and neglect the financial state of the church. They had had the chapel beautified and the treasurer was somewhat in debt, and theref&lt;.re they thought it best that the bazaar should be in behalf or the church funds for the carrying on of God's work in that part of his vineyard. They trusted that they would receive liberal snpport and that they would raise the amount of money they required. He stated that several friends in the district had responded very liberally indeed. The speaker then read a list of sub4cribers from which it appeared that £24 73. id. had been promised in donations. He also added that they had also received many generous gifts of goods. Referr- ing to Mr. Cripp3 whom he called upon to declare the bazaar open he said that that gentleman was a warm supporter of the Baptist cause and had done a good deal for the spread of Christianity in Lancashire. (Applause.) Mr. J. CRIPPS said he felt it a very great honour and pleasure to take part in that opening ceremony. He thanked them heartily for the cordial welcome they had accorded to him. He had known many who were con- nected with them, particularly Mr. Crowder. They had don a great work and had been very industrious in the cause and he hoped thcse present would appreciate vilAt had been done by purchasing the articles for sale and by doing the best they could to further the object the promoters had in view. .(Applause.) He was very glad to see other Christian brethren present, and that Nonconformity flourished there. (Applause.) He was only surprised that the Bapti,t cause had not flourished more than it had done in the town. but he hoped they would increase more and more and become a great blessing to the town of Warrington. (Applause.) Rev. J. S HUGHES. of Hill Cliffe Baptist Chapel, pro- posed a vote of thanks to Mr. Cripps. He had known Mr. Cripps for someyears and had worked on some com- mittees with him in connection with their denomination.</p>
        <p>:They had always found in him a great helper to the de- nomination. He did not confine his help to the large church which he was connected but also the smaller churches in Lancashire and Cheshire. He took that opportunity of tendering the thanks of the smaller churches of the denominatitan for his help. He was pleased to see that so much had been done to support the hands of his brother in the ministry there,and he trusted that they would be successful, and that a large amount would be gathered to the exchequer of the church. (Ap- plause.) Mr. W. HICKMAN said that for two or three reasons it gave him pleasure to second the vote of thanks. He happened to belong to another church, and as represent- ing the great Methodist Church he felt it an honour to join with them and take some share in seconding the vote of thanks. He also wished to express their warm sympathy with the Golhorne-et. friends, and wish them every success. He hoped they would realise more than they wanted. (Applause.) The sale was then proceeded with, and the result of the first day's sale amounted to £66 3s. 10?-,</p>
        <p>Rbbrrtianwnts not Cia5511103.</p>
