      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>HALTON PAROCHIAL COMMITTRE.—At the fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Sanitary Authority held on Tuesday afternoon in the Wcrkhouse, Dutton, Mr. John Mclntyre presiding, the minutes of the last meeting of the above committee, held on the 13th inst., were sub- mitted for confirmation. They showed that the Clerk</p>
        <p>had received a communication- from the Local Govern-</p>
        <p>ment Board with reference to the pollution of Halton Brook, by the drainage of certain houses therein, and stated that the medical officer had been instructed to prepare a report to be submitted to the Local Govern- ment Board, setting forth what he considered should be done to comply with the provisions of the Rivers Pollu- tion Prevention Ac's. The medical officer had reported that it would be advisable to either amend the existing arrangements by the ad lition of some of the provisions for sewage precipitation and filtration, or to ascertain if some means of irrigation could not be carried out, which would involve less labour. It should be stated, how- ever, that it was only during scarcity of rain that any objection could be taken to the existing arrangement. It was resolved that the report be referred back to the medical officer, for him to set out explicitly what he considered ought to be done to comply with the provis- ions of the Rivers Pollution Prevention Acts.—The minutes were confirmed on the proposition of Mr. Stokes. econded by Mr. E. Aaron.</p>
        <p>THE RATEPAYERS AND THE PARISH COUNCILS ACT.— A meeting of ratepayers was held on Tuesday evening in the Girls' School, Halton, for the purpose of hearing read the order received from the Cheshire County Coun- cil fixing the number of Parish Councillors under the Local Government Act, 1894. The Rev. J. D. Wray occupied the chair and there was a crowded attendance. Amongst those present were Messrs. Thomas Wright, Hugh Bowes, (overseers of the township,) Albert Woodier, (assistant overseer) Joseph Stokes, James Looker, John Barrow. John Baautey, Samuel Barrow, J. S. Peacock, John 1.4,,a, W. McCullock, J. M. Baguley, and others.—Mr. Thomas VI right read certain extracts from the Order containing nvcessary information which the villagers should know. It was set forth in some of these that the numher of parish councillors allotted to Halton was twelve ; that the day and time for the nomi- nation of candidates should be the 4-th September; and that the election of such candidates be on the 17th De- cember. A long discussion followed during which a large number of questions were asked and answered. The meeting then terminated. . _</p>
        <p>CHILD R EN'S CONCERT.—A successful concert was given on Friday night in the Halton Parochial School by the children attending the girls' school. A large audi- ence was present, and the programme, which was made up of songs, recitations, drills, and an operetta entitled "The white garland," was admirably gone through by the children. Their performances frequently elicited loud applause of which they were very evidently deserv- ing. The receipts are to purchase prizes for the day school scholars.</p>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A PUBLICAN.—At the Runcorn Petty Sessions, on Monday. before Messrs. Char!es Reynolds and G. L. Davies, Ellen Simpson applied for a warrant against her husband, John Simpson, landlord of the Castle Hotel, Halton. who had not appeared that morn- ing in answer to a summons charging him with having assaulted her. In suoport of her application for a warrant she said that. on the Bth inst. her husband threw a candlestick at her which she prevented from striking her head by putting her arm up. He had many times threatened her life. He had threatened to set the house on fire,and on one occasion had menaced her with a pick hammar.—On Tuesday morning, before Messrs. F. J. Normon and S. Taylor, the defendant John Simp- son was brought up at the Runcorn Police Court,on the charge already mentioned. He was represented by Mr. Burton, and the complainant by Mr. Allen. Defendant pleaded not guiity.ancl the case was adjourned until the Petty Sessions on Monday, bail being granted in £2, and a surety of £lO. CRICKET CLun SOIREE.—On Wednesday evening the Halton Cricket (-lob brought a pleasant termination to their season by holding a soiree, which proved a finan- cial success. The event took place in the Wesleyan schoolroom, the president of the club, the Rev. W. Lila Stone, occupying the chair. Alter a few remarks rela- tive to the improvement which such healthy recreations as cricket effected on the mind, a capital programme of vocal and instrumental tousle was given. Among the artistes were Mr. and Mrs. Burn, Mrs. and Miss War- burton, Misses A. Looker, Biys, E. and A. Barrow, Messrs. C P. Bradbury, John Saunders, S. Saunders, A. Saunders, B. Sutteat. and A;bert Rowe. Excellent refreshment; were provitled,and the evening was passed in a very enjoyable way.</p>
        <p>NEWTON VOLUNTEER NEWS. At the orterly-I•corn. Newton, on Wednesday night, z-ergeitnt':, certificates were presented by Sergeant-Ins.tructor Wooding, to the following member,- of' " G "Company 1.4 V.B.S.L.Regt. as the result of the examination held in April last, viz. : Lance-SI-rgeant Kenny, Corporal S. Roberts,— Corporal G. .Tlielron, Corporal T. Dealer, Lance Corporal J. Pouioun, Privut,t; J. Atkinson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
