      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>BEFORE_ TEE MAGISTRITES</head>
        <p>BEFORE_ TEE MAGISTRITES</p>
        <p>STRANGE CONDUCT OF A DRUNKEN MAN. Martin O'Brien, Percival's-fold, was guilty of some very strange behaviour on Saturday night, and if it was simply a drunken freak,he has had to suffer considerahly for it. He had been spending his time in some of the public-houses of the town' and after turning-out time he had to go through the Market Place and Lyme-street on his way home. On his way he kicked at the window frame of every shop he passed, and upon coming to the shop of Messrs. Neison and Co., he kicked the window itself, and smashed it. The window was a very large one, and was valued at £5 ss. He had been noticed by two persons,Hatry Green and John Cotterall,the former of whom followed him and caught him on Town Hill, while the latter went for the police. P.C. Dean and P.C. Meacock were informed, and they took O'Brien to the Police Station. He was brought before the Warrington magistrates on Monday morning, and the charge was amended to " breaking a window to the value of £4 18s. " so that the case could be tried at once. When asked what he had to say for himself, he said that he did not intend to do the -damage he did. The magis- trates decided to inflict a fine of ss. and costs upon the prisoner for being drunk, and ordered him to pay the cost of the window, or in default to go to gaol tor two months. The prisoner asked whether he would he al- lowed to pay the money in instalments, but Mr. Ref t. Lowe, on behalf of Messrs. Nelson and Co., refused his request. ALLEGED THETT OF WHISKEY FROM THE RAILWAY. On Tuesday morning Andrew Sauce and Thomas Sauce, brothers, were charged with stealing from a railway waggon stationed in Arpley siding on the evening of the 12th instoa jar containilig six gallons of Scotch whisky, the property of the London and North-Western Railway Company and valued as £4 10. In his statement to the magistrates, the Chief Constable (Mr. Hunt) stated that the complainants were the London and North-Westiern Railway Company,who were represented by their Detec- tive, John Dipole. The first priswer is a puddler work- ing at Bewsey Forge and residing with the other prisoner at 24. 'Mani-at. ; the latter is employed by the railway company as a " sheeter "and earned 18s. a week. On Friday evening at a quarter past. six Thomas Sauce left his work at Arplev Station and his last act was to slieet up the waggon which contained the six gallon jar of whiskey which was labelled to a hotel keeper in Warring- ton. On Saturday morning the jar was missed by Alick Stewart, foreman, who reported to Detective Dipole. Inquiries were made and on the second prisoner bring asked for,it was found that he had not come to his work. The detective met.P.C. Delahay. and in company with him proceeded to the residence of the prisoners, but could not find them. After some search Dipole at last found the jar on Wednesday, hidden in a bosh close to the railing whith divides the siding from Wilson- Patten-st. The same evening at a quarter to nine Dipole and Delahay hid themselves near the bush and saw Andrew Sauce come along the line and afterwards lie down near the jar. The officers then sprang upon him and found several smaller bottles in his possession, pro- bably for the purpose of taking the whiskey away in. He was brought to the police station ,nd on being charged with the theft his reply was " You're a liar." Afterwards Dipole and Delahay went to the prisoners' residence and found the second prisoner in bed, drunk. They also brought him to the police station, where he denied all knowledge of the liquor.—Detective Dipple corroborated Mr. Hunt's statement, and said that when they arrested the prisoner and asked him where he got the whiskey, ho replied that "there were a &amp;zed of them in it, not far off." meaning that it was not safe for the officers to arrest him as they were only two. There were only five quarts left in the jar when they re- covered it.—On Mr. Hunt's application the case was re- manded until next Tuesday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MIN olt CASES.</head>
        <p>MIN olt CASES.</p>
        <p>:WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT. SATURDAY.—Before Mr. John Burzess (in the chair),</p>
        <p>Messrs. S. Chandlev and L. V.l:Fv. Tug EGG THEFT.--Jane HiaffirtS, 18, Stephen-st., was charged on remand with.. stealing 36 worth of eggs on die Bth October, the property of Thomas Higgins, uncle of the defendant. It will be remembered that on Thurs- day at the Police Court, the defendant after pleadine guilty, made a statement to the effect that she had an. accomplice. She now admitted that she had been telling falsehnndsond that she had rot received any help in the theft. 5. Monaghan stated that he had made inquiries and found that there was no such person as Clara Wellington,the accomplice, and the girl had afterwards inade this admission to him. After some deliberation the magistrates sentenced the defendant to gaol for three days, that three days being covered by the im- prisonment which she had already suffered. MONDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (chairman), Mr. J. W. Hayward, Mr. S. Wright, and Alderman Harrison. DRUNKENNESS.—Ann Wilson, last from M4nchester, was fined in the costs for this offence. Catherine Molloy, no address, was also fined ss. or seven days; Margaret McLoughlin, no address, one month; Henry Ryan, Friars' Green, 2s. 6d. ALLEGED FRAnDULENT ENLISTMENT -WM. Mini well was discharged upon this charge. The prisoner had been invalided from the Royal Artillery. CRUELTY TO A HORSE.—Peter rratt, 9. Refuge Square, was charged with ill-treat iiig a horse in Horse- rnarkEt-street, by working it in an unfit state on October 11th. P.C. Chrimes stated that the girth strap was • hbing on an old wound causing blood to issue from it. A hen told to take it back he did so.—Defendant was tided 10S. THEFT FROM A FELLOW-LODGER.—Thomas Harrison, lodging in Bank-street, was charged with stealing from Charles Dunleavy, a fellow-lo3ger, on Siturday night ts. Bd. in cash. From evidence it was shown that the complainant was a Ship Canal labourer. He went to bed on Saturday night and placed his trousers contain- ing the money, under his pillow. Later on in the night prisoner was seen by another lodger named Brown, to g,et up and take the trousers from under the pillow.and take the money out. He then threw the trousers back. Be then looked for the waistcoat but could not find it. Brown asked him what he was after, and the prisoner asked for a light. He afterwards got into the bed again. Next morning, Dunleavy on awaking found his trougers out of their place and also found that his money to the amount of ss. Bd. was missing. He gave the alarmsand Brown told him what he had seen with the result that P.C. Jones was called in. Prisoner was taken to the police station, and when charged he denied the offence.— The prisoner was Sent to gaol for one month. BREACHES OF THE PEACE.—Thomas Clare, 12, Foun- tain-strret. and Robert Yardley. 40 New-road were charged with committing a breach of the peace by fight- log in New-road, on October 9. P.C. Berry who proved the case stated that the quarrel was caused by playing football. They were bound over to keep the peace for six months, and ordered to pay the costs or in default seven days.—Patrick McGrath. 35, Town Hill, was also bound over for fighting with a person named Hopkins. Hopkins was also summoned, but did not appear, and a warrant was ordered for his apprehension. OBSTRUCTION AT CENTRAL STATION.—Perick McGrath,</p>
        <p>- - Fil, SEALSKIN JACKETS AND FURS 0118 every description CLEANED or RE-DYBD and ALTERED. FUR SKINS dressed and made up into rugs tt.c.—B Comm Manufacturing Furrier, 11, Upper Newing- ton, and 21, Renshaw-street,: Liverpool. Established 20 ears.</p>
