      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>• - • • •••'• - .:• • _ _ _ _ _ _ • -</p>
        <p>35 Town Hill, John Kearns, 18 Silver-street, were sent to gaol for a month, for obstructing the footpath at Central Station, on Oct. 4:h. P.C. Corcoran stated that the two defendants were a constant source of annoyance to the police and passengers. McGrath wanted to know why he was not reported for similiar offences on other days, whereupon Mr. Hunt asked for full penalty to he put on as it was evident that they been obstructing at other times, and it was well-known to the police that they did. USING THE CATAPIILT.—SaMUeI Miller was charged with discharging a catapult in Museum-st., on Oct. 7. P.C. Sankey proved the case. He was ordered to receive six strokes.—John Warburton, 101, Bewsey-road was fined ss. and James Allen, 39, Hoyle-stieet, as ordered to receive six strokes fur throwing stones in Bewsey-st. on Oct. 9. P.S. Cheney proved the case. WEDNESDAY.—Before Alderman Wright (chairman) and</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Wright. LICENSE. PERMIT —Absalom Taylor was granted temporary autlwrity to sell at 335, Winwick-road, as an off license. .</p>
        <p>DRUNK IND DISORDERLY.—MichaeI Grant was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Church-st., on Tuesday night. A further charge was preferred against him for assaulting Robert Flatman, at the same time and place.—P.C. Baxendale stated that at 10.30 on Tuesday night he heard of a disturbance in Church- st.,and on going th9re saw defendant kicking complain- ant.—Robert Flatman, complainant, described the as- sault, which he said was entirely unprovoked. A wit- ness who was present at the time ot the assault, corrro- borated.—Defendant was fined ss. and costs, or seven days.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEnsEsnar.---Before Mr. G. Comber (chairman) and</p>
        <p>Mr. John White.</p>
        <p>DOGS AT LARGE.—Mr. Robert Garnett, J.P., was Sum- moned for having on the 7th October allowed a dog to be at large in Warringtorl-road. Ponketti. Mr. Philip Garnett stated that the dog got out of the yard acciden- tally, and had only been out a few minutes. He was ordered to pay the costs.—Samuel Fusill was similarly dealt with for allowing his dog to he at large in Heath- road, Penketh, on the 7th inst. P.C. Thorley proved the cases.—Richard Forshaw failed to answer a charge of allowing his dog to be at large at Burtonwood, on the 30th September. A fine of 2s. 61. and costs was im- posed. TEMPORARY AtrritoniTY.—Robert Ryder was granted temporary authority to sell at the Bowling. Green lon, Riston, in lieu of Thomas Lawrence.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>lioNiu.ly.—B-efore Messrs. Henry Reynolds, and G. L Davies.</p>
        <p>A NEGLECTFUL PARENT.—F4tfiCk Phillip, Of Vine-at., was fined 6d. and 4s. 61 costs for not sending his child regularly to school. LICENSING.—Peter Shaw was granted permission to sell until the next transfer day at the Shamrock Inn, Regent-st., and a similar privilege was given to James Hunt to sell at the Ship Inn, Heath-road. THE FORTHCOMING CYCLE PARADE.—Mr. Charles Dut- ton, proprietor of the Wilsoß's Hotel, Bridge-st., applied for an extension of time on the occasion of the forth- coming cycle parade at Runcorn. He stated in his appli- cation that the parade would be in aid of the Runcorn Nurse Fund and the Royal National Lifeboat Institution and would begin ot nine o'clock. He ask:,-.1 the Bench if they could allow him to keep his premises open from eleven to twelve o'clock in order that those persons tak- ing part in the parade, some of whom were coming from neighbouring towns; could be supplied with re- freshments before leaving Runcorn.—The applicltion w.as granted. • SQUABBLING NEIGHBOURS.—Ann Ashhurst, of Loch-st., summoned Elizabeth Northern, of the same street for assaulting her on the 6•,h inst.—The complainant alleged that.the defendant struck her and made use of very abusive language towards her, on the date mentioned. Defendaat stated that Ashhurst was always throwing out certain insinuations concerning her (defendant). On the occasion named she began to quarrel by making some remark which annoyed the defendant. She admitted pushing the complainant when provoked.—Fined 23. 61 and costs. _ . _ .</p>
        <p>_ A YOUTHFUL FOOTBALLER —John Ball WAS discharged, on payment of costs, for playing football in Cooper-st., on the 2nd September.—P.C. Butler supported the charge. saying that the defendant, with other lads, was kicking an old hat about the street.</p>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>SATurnmy.—B-e—for.e-1141-r". T. FT. Pearson, and Colonel J. L Wood.</p>
        <p>DRUNKEN-NESS -The following were siammoned for this offence: Thomas Higgins, Newton, 53. and costs ; Alexandra Reed, Newton 2s. 6d. and cossts ; Patrick C' Rourke, Flaydock, 53. and costs, Samuel Ball, Havdock, 2s. 6d. and costs ; Simuel Maleen, Golborne, 53. and costs'; Rchert Unsworth, Flaydock, 2s. 61. and cost • , John Gaffney, Hardock, 23. 61. and costs, Samuel Carnm, Haydock, 2s. U. and co3t3, Jo4eph Lynch, and William Carr, Flaydock 53. 61. and 23. 61. and costs respectively ; William Southern, Havdock, 21. 6,1. and costs, John Mawdesley, Haydock. 23. 61. and costs, Thomas Hazeldine, Ilaydork, 2 61. and costs ; John Atkinsnn, Golhorne. 53. and costs; Stephen Horrigan, Golhorne 23. 61. and costs. TEMPORARY AUTRORITY.-John Darhyshiro applied for temporary authority to sell in the Sir Charles Napi,r, Golhorno, in lieu of Thomas Edwards. The application was granted. Does AT LA.RGE.-For allowing their dogs ta be at large unrmizzled, Thomas Gall;gan, Newton, and James Bowcock, I,Vinwick, were each fined 73, 6,1. DRITNIC ON LICENSED PREMISES.-Rllard BattPr:bv was 'fined ss. and costs, for being drunk in the Ram's Head, Rayclock-, in the occupation of Thomas Harrison, on Octotier 691. P.S. BarTe proved the case. • BREAcn OF THE PEACH.-Allen Billidge was hound over to keep the peace for three months for fighting in Hay- dock on September 28th. DOG WITHOUT A LICENSE.-Richard Redfern was fined 10s. and costs for haying a dog without a linens?. Scnoot. CASE.-John Beckett, Golhorne, was fined 5. for negle,.ting to send his child regularly to snhool. Mr. Henry Pegg proved the case. ADULTERATION OF COFFEE.-John Howe, E arlestown , was charged with 'chine to the prejudiceof John bottom, on September 13. hot a pound of coffee. con- taining 60 per cent, of chicory. P.C. Mason and P.G. Carleton proved the ca.3e,the latter stating that defendant said after the purchase had been coMnleted, that he had not sold thm coffee as pur,. coffee. The coffee was sent to Mr. Browne, the county analyst, and h., certified as above.-The magistrates inflicted a fine of £3 and costs. ASSAULT ON A BOY.-John 'Holland was charged with assaulting and heating Nathan Taylor in Burtonwoori, on October 3rd. The defendant p!Qaded guilty, and Taylor who is but a youth said that he was met in Burtonwood hr def,ndant. who strnek him several times.- Defendant was fined ss. and costs, ALLEGED TRESPASSING IN PURSUIT OF GAMK-Nloc;os Sharpies, Wm.. Pernilehury and James Pandiebury were ea Isia charged with committing trespass and entri4ng in the day titne upon land, in the occupation of William Gilbert. at Hardock, in search of gam-. os o.tober 3,d, , WiPlout.lieen,e to do so from the owner of the land. MP.. Stuart defended.-Jimes Hall, keeper, said that on the above date he saw the three defendants walking alonir . the lane near Mr. Gilbert's land, accompanied ha' a do,. , After a time a hare ran acrnis the field, and the defend- ants sent the dog after it, they also followed behind it. The hare escaped, and, witness went to them, and after seeing who they wore, he issned summonses against them-Mr. Stuart said that his case was that there was no hare in the field at all, and if the animal was simply a rabbit, then the charge of treipAssing in search of game full through. His clients also denied that they sort the do, into the field. hut that they went after it to bring it out aaain. It was the duty of anyone to bring his do, out of the field if it was trying to catch a hire as the defendant had stated.-The case was dismissed.</p>
