        <p>)CTO</p>
        <p>aid in ousting the Brighton men,by sending the leathe into the visitors quarters. Rd vrards returned to touchr Both teams seemed now to be warming to their work and numerous exchanges were made. The combination play of the Runcornians soon earned them a try, and shortly after tne re-start another capital attempt was made to score in which at least one half the number of the home team took part, Richard- son being held on the line by Cannell and Jones. The visiting forwards then got away but were brought back by their opponents who again had a look in. flieir pas. sing was really grand ; not a bit of selfish play being seen. Abrams subsequently feigned to pass and got in under the bar, Warder missing the goal. Before half time arrived a third try was scored by the homesters, who were now in brilliant form. The visitors had nbt notched a point. Drinkwater restarted the game, and Runcorn at once began to press. After a few exchanges a scrimmage occurred from which Abrams took posses- sion, and putting in a grand run scored beautifully. The Brighton men seemed to be lacking in vigour, and the best of the play was with the homesters. Davies kicked a good goal subsequently, and the remainder of the game was of a rather one-sided nature. The final result was Runcorn one goal and five tries to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>RUGBY. LANCASHIRE CLUB CHAMPIONSHIP. FIRST CLASS. TARRING [ON v. ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>RUGBY. LANCASHIRE CLUB CHAMPIONSHIP. FIRST CLASS. TARRING [ON v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The Warrington first team journeyed to St. Helens on Saturday afternoon to meet tne premier club of that town in the Landashira Club Championship Competition. ['hey were accompanied by well on for a thousand of their supporters, a special train being run from Bank Quay. This is tile first season St. Helens have been in the first class competition' but they appear to be doing very well, being now the fourth on the list and having the same number of points as Warrington with a match to the good. They are also taking advantage of the increased interest which the championship matches arouse among St. Helens football enthusiasts, for they have doubled the charges for admission to the field. Toe new arrangemeat only came into force on Saturday, Sc. Helens no doubt thinking their supporters would glaclfy m nay double prices to see their old opponents from War- rington beaten. On the part of the Warrington specta- tors, however, there was considerable grumbling, and they will only rest satisfied when they see St. Helens • supporters charged a similar rate at Warrington. As • the increased charges for championship matches appear , to be general. Warrington will doubtless follow suit, • unless a sort of give-and-take arrangement can be made with the other clubs. St. Helens on Saturday were confident of defeating Warrington, and the result justi- fied their confidence, for they won by three tries to nothing. But the score by no means represents St. Helens' superiority over Warrington. Throughout the game the visitors had the best of the play,especially in the first half, but luck seemed decidedly against them. Had they had the good fortuae which seemed to favour the homesters, they would undoubtedly have piled up a bigscore against their opponents. In the first half, for at least twenty out of the forty minutes' play,St.Helens were hard pressed. Again and again did Warrington get the ball close to the St. Helens line but were unable to get over. In the second half they had exceptionally hard lines at the very outset. Following the kick off, Foulkes' return was charged,and it seemed certain that warringtan would score, but the ball went dead. The for wards played a splendid game, and if the backs I would pass more freely and accurately, better results I would doubtless follow. At full back Boscow kept up the previous Saturday'a form. 'me St. Helens are a capital lot of fellows. They worked hard from begin- ning to finish, and played with admirable combination. Their backs passed well, and it was to this that they owed thi ir victory. The teams were : Warrington : Full back, B )scow ; three-quarters, Cunningham, Lawless, Nevins. and Potts ; half-backs, James T. Bate and John T. Bate ; forwards, Turner, Jolley, Dakia,Taylor, Berry ,Buckley, Bailey,anci Thorni- ley. St Helens : Full back,Foullfes ; three-quarters.Sudlow, Appleton, Fairclough, and Doherty ; half backs, Cross and McLees ; forwards, Brownbill, Gladwin, Whiteley, Briers, Winstanley, Dale, Ashcroft, and Gill. The only alterations in the teams as selected VNT'.I9 tha Lawless took the plate of Heesom, who ha:l not re covered from the injury he received in the Oldham mach. The game was advertised to start at a quarter- past three, but it was nearly forty minutes past when the teams appeared on the field. There would be an attendance numberinz nearly 6,000. Cross, for St. Helens, kicked off, and Warrington failing to return, several scrimmages ensued in the Warrington quarters, but it was not long before tha visitors commenced a series of attacks on the home citadel which were main- tained more or less throughout the first half. Time after time. Warringten presse.i, but were unab e to get over. The defence of the homesters was capital, and though pay was frequently on their line they succeeded in pre- venting any score. In the second half the kick-off by Nevins was well followed up, the Warrington forwards preventing the return. They rushed the ball over the line, but unfortunately it went dead. St. Helens now played up, and with good results for in about five minutes Cross getting possession passed to Apple- ton who passed to Sudlow and that player got over at the corner. Cross mule a capital attempt to convert the point i.e.° a goal. Fortune seemed to favour St. Helens, for shortly afterwards, McLee, from a scrimmage near to the Warrington line, riot over, the p ace kick again failing, • the ball striking the uprietrit and rebounding into the field. St. Helens continued to press, Bose., w defending well. Sudlow ultitna'ely got over again, but was held by Cuntling,ham and was unable to ground the ball. Afterwards Doherty got possession, and after a grand run nut the ball almost behind the posts. Sudiow took the place kick, but the ball attain struck the post, this being the first time in fifteen kicks that Sadlow failed to place a goal. The (tame thus ended in a win for St. Helens by three tries to nil. WARRINGTON " A " v. ST. HELENS" A.."</p>
        <p>There Was a large at •rniance on the Wilderspool closure on Saturday afternoon to witness the engage- ment of these two teams. St. 11.1 ens kicked off towards the pavilion goal but the Warrington team by a series of good passing mavem mt; brought the ball into the visitors quarters. play remained here for some time a number of line-ou)s and scrimmages taking place. Several cf the St. Helens inen were bringing the hall away at their toes when Moores made a daring save. The ball was again gradually t tken near to the Helens goal line, Carey taking a prominent part, and from a scrimmage Savage cot over near to the touch-line. The goal kick was unsuccessful. 0n re-commencing again St. Helens came away with a rush and for some time they pressed• their oppormen's. H tmpson brought relief to his side by a good run. lateresting, play followod trstil half- time but nothumg furtoer was scored the half-time score being Warrington. one try, 7. Helens, nil. 0.1 the r,.- sumption of hostilities Ca.ey was conspicuous by a goad run and kick, but the visitors soon transferred plat- ts Warrington terrtory, and forced a minor paint. Play was not allowed to rem min here for long duration, for Carey accepting a p,s3, again carried the hall into the visitors' quarters. W.., accurate passing between the Warrington half backs and three-quarters, Moores gained possession and raced (war the line. scaring the second try for Warrington. Handles essayed at the goal kick hut failed to imoroye the score. On the drop out the vi4tors took the hilt to the Warrington end,hnt they shortly afterwarls lost the advantage by off-side play, Warrington tieing awarded a couple of free kicks in quick succession. Again did the Warrington backs show up wed by a splendid ex- hibition of pa,sing, and again was Maares cuccessful in scoring. 'I h' gl4l kick was this time taken by Savage hut it was a f ,ilur.. The remainder of the game"was in favour of Warrington, and Carey who had played exceedingly well throualmout the game, scored the fourth try for Warrington almost at the call ,tf time. He took the goal kick himself and was successful). The whistle then sounded with the snore stanling—Warring- ton, one goal,three tries ; St. Flelens.one minor. Teams : Warrington, full back, M amnion ; threequarcer hacks, Carey,Moores and Gibson; half- b mcks. Farster and Foderm ; forwards, Houghton, Burrows, 1-13,mile,t, Savage. Andrews, Fairhurst, Ii watt, and flampson. St. Helens, full ba-k, H. Mees ; threequarters, J. Ley- land, A. Sutton, G. Harper and J. Siincock ; half-backs, F. Little and .f. Thompson f ,rwards. F. Brown. S. Jones, J, Lloyd Davies, J. McK ty, J. Harmon, J. Patrick, J. Rennie and It. Little. WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. WIDNES "A."—Played on the Lowerhouse-lane enchsure on Saturday, hefora a fair number of spectators. The" A " team had the as- sistance ef Reece, an ex-Cheshire county rn--,n, and ilughQs of St. Helens. Swift started for the Smints,who having lost the toss had to face the wind, which blew from goal to goal. A bout of passing by the Saints' hacks resulted in Burton scoring after out-distancing, all opposition, running the length of the field. The Fame player took the place kick, but filled. From the drop-out the game open, d fast at centre, when a rush by the Saints' forwards forced Widnes to defend. Here Widnes were penalise,l fin- a breach of the off -side law. Burton took the place kick, and a good attempt was made, the ball going wile. A dodgy run by Warburton gave Toollev trouble, who, however, managed to clear. Vigorous efforts were made by Peters to get in, but. all were frustrated on the verge of scoring. The Saints were again penalised, through Ham peon being offside, hut the plac.e kick was b.dly utilised by ilighlield. The homesters dribbled finely to the line, hut Roughly cleared, , Forster returned, and O'ffren securing gave the Saints relief by a long • kick. F.-on a scrimm mge at centre O'Brien kicked acrots to Burton, who ran finely to the homesters' 25, where he was out in touch by Percival. Half-time arrived with the Stints leading by one try to nil. Most of the latter half of the game was a series of scrimmage 3, and when the whistle sounded the Saints were returned winn-rs of a very fast game by one goal one try to one try. RUNCORN V. NEW BRICIIITON.—These old Cheshire rivals met on Saturday afternoon at Rune-am nto try conclusions. Fine weather prevailed, and hostilities began before a large attendance of spectators. Faulkner set the ball in motion for Runcorn and Clwe responded by punting to touch After a few preliminaries, a good list of passing was put in by the visitors. Cannell gained possession and ran well into the homesters' territory: but Houghton was On the alert and collared Drink water as time latter was getting very dangerous. Warder rendered further</p>
