      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>ASSOC I AT I ON.</head>
        <p>ASSOC I AT I ON.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. OLD AIIPLEFORDIANS.—ThiS fixture was decided on the Cricket field on Saturday, before a poor attendance or spectators. Connor (who formerly' played for Warrineton) turned out with the Ampleford- lama' of which team he is the captain. Warrington kicked off, and tile visitors at once began to press. Linaker now passing away, and passin to T. Mather this player beat Morris with a good shot. Dukes secured and passed to Woodville, he to Carter and the visiting backs failing to clear the Warrington forwards rushed the ball through, the goal being disallowed owing to some informality. The game was now stopped for about 20 minutes owing to Woodville being injured. War- ringtol eventually started the game with ten men. The Atnplefordians now pressed the homesters, but could not break through the defence,and half-time was called with the score in favour of the homester3 by one goal to nil. Tilf visitors restarted, and some smart play by rton and Dukes gave Warrington the advantage. Connor took the ball back, shot, and Williamson failing to clear the ball was put through. Williamson soon afterwards with a shot from half-way beat Morris, and Warrington were once more ahead. It was by this time hard to dis- tinguish the players, owing to darkness, and time was called leaving Warringtoa the winners of an exciting gam3 by two goals to one. WARRINGTON Sr. MARY'S V. BASEBALL RESERVE.— Played on the ground of the former on Saturday and resulted iu a win for the homesters. St. Marys were six short of their usual team. Play throughout was of a very one-sided character, the home backs rarely letting the visitor 3 have a chance wlWe the home forwards were continually shooting at goal, J. Bannon (7), J. Hayes, J. Roddy, and A. Stirrup being the scorers. Final result : St. Marys, ten goals ; Warrington Baseball, nil. FLINT V. WARRINGTON Sr. ELPHINS.—These teams met at Flint on S tturday in the first round of the qualifying stage for the Englsin cup comp tition. There were 13ut few spent a-mrs. Play commenced briskly, chiefly on the vi-ltors' ground. Flint had the wind on their baeks,and scored corners. John Price gave a corner kick, and Bartley headed beautifully, gaining the first goal for Flint. Good play continued, and Flint scored another goal. Exciting play followed in front of the visitors' goal, and Bartley distinguised himself, Griffiths gaining the third goal for Flint just before half-time. Final re- sult 13 goals ; Warrington, one goal.</p>
        <p>3.1:073Y V. ALL - SAINTS' MISSION.—The above match was played on the ground of the former at Folly-lane on Saturday. Result : Bewsey, one goal ; All Saints', one goal. PENKETH SCHOOL V. OLD SCHOLARS.-1 dull after- noon saw the above teams face each other on Saturday. The Past had a strong team. Linney kicked off for the School at 3 20 p.m., and play was very fast and even for the fir,t twenty minutes. After half-an-hour's play Raynclds ,cored for the School, an appeal for off-side failing. The Old Boys at once ran down, the Charnock brothers on the left playing brillitntly, and Smith equal- ized a minute later. Before the interval this score was supplemented by a second for the old stagers. The second half consisted of vi.T.rous attacks on both goals, .but the Old Boys had the best of the play. Final score : Old Boys, five goals; Present, two goals. WARRINGTON RAMBLERS V. ST. MARY'S 2ND.—This match was played at Dalton Bank on Stturday. The home. team won the toss, and the visitors kicked off. Half-time result : Ramblers, two ; St. Mary's, nil. Final result : Ramblers, eight : St. Mary's, nil. LY:5131 G.S. v. WARRINGTON RESERVE.-011 the ground of the former on tturday. A very pleasant game ended in a draw of three goals each.</p>
        <p>WIDNES PARISH CHURCH V. WARRINGTON ST. ELPHIN'S RESERVE.-011 Saturday, at Widnes, under the auspices of the Warrington an District League 2nd Division. The home team won the toss, and Hughes ki,ked off against the wind. After having pl.' veil ton minutes the Parish left wing forced a corner off Cassidy, and Parr scored. Bruce, who had played a good game, had to lace the field, having twis'-ed his knee, but notwith- standing, this the Pari,h continued to press, and Hart soon afterwards scored. Half-'ime result : Parish, two goals ; St. E'phin's, nil. Parr restarted fur the Parish, and from midfiel.i play the visitors took up the running, and Hughes scored. Toe game ended in a draw of two goals each. RuNcotor RECREATION V. SOUTH LIVERPOIL.—On Sit- urdty afternoon a match between the above teams took place on the ground of the former. The game through- out was of a one-sided character and ended in an easy win for the homesters by three goals, four tries, and three miners to one minor.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN ASSOCIATION V. BARNTON ALMON.—The first of the fixtures between these clubs was placed off on Saturday afternoon at Runcorn. The Albion have so far been very staecessfill, and are at present at the top of the Warrington awl Distriot. Leagee with nine points to their credit, out of a possibls 10. Previous matches between these teams have always been nleasarit games and have resulted vietoriouslv far the Albion. The fol- lowing teams turned out on Saturday :—Runcorn : Hoteol ; full backs, W. Walker and W. Carter ; half- hacks, G. Moores, A. Richards (captain), G. C asson ; forwards, J. Lint;in. Jones, Pc.. S. Walker and C. Burrows. Birriten Albion : Gal, Rawers full backs, Bell and Arnelels ; hall-baeks. C. Hatton, Rose, Allen ; forwards, E. riateree, G. Canner, J. Capper, J. Goulding arid Acton. Girter winning the toss, Capper started, and there was soon a rush on the home goal which frially resulted in a goal kick. The homesters now pressed hard and Pve had exceedingly hard lines with a szood shot which flowers fitel well out. Rose giving Goulding and Acton pis- session, they raced away until pulled up by W. Walker, Tho Albion pressed hard and put in several good slints but failed to get the ball through. Later on a uni,tak.e bc Carter,wheaecirlentally headed the leather in the wrong direction, ahnest let Vie visitors in. The v'sitoes still attacket ant a. hut by W. Walker gave them a penalty kick which was entrusted to Rose. The latter however, snie the hall wide. From the goal kick the home forwards got along in fine style Jones cvm- tinily beating 11)w eel with a splendid oblique shot. The visitors played well to her. and passed prettilly, and from some nice play before goal E. Hatton headed through. Nothing more was recorded before the interval when the score stoeil (lee goal each. Pye restarted and Linton and Jones got along the right wing in good style hut Bell and Arnnld cleared. The Albion attacked and gained a corner which was unproductive. The game was now principally in mil-field; neither side having mach advantag,e, About ten minutes from the call of time, Goulding beat the home custodian by a low shot which he ought to have cleared easily. The home for- wards played up and endeavoured to equalize. C. Burrows having exceedingly hard lines from a clever kiek,theane waslialllcsimming the bar. The remaining portion nf the a greatly in favour of the homeaters, who tried hard to make the score even but failed and the game ended in a win for Barnton by two goals to one. The home forwards showe I a vast improvement and Richards and Moores figured prominently as half-backs. The full backs have often been seen to better advan- taee. LANCASHIRE ALLIANCE. EIt2I.I4vTONVN V. lIINDLEY.—A good gate assembled on the Mesnes ground on Saturday to witness the alliance fixture between Earlestown and Bindles', and the specta- tors were rewarded by an excellent contest, in which the home eleven proved victorious, adding another win to their unbeaten record this seasnn, flindley won the spin and choee to pley down Hill,with the wind blowing obliquely across the ground. Topping kicked off, and the hernestere at once paid a visit to the top goal, but they were repulsed, and the hill wended its way merrily down the field. Nnttall finally having a try at goal, but the ball went wide. From the kick out the visitors again atta-ked, and by eambined p'ay lenked like scor- ing, but Wilkinson saved. A penalty kick gave Earles- town an opening, hut the leather was worked out. The kick out was met, and a return visit made to the Hinclley citadel, but Brownlow fisted out, and Flindley breaking away made an erratic shot at gnat. Siddeley proved a tower strength at half haok, ,trui playing with cool indgment, fed the forwards capitally, a scrimmage in the Hindley gosl resulting in the corner. Conway cleverly placed the hall in the goal mouth, but it was headed on to the net. By a strong kick, Waring, enabled his forwards to past the home goal in danger, which Wil- kinson averted. Visiting the top g‘aal Lee waltzed round his opponents,and centreing nicely Topping failed to get up, and Waring cleared at the expense of a corner,which</p>
        <p>Conway cleverly placed, but without result. The borne goal was again the scene of warfare for a short time,but pressure being relieved, Jenkins got possession and after a clever run up the field shot in, Brownlow fisted out, and Conway meeting the ball shot it through amid cheers. After the kick off Earlestown pressed somewhat and a couple of corners fell to them in succession, but pressure was relieved and Forster and Carr became conspicuous, but they could not get past Sudworth,who played a cool and steady game. After some very even play the ball was worked up to the Hindley goal, and after a shot had been fisted out it was returned, and Conway put it between the posts a second time. The play now settled down a little and became of the give and take order,relieved by sundry rushes on the part of the Hindley forwards. Earlestown got another opening, and Hughes passing across the ball Was met by Conway who scored a third point by heading through. No further point was scored up to half-time. After chang- ing ends Earlestown at once assailed the Hindley goal but the back defence was too good, and they were sent to the right about. A pretty bit of play by Waltholt and Nuttall endangered the home goal, Peet saved, but the ball being returned Sudworth had to fist out. Hindley now pressed for a short time, the home team evidently satisfied with the score, taking matters easy, but they were careful to keep their goal intact. Visiting the Hindley goal a scrimmage ensued, and the custodian being on the ground the goal was at the mercy of the Earlestown players, but the ball was mulled and worked out. Each goal was afterwards visited in turn, but neither was in actual danger. Siddsley got hold and made a splendid run down, but the final shot was nego- tiated. Hindley taking advantage of some loose play got away, and shooting, Sudworth feebly fisted out and fell, and Wright taking advantage of this shot through. This aroused the home team and the kick-off WAS rushed down and in less than half a minute the ball was shot through by Lee. Nothing further of importance trans- pired and the game ended, Earlestown, four goals ; Flinciley,one. Toarns :—Earlestown : Goal, Sudworth ; backs, Pest and Wilkinson ; half backs, Clarke, Siddeley and Taylor; forwards,Lee and Conway, Tippinc.,,,Hughes and Jenkins. Hinclley : Goal, Brovvnlow ; backs, Kay. Waring ; h tlf backs, Thornilough, Bolton, Bold ; fcr- wards, Waltholt, Naftali, Wright, Forster and Clare. ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT AMATEUR LEAGUE,</p>
        <p>FIRST DlvisloN. Results up to Saturday last</p>
