      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL SANITARY</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL SANITARY</p>
        <p>AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Authority was held on Tuesday afternoon in the Workhouse, Dutton, Mr. John Mclntyre occupying the chair. The remaining gentlemen present were Messrs. H. E. Heywooi, J. Brandreth, John Weir, J. Stokes, S. Woodward, T. Lewis, J. Parkin- son, G. Priestner, C. T. Whitley, E. Bratherton, J. Ashton (clerk), G. Ashton (deputy-clerk), J. Far- rington (nuisance inspector), and Dr. Adams (medi- cal officer).</p>
        <p>ALLEGED DEFECTIVE DRAINAGE AT HELSBY. The Local Government Board wrote en the 2nd inst. eating that they had received a commimication from Mr. Fred rick Thornley, of Rake House, 11,Isbv, alleging that the drainage of the township (in which there were about 1,200 permanent residents) was in a very defective state, and in no way adaptA to the requirements of the place, the sewage being carried by open ditchos through grazing land to the Manchester Ship Canal. Adverting to a letter of February 15th,. and the report by the Inspector of Nuisances enclosed therewith, the Local Government Board were clearly of opinion that the cir- cumstances of the case required that the Authority should, without dela7,instruct an engineer of experience in the execution of schemes of sewerage and sewage disposal to prepare a suitable scheme for Heist-Ir.—Mr. Brandreth thought that only one point was worth noticing and that was with regard to the scouring out or the ditches. The inspector, in his opinion had look,--d after this well, and if he continued to look well after them all requirements would be met.—Mr. Farring- ton sail that the ditches were cleaned by the Authority's orders in February 1894 at the cost of £5 us. 6.1. He himself had supervised. the work, and at present the ditches were in a satisfactory state--Messrs. Stokes and Williams both remarked that it was impracticable to lay a pipe down to the Ship Canal.—After some discussion the following gentlemen were &amp;ected a committee to consider the subject :—Messrs. A, White, Joseph Ellatn3, F. Thorniey, Cr,slAncl, Taylor, A.. Greenw v, E Wondwarl, Brandreth, Horton, Stokes, Heywood, and Williams.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer of rept-Trod that during the fortnight a ease of tyhoid fever had occurred at lielsby, and remarked that the township had lon,g, been free from zymotie diseases. Several C39;-.3 of scarlet fever had occurred at Kine'sley, and others at IVestcn. The rases had been visited, and the usual instructions issued. In accordance with instructions received, he had visited and inspected certain thatched cottages situated on the Rock, Main-street, Frodsham, but he could not certify that the premises were unfit for human habitation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>WATER ANALYSES.</head>
        <p>WATER ANALYSES.</p>
        <p>The Inspector applied for and obtained an order to submit a sample of water from the pump and well to certain cottages situated at Moore for analysis.—lt was also decided that a sample of water from certain pre- mises at flelsby should also be submitted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>BUNCO EtN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>BUNCO EtN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon the fortnightly meeting of Guardians took place in the Workhouse, Dutton, under the presidency of Mr. John Mclntyre. There were also present Messrs. J. M. Williatns, S. Yar- wood, G. Priestnf3r, 11. E. Heywood, J. Stokes, J. M. Williams, John Weir, E. Aaron, S. Woodward, A. R. Norman, C. T. Whitley, E. Bratherton, J. Ashton, clerk, and G. Apliton, deputy clerk.</p>
        <p>THE DATE OF THE FORTHCOMING ELEcTioNg. Writing on the 11th inst. the Clerk to the Cheshire County Council, said that in view of a meeting of his Local Government Act Committee, to be held on the 19th inst. to take into consideration the orders recently issued by the Local Government Board under the Act, he should be obliged by their informing him whether they desired to submit to the Council any special reasons why Monday, December 17th, the date fixed by the Local Government Boar 3 upon which the elections should be held, should not be adhEred to, and if so, to be fun.- nished with particulars of such special reasons.—The Clerk was instructed to write to the Comity Council say- ity, that there was no reason to object to the election taking place on the date named.</p>
        <p>THE NEW VAGRANT WARD. The Board received an account from Mr. Percy Silcock, showing that the total cost for the erection of the female vagrant ward, at the workhouse, was £340 6s. 103.</p>
        <p>DESERTED CHILDREN. Mr. Norman said that the House Committee hid considered the subject of the deserted children in the Workhoose, and had decided to recommend that the Clerk should write to see if they could get any of the children .into the Mason's Orphanage, Bristol, and also to communicate with other Unions to see what rewards they offered for the apprehension of any parents who had deserted their children. Some of them thought if they increased the reward it would enable the Union to trace them.—Mr. Woodward remarked that the mens' side of the Workhouse was overcrowded, and that there were convalescent patients waiting for beds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>LUCKY DISCOVERY.</head>
        <p>A LUCKY DISCOVERY.</p>
        <p>Do you worry because you look so very pale, with lips almost white and feel as though nothing could re- store von to vigorous, happy life ? If so, take Cock- burn's Blood Tonic Pills and stop worrying. They make cheeks like roses and delicate ladies strong. Of all chemists in boxes at is., 2s. 6d., and 49. 6d., or per post 2d. extra direct from the makers, COCKBURN'S DRUG SroßEs, Howard-st., Glasgow</p>
      </div>
