      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>..ettero to tin (gOttor.</head>
        <p>..ettero to tin (gOttor.</p>
        <p>NEWTON CoSIMON RIGHTS. Sir,—Through the medium of your correspondence column kindly allow me to reply to Mr. Campbell re his letter of last week, in which he says: "One question proposed with all due gravity is the question of the Common lands." I will assure you, Mr. Campbell, the proposition is made in all seriousness. One reason for not sanctioning the plans for the erection of other houses on the Common was, that the new Council will be empowered to defend rights of common in their district and also to apply the rates towards such defence (see section 26, clause 2, of the new Local Government Act). Because wrOng has been done that is no reason why it should be repeated. The question of mineral rights has not been raised. I believe the lord of the Manor can legally claim them; in fact, I have been informed that the minerals have al- ready been taken from underneath the Common. The new Council take over all non-ecclesiastical chari- ties.,Will that enable them to contest the matter as free- holders in the case of Dean Trust ?" The object of the agitation, Mr. Campbell, is not to " gall thee to curse but to call upon the new Council to bless the public by maintaining their rights. Justice cannot be a curse. I cannot see that another parish, viz., Winwiek, can have prior right to common land in this parish,nor do I think they will claim any. Your next remark, Mr. Cam pbell, is the "most un- kindest cut of all." I will repeat it. 4, Which was the most assinine performance, to go to law without money or without knowledge ?" I think I have already shown you that under the new A.cr, the Council will have money (rates) "to go to law" ; as to knowledge, well, most information regarding commons is purely tradi- tional, but probably no more traditional than the claims of most lords of the manor. It cannot be denied that the place is known as Newton Common, presumably Newton Common lands, common to all. The exchange of lands, re Dean Trust, and its sanction by the legislature proves nothing only that no one was assinine enough "to go to law without money," had the land been enclosed for anumber of years. There is such a thing as statute of limitation. You will notice the agitation is not about enclosed lands but about unen- closed lands and roadside wastes. The clay from the pit was used for building the nine arches also for the workshops at the entrance to the old stone pit, but again this only proves the lack of " assinine " performers and would take too much space in the telling. Then the appointment of Common walkers proves some right, for they are appointed by the ratepayers. Surely this custom can been traced. As a means of ascertain- ing the quantity of common land in the district it has been suggested that the old rate books, (say 40 years ago,) should be examined to see which land was rated, the unrated land being common. In conclusion I would draw your attention to the Commons Act, 1876, also to "English Commons and Forests" by the Right Hon. G. Shaw-Lefevre, M.P., in wbich he says: "The result of the movement described in this work has been to reverse the idea of absolute ownership of lords of the manors in the waste lands of their districts, and to establish the principal that they concern the interests of the people of the district e'en more than of the lords of the manors and their com- moners." "All the remaining commons should be placed under the protection and management of local authorities ; " and "It has been well settled law for many years past that the public have the right of way over the roadside wastes, and that any obstruction erected on them is a nuisance for which the author may be indicted in the Criminal Court." Earlestown, Oct. /5, 1894.</p>
        <p>ELECTOR.</p>
        <p>DR. OLIVER WENDELL HOLMES. have seen with considerable satisfaction the many special tributes of respect and admiration paid by the press universally to the genius and many excellencies of that great and good man Oliver Wendell Holmes, who has just gone to his rest. But in onl: one paper,the " Inquirer," have I seen any allusion to the fact ",-iat like his great contemporaries, Emerson, Russell, Lowell, Longfellow, Whittier, Ace., all of whom stood high in the estimation of good men everywhere, he was up to lis death in active sympathy with the bard of Christian churches known as Unitarian. Dr. Rverett Hale, a venerable Unitarian minister, officiated at his funeral obsequies, and in many churches on Sunday last, con- gregations joined in singing some of his soul inspir- ing hymns. Now, if the Words of the Master have any meaning. that " a tree is known by its fruits," surely a faith which could train and satisfy such men as these, might be spared the petty attacks from pulpits in this town, where they can not be replied to—by men who are evidently unable impartially to estimate the value of a faith they would depreciate.—Youm Sce., UNITY. Warrington, October 18, 1894.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>LYiIM.</head>
        <p>LYiIM.</p>
        <p>THE CONGREGATIONAL CHURCll.—Anniversary services were held at the Congregational Church or. Sunday. In the morning the Rev. Mr. Maconochie, of Manchester, preached. In the afternoon a children's service was held, when Dr. Goodrich gave an address and in the evening the Rev. Dr. Goodrich preached from the words "Ye are the light of the world." The choir, which was under the conductorship of Mr. Astley, sang a special anthem, "0 worship the Lord." Mr. Leather presided at the organ. There was a large congregation at each service, and the collections were in aid of the Sunday school funds.—On Tuesday evening, under the auspices of the Lymm Congregational Church Fellowship Associa- tion, the Rev. Carey Bonner delivered a lecture on "Longfellow." FUNERAL OF MISS HENSIIALL.—The remains of Miss M. Henshall, who died on Sat i.7,day morning, were in- terred in Lymm Churchyard on Tuesday afternoon. The deceased was the eldest daughter of Mr. Henshall, chemist, the Post Office, Lymm. Mr. Ardern, of Lymm, was the undertaker, and the Rev. J. Guest,vicar read the burial service. Wreaths were received from Mr. and Mrs. Lister, Mr. and Mrs. Lyttleton Dewhurst, Mr. and Mrs. Alfred Swanwick, Mr. and Mrs. Oddie, Mr. H. Ferris, Mr. and Mrs. F. Brazendale, Mr. and Mrs. Ardern, Mr. and Mrs. Ridgway, Mr. and Mrs. Swallow, Mr.7and Mrs. Gregson, Mr. and Mrs. Swarby, Captain Douglas-Lane, Mr. and Mrs. Martin, High Legh; and Mr. Knight, L )ridon, sent a heart composed of violets. There were about 20 other wreaths from friends ^ and relatives of the deceased's family. Many of the tradespeople of Lymm walked in procession to the church.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO A LONDON AND NORTH-WESTEN</p>
        <p>EMPLOYE. The members of the London and North-Western Railway Telegraph Department met at the Bridge Inn, Warringten, the object being to present an old friend and comrade with a handsome travelling bag as a mark of their esteem. The recipient of the gift was Mr. Samuel Davies, of Newton-le-Willows, who for the past 15 years has served in the War- rington district, but has recently been transferred by the department to Crewe. In the absence of Inspector Jones, sir. Tuson, foreman, was elected to the chair, and made a few appropriate remarks. A note of apology was read from Inspector Jones for his inability to attend. The Chairman then called upon Mr. R. Chester (being the oldest lines- man present) to make the presentation which he accordingly did. Mr. Davies, on rising, said it gave him great pleasure to accept that handsome present. He could hardly find words in which adequately to express his feelings. They might be sure he should always remember and never regret the 15 years spent in the 'Warrington Division. Song 3 and reci- tations were afterwards rendered by the various members of the staff, and after the usual votes of thanks to the Chairman and host the meeting ter- minated.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL. The League matches which Warrington have engaged in are exhibiting plenty of variety, first a victory,then a defeat, and ditto again. The matches have been equally divided between" home" and "away," and in each case the home team have won. As a result of the match with St. Helens, Warrington have had to "climb down one " in the League ladder, and are now the fifth on the list.</p>
        <p>The result of the St. Helens match does not fairly rep- resent the game at all. It was alniost entitely due to the lack of scoring powers that the game was lost. During the first half especially, the St. Helens men were kept in their own quarters for the greater portion of the time, and they had a very anxious time of it. Even in the second half when all three tries were scored, St. Helens had very little the best of it with the exception of superior scoring powers. The Warrington team had hard lines indeed in the match, for time after time, they missed scoring by the narrowest chance.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens team had looked upon the match as a certainty, but they nevertheless had to fight their hard- est to pull the match out of the fire. Boscow was at his best, and gave stronger proof than ever that his relapse last year was only temporary. The threequarter line was again weakened by the continued absence of Heesom who has not yet recovered from the injuries received at Oldham. Lawless who filled his place did not do badly, but still the threequarter line was not up to the mark. The halves played their usual game, while the forwards played exceedingly well, and were more than a match for their opponents. The Warrington forwards are in fact the backbone of the team at present.</p>
        <p>To-day the all-conquering Wigan team have to be faced, and on their own territory too. This team has at present the best record of any club in the League, for while not scoring quite at the same rate as Oldham,they have not had a single point scored against them. War- rington will therefore have to fight with grim e3rnest when they tackle the team. The result of the corres- ponding match last year was a goal and a try to nil. Let us hope Warrington will have better luck to-day.</p>
        <p>The following is the team selected to do battle with the Wiganers : Full back, Boscow ; three-quarter backs, Potts, Cunningham. Hampson and Burton; half-backs, James T. Bate and Ciark, forwards, Fair Turner, Jolley, lhorniley, Nevins, Buckley, Bailey, Taylor and Dakin.</p>
        <p>The following is the result of the League competition up to date. For. Agst. Pld. Won. Lost. Drn. G. T. G. T. Pts Wigan 3 ... 3 ... 0 ... 0 ... 6 5 ... 0 0 ... 6 Oldham Tyldesley St. Helens 3 2 1 0 3 6 ... 2 1 ... 4 Warriagton Salford Broughton Rangers 4 ... 1 ... 3 ... 0 ... 3 2 ... 6 6 ... 2 Swinton 4 0 3 1 1 2 ... 5 5 ... 1 Rochdale Hornets *Leigh * Leigh have been suspended till the end of November for professionalism.</p>
        <p>Salford have now been ordered to keep Leigh com- pany, and they have to play no more matches until the new year comes in. They have also been punished by being expelled from the League, so that they have been dealt with more severely than Leigh. This is because the came was much more flagrant. The guilt was in fact not denied, and the officials cf the club no doubt hoped thus to encourage other clubs to do the same, and thus bring ia recognised professionalism. Now that they have been suspended they have " told the teacher" of other clubs whom they say have done the same.</p>
        <p>There are no less than five clubs implicated, viz., .Wigam. Swinton, Rochdale Hornets, Broughton Rangers, and Tyldesley, so that if all these are suspended it will mean the break up of the League competition, unless professionalism is adopted. Whether this will be the case is another question, for the professional clubs will then be out of the pale of the English Rugby Union. Their example will be contagious however, and once set agoing it will be difficult to say when they will stop. It is a very singular thing that neither the suspension of Leigh nor Salford affect the Warrington fixture list, for whereas Warrington is the first club to antagonise Leigh after the period of their suspension,they will also be the first League Club to do likewise to Salford after their suspension terminates.</p>
        <p>Victory again crowned the efforts of the Warrington "A " team on Saturday afternoon, when they met the " A " team from St. Helens on the Wilderspool enclosure. To win by fourteen points to nothing is no mean per- formance, and if the team can show the same combina- tion as they exhibited on Saturday they will stand a good chance of ending the season near the head of the "A " team championship competition. Mannion at full back had not much work to do but what he did was done well. The three-quarter backs played unselfishly. Their excellent passing accounted in no small measure for the I victory gained. Carey who had been transferred from the first team played with more dash and confidence than te has done for some time past. Foden and Fors- ter were two good half-backs, working together splen- didly, but unfortunat.ly this latter player had his face, disfigured early in the match. The forwards ot both teams appeared t,4 be evenly matched, but in the back division, the Warrington team was superior. The "A team are to play "at home" again to-day, when their opponents will be the Wigan "A " team, and there will no doubt be a struggle for supremacy.</p>
        <p>Cur local junior teams have been at it again, and on Saturday St. Mary's inflicted a defeat to Widnes " A" at Widnes by a goal and a try to a try, notwithstanding that they had to play against an ex-Cheshire county player, and also a crack St. Helens player.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath scored similar points to St. Mary's over Wigan St. Clement's, while they would not let the Wiganers have even a solitary try. Webster was unable to play, but an efficient substitute was found in the person of Isherwood.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Board was held in the Board room of the Workhouse on Wed- nesday morning. Mr. W. Houghton presided, and there were also present the Rev. G. F. Roberts, Msssrs. G. Pilling, R. Heaton, J. Hill, W. Gill, J. Houghton, A. J. Jolley,Mather, Dr. Patterson, Fair- dough, G. Ratcliffe, E. Aukland, A. Jolley, W. Bal- harry, W. J. Shaw, 'rurton, J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (deputy clerk), C. H. Ward (master), H. M. Mines (superintendent).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>GIFTS ACKNOWLEDGED.</head>
        <p>GIFTS ACKNOWLEDGED.</p>
        <p>'The Master in his re;ort acknowledged the following gifts : periodicals for the inmates from Mr. Frank J. White, Church-street; flowers for the wards from Rev. B. B. Prescott, Christ Church, Latchford ; flowers and fruit from Mrs. Broadbent, the Hales, Latchford ; two dozen dumbbells for the school children in Workhouse from Councillor Shaw. MASTER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The vagrants relieved during the week were,tren Z 65, women, 38, children, 22. Numb9r of inmates in the house was 344, corresponding, week last year 334.</p>
        <p>WELCOME TO A VISITOR.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN in the name of the Board extended a hearty welc,me to Mr. Hough, the chairman of the Altrincham Union, who was present at the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>APPLICATIONS FOR INCREASE OF SALARIES.</head>
        <p>APPLICATIONS FOR INCREASE OF SALARIES.</p>
        <p>At a Meeting of the Flou'e Committee on the previous Friday, several applications for increase of salaries were received, and the Master was instructed to call a speCial meeting by circular to consider the matter.</p>
        <p>TFIE DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The CLERK announced that he would be the returning officer at the forthcoming district Council elections, and would also appoint assistant retutning officers. The ASSISTANT CLERK said that November 24th would be the last day for the publication of notice of parish meetings. On December 4th, all parish meetings must be held, not earlier than six o'clock in the evening. For the election of Parish Councillors, the time had to be taken between December 15'h and December 19th. arid December 17th was suagested as a possible date. For the District Council and Board of Guardians December sth was the last day to send in nominations to the re- turning officer, and the elections must take place be- tween Saturday,December 15th and Wednesday. Decetn- ner 19th. The elections would probably take place on December 17th.</p>
        <p>APPLICATION FOR INCREASE OF SALARY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frank H. Aukland made an application for in- crease of salary. In reply to questions, the CLERK said that Mr. Auk- land received £95 per annum besides vaccination fees, and he had performed his duties very well although he came as a young man unexperienced in poor law admin- istration. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. G. PILLING ;aid that he would give notice that at the next meeting he would propose that Mr. Aukland have an increase of his salary to £lO5.</p>
        <p>Sir Andrew Clark, preside n` of the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons, has advised that each mouth- ful of food should receive thirty-two hites—that is, one for every tooth—if one wishes to avoid dyspepsia.</p>
        <p>BOARD OF TRADE INQUIRY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>THE FATAL EXPLOSION ON A BARGE AT</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Town Hall on Wednesday, Mr. Howard Smith, barrister-at-law, and Mr. Alexander Gray, consulting engineer, commissioners appointed by the Board of Trade, held an inquiry in respect of the boiler explosion on board the Ship Canal fie, Amy, which occurred at Atherton's Quay, Warring- ton on the 18th September. As a result of the explosion, Ralph Lowe, of Leinster-street, Run- corn, the engineer of the vessel, was scalded to death.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gough conducted the case on behalf of the Board of Trade, and Mr. Collingwood Hope, barris- ter, of Liverpool, represented Mr. Richard Abel, boat builder and carrier, of Runcorn, the owner of the barge. Mr. Walter Dean, of Manchester,watched the proceedings on behalf of the relatives of the deceased, and Mr. C. H. Hilton, of Liverpool, appeared for Mr. W. Marsh, engineer, of Widnes. Inspector Spinks, of Warrington, watched the pro- ceedings on behalf of the county coroner (Mr. S. Brighouse). Mr. Gough, in opening the proceedings, said the boiler which was the subject of the present investigation was constructed sixteen years ago. The person who con- strected it had been dead some time, but it had been examined since the explosion by Mr. Shott, engineer to the Board of Trade, who would prove the details of its construction. The boiler was made by Mr. Chappell, of Warrington, and was tested by hydraulic pressure 220 lbs. to the square inch. It was fitted on board the yacht Cuckoo,and worked at a pressure:of from 751bs. to 80Ibs. per square inch. About thirteen years ago the yacht broke from her moorines, went over a weir, filled, and remained under water in the Mersey for some time. Subsequently the yacht was raised, and the boiler taken out and removed to a yard at Warrington, where it re- mained for a long time. Six years ago it was purchased by Mr. Boscow, and fitted on board the yacht Beacons- field ,and was tested at 120Ibs. pressure. It was worked at about 651bs. to 701bs. per square inch. Three and a half years ago the boiler was retubed. In 1892, Mr. Boscow died, and the boiler was subsequently re- moved from the Beaconsfield to the flat Amy, which had been purchased from Mr. Abel and fitted up with engines and propeller. Thereafter the Aniy was used as a steam barge. Mr.Boscow's widow was the proprietress. At the end of 1893,Mrs.Boscow difd,and the Amy was resold by the son to Mr. Abel early in the present year. Shortly after obtaining the barge, Mr. Abel found that the boiler tubes leaked, and he called in Mr. Joseph Senior, boiler-maker, to see if the boiler was worth retubing. Senior informed Mr. Abel that if the boiler was retubed it would be as good as ever, or used words to that effect. The boiler was accordingly retubed. and Mr. Marsh,fore- man fitter to the Ship Canal Company, lent a pump, by which the boiler was tested to 1001bs. pressure per square inch. After this the owner applied to Mr. Marsh for the loan of a hydraulic pump which was granted. The boiler was tested to the extent of 2001bs per square inch. Within a few days after that Mr. Marsh, Mr. Senior,and others proceeded up the Manchester Ship Canal in the lighter, after which Mr. Abel stated that he consulted Mr. Marsh, as to the working pressure of the boiler.and he was advised that it was fit to work at some 601b5 pressure. Mr. Marsh further adjusted the safety valve for him. Mr. Marsh however would state that he never examined the boiler. About this time the boiler was supplied with a new steam gauge. On September 18th last the Amy was at Warrington. About 11 o'clock in the morning the engineer filled the boiler with some twelve or thirteen buckets of water. The fire was lighted and steam got up to about 501bs. At about 1.30 Ralph Lowe, the engineer, was in the engine-room when the boiler explodel. The place was filled with steam and he was severely scalded. He was removed to the Infirmary but died within a few minutes of his admission. As they were already aware the Coroner adjourned the conclusion of the inquest until after this investigation. The boiler had since been examined by Mr. Shott, engi- neer, of the Board of Trade, who found the plate of the combustion chamber excessively corroded; the heads were eaten away, and the central stay covered with a patch. James Shaw wire drawer ; Alfred Gibson, fitter; and Edwin Boscow all gave evidence concerning the history of the boiler, which confirmed on the whole Mr. Gough's statement.</p>
        <p>Mr. Richard Abel, carrier, of Runcorn, deposed that the Amy was the only steam barge he had. He had not received any mechanical training. Two or three weeks after re-purchasing the boiler, he had it overhauled and cleaned. Mr. Senior when called in to look at the boiler told him that it was a very good one and only wanted re-tubing. He further said that it would work at a pressure of 60 to 70 lbs. The boiler was then retubed by Mr. Senior, and subsequently tested by Mr. William Marsh who told him afterwards that the boiler was fit to work safely at a pressure of 65lbs. Witness's brother and Mr. Marsh went no the Ship Canal with the boat and the last-named man told witness that he put the safety valve working at 651b5., at which it was fit to work. Neither the boiler nor the fiat were insured, He never asked for this to be done. Be was not present , at the explosion and did not employ Lowe as the engineer. He was employed by the captain.—By Mr. Dean : He had nothing to do with the engagement of the deceased. —Questioned by Senior witness adhered to his former statement and denied that Senior had told him that the boiler would be safe to work only at from 45 to 60Ibs.— By Hr. Hope : After the test had been made with the hydraulic pump he (witness) was told by Mr. Senior and Mr. Marsh that the test was up to 100 lbs. pressure per square inch. He could not exactly say if Mr. Marsh had examined the boiler but fully understood that he must have done so before giving such instructions.—The Chairman : Was any suggestion made at the inquest that there was neglect on the part of the engineer.—In reply Mr. Hilton said that a suggestion was made that the explosion was due to want of water in the boiler.—The Chairman re- marked that that was the common suggestion and migh have been the case in the present instance.—Mr. Abel ret called, stated that he had made a statement to that effect because of the report he received from the captain who said that the boiler was short of water, and Lowe had been putting some in. Joseph Senior, boiler maker, 52 years of age deposed that he had never had any experience in the practical examination of boilers. Having received instructions trom Mr. Abel to examine the boiler on the Amy he went and sounded it with a hammer,sounding the bottom part of the combustion chamber first. He did not examine any other part with the hammer. Fle-did not think he inspected the sides of the chamber. On examining the stays lie found one looking deficient about the bottom of the centre row. He put a new one in here with nuts The rivets all looked sound. A little c@rrosion was present, but where this existed it was uniform. He did not examine the shell because he could not get at the bottom of it. He saw a patch but did not take it off. He did not know why it had been placed there, but thought it might have been put on to stop leakage. He could not have made an examination of the stay without taking off the patch. The heads of the rivets in the other two stays were very much corroded. lie ceu'd not say that he had made a satisfactory exam-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY OCTOBER 20, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY OCTOBER 20, 1894.</p>
        <p>ination of the boiler. That was not the question, as he was told to see what repairs were required. All the rivets, excepting about six. were nearly worn out. Sub- sequently he reported to Mr. Abel that the boiler was ' worth retubing and wanted a stay at the back end of the combustion chamber. He denied having told Mr. Abel that the boiler was in a good condition; but he did state that he thought it would wear another set of tubes. When the work was completed, and the test made, he told Mr. Abel it would work from 45 to 501bs. pres- sure. Tke engineer had told witness that the boiler had worked up to 80Ibs. pressure. Witness had no idea at what pressure the boiler would burst. He calcu- lated the pressure on the pump to be equal to 751bs. of steam and thus allowed about 45Ibs. The plate had corroded in the vicinity of the stay. When it was com- pleted it was tested by David Morgan up to 100Ibs., and the boiler was quite tight. He saw no defects whatever, and made a report to the effect that everything was satisfactory. He told the owner both before and after the test that it was safe to work from 45 to 501bs. ; and he also told the driver not to work it at more than 50Ibs. He did not think it would explode under 701b5., and in his opinion it would bear more.—By the Chairman : Witness thought it would go off t Nibs. He did not know what the factor of safety was.—By Mr. Hope: This was the first boiler he had examined. He did not hire himself out to examine. He was unaware that he was disqualified, and incompetent to examine the boiler. He had more " slates "on than to think that.—(Laughter.)—He was quite capable to ' examine the boiler in his opinion. He only sounded the weakest parts presuming that the rest were alright. He looked at the age of the plates. He told the coroner that the boiler appeared to him to be an uncommonly well-made one—one of the old-fashioned sort. He (wit- ness) was still of the same opinion. Since the explosion he had seen the boiler and thought the cause was its being short of water. He arrived at this conclusion from the absence of soot at the back end. He thought that black heat had caused the explosion. David Morgan, fitter, employed by the Manchester Ship Canal stated that he last saw the boiler alluded to in April when he took a hydraulic pump aboard. He put on 1001bs. pressure. He did not make any examina- tion of the boiler either before or after the test, and he was not aware that Mr. Marsh did. Subsequently the barge went up the canal under steam to the pressure of 1001bs. The boiler on that occasion lifted very hard. Witness thought that the explosion was due to the scarcity of water for the tube plate seemed to have been penetrated with heat. When the test was made, Joseph Senior, the engineer, and himself were present. The pressure of 1001bs. was withstood for ten minutes. Mr. Wm. Marsh, employed recently by the Manchester Ship Canal Company as ioreman fitter spoke to superin- tending certain repairs to the engine. He did not report to Mr. Abel more than that the engine was working well under 601b5. pressure. He knew nothing about the boiler, and had no reason to suspect that the pressure was a very high one.</p>
        <p>Thomas Gibbons: master of the "Amy" spoke to employing Lowe as engineer. Deceased had been brought up among machinery all his life. Witness had no testimonial with Lowe. On the 18th September witness saw the gauge two or three minutes before the explosion. It then registered a pressure of 501b5.—8y Mr. Hope: Lowe had worked on steam cranes before.— By Mr. Dean: He was not authorised by Mr. Abel to employ Lowe. Witness himself engaged Lowe.—Reply- ing to Mr. Hilton, witness said he had seen the engines worked at a pressure of 80Ibs. before the repairs were made.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Shott, engineer to the Board of Trade, described in detail the construction of the boiler which was in a fairly good condition when he examined it after the explosion. The cause of the explosion, in his opinion, was that the central stay in the combustion chamber had been covered with a small patch. The stay must have been corroded away before the patch was put on, and it must have been held slightly by a few threads. The heads of the stays were corroded more or less, and it would not have been difficult to have detected corrosion if examined by a proper pergola. A thorough inspection could not have been made without removing the catch, and if this had been done, and the plate had been seen, it would have aroused suspicion. There was not the slightest evidence of fire heat.— Answering Mr. Gray witness said he would not have put on any useful pressure if the central stay was out. The stay would practically come out if calculated at 9 or 10Ibs.</p>
        <p>This concluded the evidence, and Mr. Gough then drew attention to points which, he said, arose out of the evidence.</p>
        <p>Both Mr. Hilton and Mr. Dean said they were willing to leave the !Natter in the hands of the court.</p>
        <p>The Chairman then asked Mr. Senior if he was to blame for the explosion. One of the reasons why they suggested that he was arose out of the question whether he properly and thoroughly examined the boiler. He informed Senior that he was now at liberty to address the court in his own interests. Mr. Senior replied that he did not properly and thoroughly examine the boiler. He did not wish to address the court further.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hope in an exhaustive address on behalf of Mr. Abel said that his client was justified in his actions con- sidering the advice which had been tendered to him.</p>
        <p>The .Chairman remarked that Mr. Senior had insisted that the pressure he gave to Mr. Abel was only 451b5., but the court must consider the owner's statement, con- firmed by Mr. Marsh.</p>
        <p>After further discussion the inquiry ended, and the Chairman and his colleagues consulted in private for about fifty minutes. On returning The Chairman, before delivering his judgment, re- viewed the circumstances of the case at great length, after which he said that the court was of the opinion that Senior was competent enough to have detected whether corrosion existed, and to have ascertained whether the plates had deteriorated and were too thin or in a proper condition. Continuing, he said that the explosion was caused by the rivet end of the central screw stay and the back plate of the fire box having become corroded away, and the Place immediately around it having been reduced to such an extent that it would not withstand the working pressure. Mr. Shott had examined the boiler after the explosion and found it generally in a good condition but considering the plate, it was not fit to be worked at more than 9 or 10 lbs. pressure. The court had arrived at the same conclu- sion. They had no doubt that Mr. Joseph Senior was very much to blame for the explosion for he had knowledge and experience to enable him to make a proper examination of the boiler. He must have seriously neglected his duty in not removing the patch. Mr. Abel thought him efficient for the work required. In view of the long practice and experience which Mr. Senior had had, the court thought that he was quite capable of making a proper examination and report. They were, however, of the opinion that he was incapable of determining the working pressure inasmuch as he did no know what the factor of safety was. He had made a very superficial and perfunctory examina- tion. He had not done what he ought to have done. namely, remove the patch in order to make a thorough examination. The explosion would never have hap- pened it he had thoroughly examined the boiler. As to the question of Mr. Abel being justified in employing Senior and acting on his report, the Court were of the opinion that he took proper measures. They thought, however, that it would have been better if he had made more enquiries as to Senior's capabilities, seeing that he was only a journeyman boiler-maker. Mr. Abel, not</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>having had any experience with steam machinery,might have thought that a person in Mr. Senior's position was fit to do the woek. Under the circumstances the Court were not prepared to say that Mr. Abel was not justified in giving the work to Senior, and in acting upon his report. They thought, however, that he should have relied upon his opinion as to the working pressure of the boiler. They were also of the opinion that Mr. Marsh was not to blame. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Gough thought that Mr. Senior should be called upon to pay a p®rtion of the costs of the inquiry. Mr. Senior in reply to the Chairman, said he was wil- ling to pay as much as he could, but that was not very much.</p>
        <p>The Chairman remarked that they wished to mark the sense of the court as to the conduct of Mr. S-nior. He should like to order him to pay £6O which was half the costs of the inquiry, but owing to his humble position he would order him only to pay £lO. It must be under- stood, however, that the sum had been made 30 smal because of his humble position. THE INQUEST. Mr. S. Brighouse held the adjourned inquest upon the deceased yesterday, at the parade room of the police station. Mr. Abel was represented by Mr. Fowler. Mr. Brighouse, in opening the proceedings, said that the inquest had been adjourned to await the report of the Board of Trade inquiry. That inquiry had been made, and Inspector Spinks kad attended on his behalf. The result of the inquiry was that Mr. Abel and Mr. Marsh had been exonerated from blame, but they had found that Mr. Senior was very much to blame for the explosion in not examining the boiler properly. The evidence given by Mr. Senior at the last sitting of , , the inquest, and also at the inquiry at Runcorn was then ' read. After the evidence had been read, Mr. Senior was asked by the Coroner whether he had anything to say in addition to the jury. In reply he said that he could not add much to his statement. No doubt the Board of Trade engineer had more theoretical experience than he,but he could not admit himself to be in error. Inspector Spinks was called to give evidence to the effect that the report of Mr. Shott, the Board of Trade Inspector's evidence was correct. Mr. Brighouse said that it seemed to him that they had heard as much evidence on the last occasion as was possible, and the jury was practical enough to make an inspection of the place where the accident occurred. They, of course, had not the benefit of the inspection of the boiler as the Board of Trade had had. When au explosion of a boiler occurred it was very proper that an exhaustive inquiry should have been made,followed by a Board of Trade inquiry. Nobody could complain that there had been a superficial inquiry on the present occasion. His (the C)roner's) opinion was that as Mr. Abel had called in % scientific man, he was not to blame in the matter. If a man gave a wrong opinion,he and not the man who called it was to blame in the matter. After twenty minutes consultation Mr. Birtles the foreman of the jury said that the jury had gone carefully into the matter and the following was their decision : "The jury are of opinion that Richard Abel was to blame in not making more inquiries as to the capa- bilities of Joseph Senior, and that Mr. Senior was to blame for not making a better. inspection and more de- tailed report to Mr. Abel as to the state of the boiler." They considered that the neglio•ence was blameable but not culpable, and they returnecra verdict of " Accidental death."</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse said that he thought the jury ought to be excused up to the end of the year from attending any more inquests, unless they desired to do so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>GOOD SLEEP OF A BAD MAN.</head>
        <p>THE GOOD SLEEP OF A BAD MAN.</p>
        <p>In a certain prison that we all have heard of lay a convict upon his narrow iron cot. He was to be hanged the next morning. Yet he lay there,covered by a rough blanket, sleeping as quietly and soundly as a tired schoolboy. Occasionally the guard in the passage out- side peered between the bars of the cell, only to find his charge breathing deeply and regularly. This man had violated the law prohibiting murder; yet he had not violated the physical laws governing his own body, and Nature rewarded him as if he had been the noblest of his race.</p>
        <p>That same night, less than a mile away, a rich man tossed and tumbled upon his luxurious bed. He was a good and useful member of society, yet he could not sleep. And, worse still, this happened to him every night. Sleep—that blessing which the Psalmist says, God giveth his beloved," was practically a stranger to this man. What ailed him ? The tortures of conscience? Want of money? The fear of enemies? Nothing of the sort. Then why didn't he sleep as well as the murderer You would like to know? Right, let us look into the matter.</p>
        <p>I got no sleep at night ; I would lie for hours tossing about. In the morning I was worse tired than when I went to bed."</p>
        <p>Thus writes Mrs. Eliza Mathews, of 1, North-road, Burnt Oak, Edgware, near London, under date ot Sept. 22nd, 1892. Just two years before this time she lost her health. A foul taste in the mouth, loss of appetite,and great distress after eating were among the first things she complained of. She craved food at times, and fancied she could eat heartily, yet when the very dishes sne had asked far were set before her she turned from them as though they were filth from the gutter. Her skin grew sallow, her eyes yellow, and she had a con- stant pain at her chest, sideo,and between her shoulders. Her bowels were constipated, and the least exertion set her heart thumping as if it utast jump up into her mouth. At such times it was as much as ever that she could do to get her breath. She got so thin and weak she was no good for work. She couldn't walk out-doors without stopping to rest every few rods almost. The doctor did what he could for her, all any doctor could do. At first he said he thought her illness was owing to the smell of the farm-yard. This looked possi- ble. Even the smell of violets has made strong men turn pale and faint dead away. Yet the doctor was wrong. If he had been right,she would have get better when the family left the farm at Bentley priory and went to live at Burnt Oak. But she was not improved by the change of air ; she grew worse and worse. " In May, 1887," says Mrs. Mathews, " I went over to Chelmsford to visit my aunt, Mrs. Throoghton. She told me of the good, Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup had done her when she had indigestion and dyspepsia. She bought me a bottle, and I began taking it. After a few doses I felt relief. I kept on taking it, and in two months I was strong and well as ever. My husband and friends were astonished, yet I assured them that Seigel's Syrup had done it. Yours truly, (Signed) Emma MATHEWS."</p>
        <p>The point is plain enough. The convict slept soundly because he was a healthy man, although he was a wicked one. Our rich friend rolled about all night because his nerves were unstrung by the state of his stomach. Our correspondent was prostrated by the same thing—indi- gestion and dyspepsia. The remedy named cured her because it has that power. The reason remains a secret with the roots and herbs from which it is made. Yet so long as it drives away disease and gives us back our health and strength,who cares for its mystery? Results not arguments, are what we all want. "Burnt Oak House, Edgware, September, 22nd, 1892. I have known Mrs. Mathews for some seven years, and remember her long and lingering illness. She informs we that Mother .Seigel's Curative Syrup cured her,after medical and other means failed. Mrs. Mathews is a lady of respectability,and her word can be implicitly relied upon. You can use this statement in any way you may think proper. Yours truly, (Signed) T. H. Housn, Grocer and General Provision Dealer, Burnt Oak Stores, Edgware."</p>
        <p>The largest tree in the world lies broken and petrified at the end of a defile in North-Western Nevada. It is said to be six hundred and sixty-six feet long. To tell mushrooms from toadstools, without eating and waiting for results, peel an onion and put it with the fungi while being ccoked. If the onion remains white you may eat it with confidence; if it turns black eat it not if you have the slightest desire for life.</p>
        <p>In cases of well-developed consumption the wonderful strength-giving properties of Sco;,t's Emulsion are clearly shown. Consumptives often prolong life for years with Scott's Hmulsion when their phvsicans say there is no other hope When taken at the beginning of lung troubles Scott's Emul- ,ion prevents consumption. Chemists sell it. 2s, 6d. and 4s. 6d.—(18)</p>
        <p>Among the products which science has put to valuable service is the nettle, a weed which is now being culti- vated in some parts of Europe, its fibre proving useful for a variety of textile fabrics. In Dresden a thread is produced from it so fine that a length of sixty miles weighs only two and a half pounds.</p>
        <p>WORTH KNOWING.—WOODEND'S FAMOUS NINE OILS. is s boon to sufferers and sets like magic in all cases of RHEUMATISM, SPRAINS, BRUISES, and STIFF JOINTS. Pri-e is. 4d. her bottle, carriage paid.—J.WoohEND chemist. 22. Pitt-street. Liverpool.—Advt.</p>
        <p>R,•cent experiments for determining the effect of massage upon the blood show that it increases the num- ber of red blood corpuscles enormously, and also the hemoglobin, though to less extent. In some cases there was an even greater increase in the white-blood corpus- cles.</p>
        <p>" CsDnußY's Cocos has in a remarkable degree those natural elements of sustenance which give the system endurance anil hardihood, building up muscle and bodily vigour with a steady action that renders it a most reliable and acceptable bevel age."— Health.</p>
      </div>
