      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>DAItESBUR,Y HIGHWAY BOARD.</head>
        <p>DAItESBUR,Y HIGHWAY BOARD.</p>
        <p>The meeting of the above Board was held on Thursday at the Ring o Bells, Daresbury, owing to the Board-room being used for the tenants' dinner. Mr. Burgess presided. A letter from Sir Leader Williams was read with reference to a footpath at Latchford leading from Acker's-lane to the high level bridge, stating that the Board could make the footpath on the Ship Canal Company's laud at the cost of the Highway Board.</p>
        <p>SHIP-CANA.I COMMITTEE'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Ship Canal Committee's report was read. A meet- ing was called with respect to terminating the claims be- tween the Board and the Canal Company. The claims were drawn up and signed by the Chairman of the Board on behalf of the Highway Board. Mr. Long asked if all the conditions had been carried out, and was told that all except the footpath leading to Lower Walton had been done.</p>
        <p>It was stated that the level railway crossing for the Ship Canal Company's line had been removed the same day as was ordered, but the company were running it under the Stag Inn swing bridge. The bridge would have to be opened for trains as well as boats, and that would be inf tinging the agreements with the Board. Mr. Long stated that they generally kept the engines waiting until a boat went through and dodged it that way. Mr. Long also spoke of the wicker basket signal on Latch- ford swing bridge as not being high enough. From one side the people could not see whether it was raised or not as it did not stand up against the sky through the • railway bank being behind it. The minutes of the Ship Canal Committee were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>PATH ACROSS LATCHFORD LOCKS.</head>
        <p>THE PATH ACROSS LATCHFORD LOCKS.</p>
        <p>With regard to the right of the public to cross the locks at Latchford it was stated that Sir Leader Williams admitted the public right to cross. Mr. Hall proposed that the notice at the locks stating that there was no road be taken down and a fresh oue put up by the Board to show that there was a public road over. Mr. Barber seconded the motion.</p>
        <p>It was finally agreed to ask the Canal Company to take their notice down, but it was not thought necessary to put a new one up as the public would soon know of their right to cross.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>COMPLAINT.</head>
        <p>A COMPLAINT.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from Mr. R. Curran stating that he received notice from the Board's Surveyor to remove a fence enclosing a piece of ground that the Board were to have for putting paving materials on. His (Mr. Curran's) quick set hedge had been removed at that place mad he did not understand the letter. He asked them to explain it. The Surveyor said that no quick set fence had been taken down as there was none there. Several posts and some wire which formed the fence had been removed. A committee wag appointed to see Mr. Curran.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>CLOSING FOOTPATHS.</head>
        <p>CLOSING FOOTPATHS.</p>
        <p>A letter was read stating that an order had been made to close Heyes-lane and the road leading te Morley Common.</p>
        <p>Mr. Darbyshire said he did not think those lanes ought to be closed. He liked to ride along those lanes at times. It was explained to Mr. Darbyshire that those roads led to nowhere now as they were cut across by the canal. It would save the Board tbe expense of keeping them in order if they were closed. The closing would be a good thing for the Board.</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE. The report of the Finance Committee was read and showed that accounts to the amount of £291 12s. 7d. had been examined and recommended for payment. There was a balance iri the Surveyor's hands of £1,464 16s. id. All calls had bet.n paid. There was no other business of importance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>GRAND BALLAD CONCERT AT RUNCORN. --- OPENING OF THE TONIC SOL-FA CHOIR SEiSON.</head>
        <p>GRAND BALLAD CONCERT AT RUNCORN. --- OPENING OF THE TONIC SOL-FA CHOIR SEiSON.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Tonic Sol-Fa prize choir opened their season on Wednesday night with a grand ballad concert, and judging from the success with which it was attended we venture to prophesy for the choir a very successful season. The choristers are apparently increasing very rapidly in the favour of the public, inasmuch as the audience they entertained on Wednesday night was a very large one considering the present depressed state of trade. A number of the seats in the front of the hall had been booked but were not occupied on the night of the concert. The committee had secured the services of Miss Marjorie Eaton, who is a soprano of merit and well-known to London, Man- chester, and Glasgow audiences also Mr. John Pownall, a native of Runcorn but who has climbed the ladder of fathe so high as to be the principal tenor of the Gordon. Hicks' Opera Company ; Mr. Stanley Cookson (bass) and Mr. V. L. Needham, flautist from Sir Charles Halie's Orchestra. The choir, though minus several of its members, was nevertheless strong, and was well able to sustain all the tasks allotted it. Mr. John Holford, L.T.S.C. conducted them with his accustomed ability, and Mrs. W. B. Kay made a skilful accompanist. Previous to beginning the concert, Mr. John Holford said that he was very sorry to have to announce that Miss Eaton was suffering from a cold and was,therefore, not in such good voice as she could have wished. The programme opened with the chorus "Magnify, Glorify," from the "King's Error," and was interpreted most expressively by the choristers who were rewarded with loud plaudits from their hearers. Next came a song by Mr. Stanley Cookson, entitled" My Love, my Crown," which was rendered with much taste and feeling. Mr. Cookson's powerful bass voice at once established him in favour with the audience. Miss Marjorie Eaton then sung Gounod's "Jewel Song," from Faust, but owing, no doubt, to the cold with which she was troubled, both the recitative and the aria fell rather flat upon the audi- ence. The eight part chorus "The word of the Lord giveth life" (King's Error),. was admirably sustained. This item was succeeded by a song from Mr. John Pownall, the title of which was " Come into the garden Maud," (Balfe). His fine tenor voice seemed even better than when he was last heard in Runcorn, and when he finished he was vociferously applauded and responded to an encore. Mr. V. L. Needham completely enchanted his hearers by his execution on the flute of a solo entitled "Souvenir de Verdi." Of the flute Mr. Needham is a thorough master, and his playing won for him many golden opinions. The choir effeciontly sang "Tle Silent Tide," and was followed by Messrs. J. Pownal and S. Cookson who gave a very masterly interpretation of the duet" Flow gently, Deva." This earned them an encore to which they responded by singing " All's well that ends Well." Miss Eaton sang " Songs we used to sing," with flute obligato by Mr. Needham. Her voice, though somewhat sweet at times, was too low. Mr. Needham executed his portion with great skill and taste. The first portion of the programme was brought to a close by the singing of the part song "Charming May" in which the choristers acquitted themselves with great credit. They also showed capital style in the re-open- ing item "The Corsair's Home." Mr. Stanley Cookson delighted the audience with his rendition of the old Irish song "Molly Carew," for which his voice was very agreeably suited. His accent was especially .00d,whilst the fervour he threw into it won him a well-deserved encore. To this he gave a happy response by singing 66 Off to Philadelphia "and was enthusiastically cheered at its finish. Mr. Needham again exhibited his almost marvellous power over the flute in the rendering of a solo of his own (apparently) composing, and entitled "Fantasia on Scotch Airs." For this he was repeatedly applauded, and at length answered tho recall by execut- ing a selection without the aid of the accompanist. At, this stage of the proceedings Mr. Holtord in a few words informed the audience that through the non- arrival of the copies they were conipeiled to omit the trio "I Nat iga rid," and the son. " Terence's Farewell to Kathleen," the last of which had been entrusted to Miss Eaton. Following upon this announcement came a glee by the choir "0 snatch me swift," and a song by Mr. John Pownall named " The Garonne" (Stephen Adams). In this he scored a big success, his expression being exceptionally good. He replied to the recall by contributing 66 Ott Marguereta." Mr. Cookson's rendi- tion of the song "Unrequited Love " gained him much appreciation. The last, but by no means the least item on the programme, was in the care of Mr. John Pownall and was a song called "The Message" (Blumenthal). It could not have been placed in more clever hands,and the audience evidently recognised this if applause is to be taken as the reflection of their opinion. The singing of the National Anthem terminated a most enjoyable and very successful concert. The choristers deserve great praise for the way in which they performed the various selections. Mr.Holford as usual proved himself a clever conductor,and Mrs. Kay an accomplished pianist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>NEWTON-LB-WILLOWS BOWLING CLUB.</head>
        <p>NEWTON-LB-WILLOWS BOWLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The annual bowling handicap, which commenced on Satureav the 7th inst., and closes the season, was concluded on Saturday last. There were 77 entries, and each of the days being fine the games were of a pleasant character. Contrary to custom, those most favourably handicapped were this year successful in carrying off the best prizes, the results of the first ten being as follows :-1, G. Harrison; 2,T. Boardman ; 3, W. Sutton; 4, W. Longton; 5, D Rylance; 6, J. Crouchley ; 7, W. Hall; 8, B. Corlett, 9, I. Bateman; 10, H. Buckley. The prizes con- sisted of useful articles and orders on tradesmen, each competitor receiving a prize of more or less value. At the conclusion of the game the players adjourned to the Pied Bull hotel, where about 70 .at down to a knife and fork tea provided by Mr. Dornrin. The after proceedings, during which the prizes were distributed, were presided over by Mr. J. Liptrott, and were of a convivial character. Songs were rendered by Messrs. W. Devereux, R. Lythgoe, W. Liptrott, W. Sutton, L. Bartlett.,E. Grace, R. Smith, W. Lane, and J. Schofield. At he close the usual vote of thanks was accorded the Chairman.</p>
        <p>C GARLAND, 51, Bridgoltr eet, Warrington. Adv</p>
