        <p>"PLEASANT SUNDAY AFTERNOON" AT RUNCORN. 'GOLD MEDAL DIETETIC HAS. On Sunday last the firs tof the series of Pleasant Sun- day Afternoon Services was held in connection with the SPECIALLY RECOMMENDED TO DYSPEPTICS, St. Paul's Wesleyan Church Bible Class, Runcorn. Mr. and a great boon to any who have been denied the B. liniveton filled the chair, and the services was largely use of other teas by their Medical Advisers. attended. Mr. R. Posnett gave an instructive address, jlMessrs. B. Crosby and E. Worrall sang solos, and Mr. ENRY MILLING &amp; CO. LTD. Wm. Cooke gave a recital on the organ. MERCHANTS, WARfRINGTON. THE ELEVATION OF SIR JOHN RIGBY, FORMERLY OF RUNCORN. Rnncornians will, no doubt, have heard with great pleasure the news published in our last issue to the effect that Sir John Rigby, M.P., whose native town is Run- corn, had been appointed a Lord Justice of Appeal. It is the one topic of conversation in the legal world, and has formed the subject of numerous articles in the lead- ing daily journals. [he transfer from the Treasury bench to the position of a Lord Justice will, of course, leave Forfarshire without a representative ha Parliament. It is, however, a tolerably secure Liberal seat, Sir John Rigby's majority over Mr. J. W. Barclay at the last general election being 886 Sir John Riisby will be suc- ceeded in the Attorney Generalship by Sir R. T. Reid. FORTHCOMING LANTERN PARADE AT RUNCORN. As will been seen in our adt ertising colums a grand lantern parade and fancy dress carnival will take place at Runcorn on Wednesday evening next in aid of the National Lifeboat Institution, and the Runcorn District Nurse Fund. The originators of thR event are the mem- bers ef the Runcorn Cycle Club who recognised the worthiness of the two objects named, as well as their sore need of assistance. We need hardly speak of the great good which the National Lifeboat institution has effected, and the numberless lives that have been saved through the instrumentality of its officers. This is too generally known to require such notice whilst it is equally needless to draw attention to the value of a district nurse in our midst,because the presence of Nurse Beckenham is known in almost every poor man's house where illness has existed recently ; and her valuable services are being appreciated more and more every day Suffice it to say therefore that these two objects are two of the most worthy which the members of our local Cycle Club could poesibly have selected for the bestowal of their sympathy. Our wheelrnen have cer- tainly gone into the matter with no small amount of enthusiasm. They have determined to make the event a success, and there is no reason to believe that their efforts will fail. They will st.irt from the Wilson's Hotel at seven o'clock, and will be joined by cyclists from Chester, Widnes, Northwich. Warrington, Hough Green, St. Helens, and a number of other districts. The procession will also be augmented by the presence of the Army Cycle Corps from Warrington, the 2nd C.R.V., the Runcorn Fire Brigade, Helsby Fire Brigade, and several brass bands. The cyclists have made great preparations for the parade, they having procured special gaily-coloured costumes to represent all the most well-known characters of the day. There is also expected to be seen a great novelty, the nature of which it is not for us here to describe. the committee, of which Mr. Harry Wright is the treasurer, and Messrs.R. Moores and A. Riley, the secretaries, have arranged for a number of collectors to follow the procession with official boxes. These gentlemen will also deck themselves out in representative costumes. It is to be hoped that the public will approve of the enterprise of our cyclists by liberally subscribing.</p>
