        <p>FINANCIAL. Mr. Brandreth presented the report of the Finance Committee and proposed its adoption. It showed that there was a balance in the treasurer's hands of £1,545 7s. 51. The total amount of thP various accounts which were to be paid that day was £582 is. 31. Included in these accounts was one amounting to £lB3 lls, for the maintenance, etc., of lunatics. There were 48 lunatics in all; 46 ef whom were lodged in Chester Asylum and 2 in Parkside, Macclesfield.—Mr. Parkinson seconded the adoption, and the proposition was carried. THE HOUSE OVERCROWDED. Mr. Norman stated that the House Committee had had two or three matters under consideration. One of them was the recommendation of the Ladies' Committee ask- ing that in cases where girls were going out to service they might be advised so that they might have the opportunity of investigating the homes where they were going. The committee could then inform the Guardians of the suitability of those homes or otherwise. The House Committee asked the Board to comply with the suggestion. The Master had reported to them that the part of the house occupied by the males was overcrowded. In face of the fact that there was a note made by Mr. DDlby, the auditor, in the journal some months ago that they would have to consider the necessity of making further accom- modation for males, the committee thought it wise to mention the matter so that they might ponder over the subject. If matters went on as they had been going for some time they would have to provide further accom- modation. He moved the adoption of the report.— Dr Gastaldi seconded the proposition. He thought the re- commendation of the Ladies' Committee was a very wise ow. He was sure the ladies would look aft,' the best welfare of the girls who were about to enter service —The motion was carried. Lifeboat stat ions on the French coast are hereafter to number among their equipment trained dogs. The rudder of the Cunard steamship Campania eon sists of a single plate of steel, twenty-two by eleven feet six inches and one and one fourth inches thick, it was rolled at Krupp's German gun factory. Artificial whalebone is now being made from leather, which is soaked for two or three days in sulphate of potassium, then stretched, slowly dried, subjected to a high temperature, and then to a heavy pressure, which makes it hard and elastic. The Carrara marble quarries are practically inexhaus- tible. The entire mass of Monte Sagro, five thousand six hundred feet high, which dominates Carrara, is solid m irble. About ene hundred and sixty thousand tons of marble are annu“iiy exported, most of which goes to America.</p>
